insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$ibrahim$tk$, 'fr', 6, $tk$Ibrahim (Abraham)$tk$, $tk$إبراهيم$tk$, $tk$L'ami d'Allah et le père des prophètes$tk$, $tk$Ibrahim, paix sur lui, grandit au milieu de gens qui adoraient des idoles et des astres. Son propre père, que le Coran appelle Azar, était l'un d'eux. Dès sa jeunesse, Allah avait donné à Ibrahim un jugement droit, et il ne pouvait accepter que l'on se prosterne devant des statues façonnées de ses propres mains.

Le Coran raconte comment il raisonna avec son peuple. Il regarda une étoile, puis la lune, puis le soleil. Chacun se coucha et disparut, et chaque fois il montra que ce qui disparaît ne peut pas être le Seigneur. Il déclara alors qu'il tournait son visage vers Celui qui a créé les cieux et la terre.

Un jour, alors que les gens étaient partis, Ibrahim brisa leurs idoles en morceaux, sauf la plus grande. Quand ils l'interrogèrent, il leur dit de questionner la grande idole, si toutefois les idoles pouvaient parler. Un instant, ils comprirent combien leur culte était absurde. Mais l'orgueil l'emporta, et ils décidèrent de le brûler. Allah ordonna au feu d'être fraîcheur et salut pour Ibrahim, et il en sortit indemne.

Ibrahim quitta alors son peuple pour son Seigneur et Lui demanda un enfant vertueux. Dans sa vieillesse, Allah lui accorda Ismaïl et Ishaq, et il rendit grâce à Celui qui entend toute prière.

Sur l'ordre d'Allah, il installa une partie de sa famille dans une vallée sans cultures, près de la Maison sacrée, et pria pour que des cœurs se tournent vers eux et qu'ils reçoivent des fruits. Plus tard, il éleva les fondations de la Kaaba avec son fils Ismaïl. Tout en bâtissant, ils demandaient à Allah d'accepter leur œuvre et d'envoyer aux habitants de cette terre un messager issu d'eux-mêmes.

Ibrahim fut aussi éprouvé de la manière la plus dure : il vit en rêve qu'il sacrifiait son fils. Il lui en parla, et le fils répondit qu'il devait faire ce qui lui était ordonné. Quand tous deux se furent soumis, Allah appela Ibrahim pour lui dire qu'il avait accompli la vision, et le fils fut racheté par un sacrifice immense. Les musulmans s'en souviennent chaque année lors de l'Aïd al-Adha.

Le Coran dit qu'Ibrahim fut éprouvé par son Seigneur par des commandements et qu'il les accomplit tous, et qu'Allah fit de lui un guide pour les hommes.$tk$, $tk$Sers-toi de ta raison pour chercher la vérité, et aie le courage de la suivre même si tu es seul. Ce que l'on abandonne pour Allah n'est jamais perdu.$tk$, $tk$["6:74-83","21:51-70","2:124-129","37:99-111","14:35-41"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$lut$tk$, 'fr', 7, $tk$Lout (Loth)$tk$, $tk$لوط$tk$, $tk$Le prophète resté ferme au milieu d'un peuple corrompu$tk$, $tk$Lout, paix sur lui, vécut à l'époque d'Ibrahim, paix sur lui. Le Coran dit qu'il crut au message d'Ibrahim. Il fut ensuite envoyé comme prophète à un peuple dont le Coran ne nomme pas la cité.

Ce peuple était tombé dans de graves dérives. Le Coran dit qu'ils commettaient une turpitude qu'aucun peuple n'avait commise avant eux : les hommes allaient vers les hommes avec désir, au lieu des femmes. Ils attaquaient aussi les voyageurs sur les routes et faisaient le mal ouvertement dans leurs assemblées. Lout les appela à craindre Allah et à renoncer à ces actes. Il leur dit qu'il ne demandait aucun salaire et qu'il était pour eux un messager digne de confiance.

Pour toute réponse, ils se moquèrent de lui et le menacèrent. Ils dirent : chassez Lout et les siens de la cité, ce sont des gens qui veulent rester purs. Ils le défièrent même de faire venir le châtiment d'Allah s'il disait vrai. Lout demanda alors l'aide de son Seigneur contre ceux qui semaient la corruption.

Des anges furent envoyés en mission. Ils passèrent d'abord chez Ibrahim, à qui ils annoncèrent la bonne nouvelle d'un fils, et lui dirent qu'ils se rendaient auprès du peuple de Lout. Puis ils arrivèrent chez Lout sous l'apparence d'invités. Il ne savait pas qui ils étaient, et il fut angoissé, car il craignait ce que son peuple leur ferait. En effet, les hommes de la cité accoururent vers sa maison. Lout les supplia de craindre Allah et de ne pas le couvrir de honte devant ses hôtes. Il aurait voulu avoir la force de les arrêter.

Les anges révélèrent alors qui ils étaient : des envoyés de son Seigneur, que ces gens n'atteindraient jamais. Ils dirent à Lout de partir pendant la nuit avec sa famille, et que personne ne se retourne. Son épouse, elle, serait de ceux qui restèrent en arrière et partagerait le sort des autres.

Au matin, le châtiment s'abattit. La cité fut renversée de fond en comble et une pluie de pierres d'argile durcie tomba sur elle. Lout et ceux de sa famille qui l'avaient suivi furent sauvés.

Le Coran dit que les vestiges de ce lieu ont été laissés comme un signe clair pour des gens qui raisonnent.$tk$, $tk$Bien agir ne dépend pas de ce que fait la majorité. Reste fidèle à tes valeurs, même quand on se moque de toi parce que tu veux rester pur.$tk$, $tk$["7:80-84","11:77-83","15:57-77","29:26-35"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$ismail$tk$, 'fr', 8, $tk$Ismaïl$tk$, $tk$إسماعيل$tk$, $tk$Fidèle à sa promesse, bâtisseur de la Kaaba avec son père$tk$, $tk$Ismaïl, paix sur lui, est le fils d'Ibrahim, paix sur lui. Ibrahim avait demandé à son Seigneur un enfant vertueux, et il remercia Allah de lui avoir accordé Ismaïl et Ishaq dans sa vieillesse.

Le Coran rapporte la prière d'Ibrahim disant qu'il avait installé une partie de sa descendance dans une vallée sans cultures, près de la Maison sacrée, afin qu'ils accomplissent la prière. Un long hadith du Sahih al-Boukhari (n° 3364) raconte comment Ibrahim laissa Ismaïl, encore nourrisson, avec sa mère Hajar dans cette vallée déserte de La Mecque, comment elle courut entre les collines de Safa et Marwa à la recherche d'eau, et comment la source de Zamzam apparut. Les pèlerins marchent encore entre ces deux collines en souvenir de sa confiance en Allah.

Le Coran raconte ensuite une grande épreuve. Ibrahim vit en rêve qu'il sacrifiait son fils, et il lui demanda ce qu'il en pensait. Le fils, décrit comme doux et patient, répondit que son père devait faire ce qui lui était ordonné, et qu'il le trouverait, si Allah le voulait, du nombre des endurants. Quand tous deux se furent soumis, Allah appela Ibrahim pour lui dire qu'il avait accompli la vision, et Il racheta le fils par un sacrifice immense. Le Coran ne nomme pas le fils dans ce passage ; la plupart des savants musulmans considèrent qu'il s'agit d'Ismaïl.

Le père et le fils accomplirent ensuite ensemble une œuvre qui marque encore la vie des musulmans. Allah chargea Ibrahim et Ismaïl de purifier Sa Maison pour ceux qui tournent autour d'elle, y demeurent en adoration, s'inclinent et se prosternent. Ensemble, ils élevèrent les fondations de la Kaaba, en demandant à Allah d'accepter cela de leur part, de faire d'eux et de leur descendance des gens soumis à Lui, et d'envoyer aux habitants de cette terre un messager issu d'eux-mêmes. Les musulmans voient dans la venue du Prophète Muhammad ﷺ, descendant d'Ismaïl, la réponse à cette prière.

Le Coran résume le caractère d'Ismaïl en quelques mots. Il était fidèle à sa promesse, messager et prophète. Il recommandait aux siens la prière et l'aumône, et son Seigneur était satisfait de lui. Ailleurs, il est compté parmi les patients et parmi ceux qu'Allah a favorisés.$tk$, $tk$Tiens tes promesses, même les petites, et soucie-toi de la foi de tes proches. La confiance en Allah grandit par la patience dans les moments difficiles.$tk$, $tk$["2:125-129","19:54-55","37:101-107","14:37-39","21:85-86"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$ishaq$tk$, 'fr', 9, $tk$Ishaq (Isaac)$tk$, $tk$إسحاق$tk$, $tk$La bonne nouvelle annoncée à Ibrahim dans sa vieillesse$tk$, $tk$Ishaq, paix sur lui, est le second fils d'Ibrahim, paix sur lui. Le Coran raconte sa naissance avec un certain détail, mais dit peu de chose du reste de sa vie.

Un jour, des hôtes se présentèrent chez Ibrahim et le saluèrent par la paix. Il leur rendit leur salut et, généreux comme il l'était, leur apporta sans tarder un veau rôti. Mais les hôtes ne tendaient pas la main vers le repas. Ibrahim en fut inquiet. Ils lui dirent de ne pas avoir peur : ils étaient des anges, envoyés vers le peuple de Lout, et ils lui annonçaient la bonne nouvelle d'un fils plein de savoir.

L'épouse d'Ibrahim se tenait tout près. Le Coran dit qu'on lui annonça la bonne nouvelle d'Ishaq et, après Ishaq, de Yaqoub. Elle fut stupéfaite : comment aurait-elle un enfant, elle, une vieille femme qui n'avait jamais pu en avoir, alors que son mari était un vieillard ? Les anges répondirent : s'étonnait-elle du décret d'Allah ? Ils souhaitèrent la miséricorde et les bénédictions d'Allah sur les gens de cette maison.

La promesse s'accomplit. Ibrahim loua Allah de lui avoir donné Ismaïl et Ishaq dans sa vieillesse, et proclama que son Seigneur entend toute prière.

Le Coran décrit Ishaq comme un prophète du nombre des vertueux, et dit qu'Allah bénit Ibrahim et Ishaq. Il ajoute qu'Allah fit d'Ishaq et de Yaqoub des guides qui dirigeaient selon Son ordre, et leur inspira de faire le bien, d'accomplir la prière et de donner l'aumône ; ils étaient de fervents adorateurs d'Allah. Lorsque Yaqoub fut proche de la mort, ses fils promirent d'adorer le Dieu de ses pères, Ibrahim, Ismaïl et Ishaq, un Dieu unique.

Au-delà, le Coran ne rapporte ni la mission d'Ishaq ni les événements de sa vie. Les nombreux détails que l'on trouve dans d'autres sources ne sont confirmés ni par le Coran ni par les hadiths authentiques, et nous ne les reprenons donc pas ici.

D'Ishaq est né Yaqoub, et de Yaqoub une longue lignée de prophètes parmi les Enfants d'Israël, dont Youssouf, Moussa, Dawoud et Issa, paix sur eux tous. La naissance d'Ishaq montre qu'Allah tient Ses promesses au moment qu'Il choisit, même lorsque toutes les causes visibles semblent dire qu'il est trop tard.$tk$, $tk$Ne pense jamais qu'il est trop tard pour qu'une prière soit exaucée. La générosité envers les invités et la confiance en Allah ouvrent des portes qui semblaient fermées.$tk$, $tk$["11:69-73","37:112-113","51:24-30","14:39","21:72-73"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$yaqub$tk$, 'fr', 10, $tk$Yaqoub (Jacob)$tk$, $tk$يعقوب$tk$, $tk$Le père à la belle patience$tk$, $tk$Yaqoub, paix sur lui, est le fils d'Ishaq et le petit-fils d'Ibrahim, paix sur eux. Il est aussi appelé Israïl, et ses descendants sont appelés les Enfants d'Israël. Le Coran parle de lui surtout comme d'un père, dans l'histoire de son fils Youssouf.

Quand le jeune Youssouf lui raconta un rêve où onze étoiles, le soleil et la lune se prosternaient devant lui, Yaqoub comprit qu'Allah avait choisi cet enfant. Il lui conseilla de ne rien dire à ses frères, de peur que la jalousie ne les pousse à comploter contre lui.

Les frères complotèrent bel et bien. Ils demandèrent à emmener Youssouf avec eux. Yaqoub dit qu'il était triste de le laisser partir et qu'il craignait qu'un loup ne le dévore. Le soir, ils revinrent en pleurs, avec un faux sang sur la tunique de Youssouf. Yaqoub ne crut pas leur récit. Il leur dit que leurs âmes les avaient poussés à commettre quelque chose, et il choisit une belle patience, ne demandant de l'aide qu'à Allah.

Les années passèrent. Quand un deuxième fils fut retenu en Égypte, la douleur revint. Yaqoub se détourna et pleura Youssouf, et ses yeux blanchirent sous l'effet d'un chagrin qu'il gardait en lui. Ses fils lui reprochèrent de penser encore à Youssouf. Il répondit qu'il ne se plaignait de sa peine et de son chagrin qu'à Allah, et qu'il savait de la part d'Allah ce qu'ils ne savaient pas.

Puis il leur donna ce conseil : allez chercher Youssouf et son frère, et ne désespérez pas de la miséricorde d'Allah.

Son espoir fut récompensé. Youssouf, devenu un haut responsable en Égypte, envoya sa tunique pour qu'on la pose sur le visage de son père. Alors que la caravane quittait l'Égypte, Yaqoub dit qu'il percevait l'odeur de Youssouf. Lorsque la tunique fut posée sur son visage, il recouvra la vue. Ses fils lui demandèrent de prier pour leur pardon, et il le leur promit. Toute la famille fut réunie en Égypte.

Le Coran rapporte aussi le dernier souci de Yaqoub. À l'approche de la mort, il demanda à ses fils ce qu'ils adoreraient après lui. Ils répondirent qu'ils adoreraient son Dieu et le Dieu de ses pères, Ibrahim, Ismaïl et Ishaq, un Dieu unique.$tk$, $tk$La tristesse n'est pas un manque de foi : confie ta peine à Allah et garde espoir. Ne désespère jamais de Sa miséricorde.$tk$, $tk$["2:132-133","12:4-6","12:83-87","12:93-100"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$yusuf$tk$, 'fr', 11, $tk$Youssouf (Joseph)$tk$, $tk$يوسف$tk$, $tk$Le véridique, du puits aux greniers d'Égypte$tk$, $tk$L'histoire de Youssouf, paix sur lui, occupe toute une sourate du Coran. Enfant, il rêva que onze étoiles, le soleil et la lune se prosternaient devant lui. Son père Yaqoub, paix sur lui, lui dit de ne pas raconter ce rêve à ses frères.

Les frères étaient jaloux de l'amour que leur père portait à Youssouf. Ils l'emmenèrent et le jetèrent au fond d'un puits, puis dirent à leur père qu'un loup l'avait dévoré. Des voyageurs de passage tirèrent Youssouf du puits, et il fut vendu en Égypte pour quelques pièces.

Il grandit dans la maison d'un haut dignitaire égyptien. Lorsqu'il devint un homme, l'épouse de ce dignitaire chercha à le séduire. Youssouf chercha refuge auprès d'Allah et courut vers la porte ; elle déchira sa tunique par-derrière, ce qui prouva son innocence. Il fut pourtant envoyé en prison. Il avait même dit que la prison lui était préférable à ce à quoi on l'invitait.

En prison, Youssouf était connu pour sa bonté. Il interpréta les rêves de deux compagnons de cellule et en profita pour leur parler du Dieu unique. L'un d'eux fut libéré, mais oublia de parler de Youssouf à son maître, et Youssouf resta en prison plusieurs années.

Puis le roi fit un rêve : sept vaches grasses mangées par sept vaches maigres, et sept épis verts avec d'autres, desséchés. Youssouf l'expliqua : sept années de bonnes récoltes, puis sept années difficiles ; il fallait donc mettre le grain en réserve. Avant de sortir de prison, il demanda que son innocence soit établie, et l'épouse du dignitaire reconnut la vérité. Youssouf demanda à être chargé des greniers du pays, et il les géra avec soin.

Quand la famine arriva, ses frères vinrent en Égypte chercher des vivres. Youssouf les reconnut, mais eux ne le reconnurent pas. Après plusieurs voyages, il leur révéla qui il était. Ils reconnurent leur tort. Il répondit qu'aucun reproche ne leur serait fait ce jour-là et qu'il espérait qu'Allah leur pardonnerait.

Youssouf fit alors venir ses parents et toute sa famille en Égypte. Son rêve d'enfant s'était réalisé. Il conclut par une prière, demandant à son Seigneur de le faire mourir soumis à Lui et de le faire rejoindre les vertueux.$tk$, $tk$Reste honnête et pur, que tu sois tout en bas ou tout en haut. Quand tu as enfin le pouvoir sur ceux qui t'ont fait du mal, le pardon est le choix le plus noble.$tk$, $tk$["12:4-21","12:23-35","12:36-57","12:88-101"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$ayyub$tk$, 'fr', 12, $tk$Ayyoub (Job)$tk$, $tk$أيوب$tk$, $tk$Le modèle de la patience$tk$, $tk$Ayyoub, paix sur lui, est le prophète dont le nom est devenu le symbole de la patience. Le Coran le compte parmi les prophètes qui ont reçu la révélation et parmi les descendants d'Ibrahim qu'Allah a guidés.

Le Coran raconte son histoire en quelques versets, sans beaucoup de détails. Ayyoub fut frappé par une dure épreuve qui toucha sa santé et sa famille. Le Coran ne décrit pas sa maladie et ne dit pas combien de temps elle dura. Beaucoup de récits circulent à ce sujet, certains exagérés et indignes d'un prophète ; ils ne sont pas établis et nous les laissons de côté.

Ce que le Coran a conservé, c'est la manière dont Ayyoub s'adressa à son Seigneur. Il ne se plaignit pas aux gens et n'accusa pas Allah. Il dit simplement que le mal l'avait touché, et qu'Allah est le plus Miséricordieux des miséricordieux. Dans un autre passage, il dit que Satan l'avait atteint par la détresse et la souffrance. Sa prière ne contient aucune exigence : il décrit son état, il loue la miséricorde de son Seigneur, et il Lui laisse le reste.

Allah l'exauça. Il lui dit de frapper le sol du pied : une source d'eau fraîche apparut, pour se laver et pour boire. Sa souffrance fut dissipée. Allah lui rendit sa famille, et autant avec elle, par miséricorde de Sa part et comme rappel pour les adorateurs et pour les gens doués d'intelligence.

Le Coran mentionne aussi qu'Ayyoub avait fait un serment pendant son épreuve. Allah lui indiqua une manière douce de tenir parole sans faire de mal : prendre dans sa main une poignée d'herbes et frapper avec. Le Coran n'explique pas en détail les circonstances de ce serment.

Le passage se termine par l'éloge d'Allah Lui-même : Il a trouvé Ayyoub patient, un excellent serviteur, qui revenait sans cesse vers Lui.

La patience d'Ayyoub n'était ni le silence ni l'absence de douleur. Il ressentait sa souffrance et il la disait, mais il la disait à Allah, avec respect et espoir. Son histoire console depuis des siècles les malades et les personnes en deuil, car elle montre qu'une épreuve n'est pas le signe d'un rejet de la part d'Allah, et que le soulagement peut venir de là où personne ne l'attend.$tk$, $tk$Dans l'épreuve, parle de ta peine à Allah sans perdre ni le respect ni l'espoir. La patience ne consiste pas à ne rien ressentir, mais à rester fidèle quand cela fait mal.$tk$, $tk$["21:83-84","38:41-44","4:163","6:84"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$shuayb$tk$, 'fr', 13, $tk$Chouayb$tk$, $tk$شعيب$tk$, $tk$Le prophète du commerce honnête$tk$, $tk$Chouayb, paix sur lui, fut envoyé au peuple de Madyan. Le Coran l'appelle leur frère, car il était l'un des leurs. Il mentionne aussi sa prédication auprès des gens du Fourré ; les savants divergent sur le point de savoir s'il s'agit du même peuple que Madyan ou d'un groupe voisin.

Les gens de Madyan étaient prospères, mais leur commerce reposait sur la fraude. Ils donnaient moins que le dû quand ils mesuraient et pesaient, et ils privaient les gens de ce qui leur revenait. Ils se postaient aussi sur les chemins pour menacer les gens et détourner les croyants de la voie d'Allah.

Chouayb les appela d'abord à adorer Allah seul. Puis il leur parla de leurs affaires de tous les jours : donnez la pleine mesure et le plein poids avec équité, ne lésez pas les gens dans leurs biens, et ne semez pas la corruption sur terre après qu'elle a été mise en ordre. Il leur dit que ce qu'Allah leur laisse comme gain honnête est meilleur pour eux. Il leur rappela qu'ils avaient été peu nombreux et qu'Allah les avait multipliés.

Ils répondirent en se moquant. Sa prière lui ordonnait-elle qu'ils abandonnent ce qu'adoraient leurs pères, ou qu'ils cessent de faire ce qu'ils voulaient de leur argent ? Chouayb répondit par des paroles restées célèbres : il ne voulait pas faire lui-même ce qu'il leur interdisait ; il ne cherchait qu'à réformer autant qu'il le pouvait, et sa réussite ne dépendait que d'Allah, à qui il s'en remettait.

Il les avertit de ne pas laisser leur opposition à son égard leur attirer un sort semblable à celui des peuples de Nouh, de Houd, de Salih ou de Lout. Il les invita à demander pardon, en leur disant que son Seigneur est miséricordieux et plein d'amour.

Les notables passèrent aux menaces. Ils dirent qu'ils ne comprenaient pas grand-chose à ses paroles, qu'ils le voyaient faible parmi eux, et que sans sa famille ils l'auraient lapidé. Ils menacèrent de l'expulser avec les croyants s'ils ne revenaient pas à leur religion.

Le châtiment arriva. Le Coran parle d'un séisme et d'un cri terrible qui les laissèrent sans vie dans leurs demeures, et du châtiment du jour de la nuée sombre. Chouayb et ceux qui avaient cru avec lui furent sauvés par la miséricorde d'Allah.$tk$, $tk$La foi se voit dans la manière d'acheter, de vendre et de travailler : donne à chacun ce qui lui est dû. Applique-toi à toi-même ce que tu demandes aux autres.$tk$, $tk$["7:85-93","11:84-95","26:176-191","29:36-37"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$musa$tk$, 'fr', 14, $tk$Moussa (Moïse)$tk$, $tk$موسى$tk$, $tk$Celui à qui Allah a parlé$tk$, $tk$Moussa, paix sur lui, est le prophète le plus souvent cité dans le Coran. Il naquit en Égypte à une époque où Pharaon opprimait les Enfants d'Israël et faisait tuer leurs nouveau-nés garçons. Allah inspira à la mère de Moussa de déposer son bébé dans le fleuve, en lui promettant de le lui rendre. Les gens de Pharaon le recueillirent, et l'épouse de Pharaon demanda qu'on l'épargne. Comme le bébé refusait toutes les nourrices, il fut rendu à sa propre mère pour être allaité.

Devenu un jeune homme, Moussa frappa un Égyptien qui se battait avec un homme de son peuple, et l'homme mourut. Moussa n'avait pas voulu le tuer ; il le regretta aussitôt et demanda pardon à Allah. Il s'enfuit à Madyan. Là, il aida deux femmes à abreuver leur troupeau, puis s'assit à l'ombre et dit dans sa prière qu'il avait besoin de tout bien qu'Allah lui enverrait. Leur père l'accueillit, lui donna l'une de ses filles en mariage, et Moussa travailla pour lui pendant des années.

Sur le chemin du retour avec sa famille, il aperçut un feu près du mont Tour. Là, Allah lui parla, fit de lui Son messager et lui donna des signes : son bâton se changea en serpent et sa main devint d'une blancheur éclatante. Moussa demanda que son frère Haroun soit envoyé avec lui. Allah leur dit d'aller trouver Pharaon et de lui parler avec douceur.

Pharaon refusa d'écouter et convoqua ses magiciens. Mais quand ils virent le bâton de Moussa engloutir leurs artifices, ils reconnurent la vérité et proclamèrent leur foi, malgré les menaces de Pharaon.

Allah ordonna alors à Moussa de partir de nuit avec son peuple. Pharaon les poursuivit avec son armée jusqu'à la mer. Le peuple s'affola, mais Moussa répondit que son Seigneur était avec lui et qu'Il le guiderait. Il frappa la mer de son bâton ; elle se fendit, et ils traversèrent. Pharaon et ses soldats furent noyés.

Moussa partit ensuite à la rencontre de son Seigneur pendant quarante nuits et reçut les Tables. En son absence, une partie de son peuple se mit à adorer un veau fait de leurs bijoux. Moussa revint en colère et affligé, et les appela à se repentir.

Jusqu'à la fin de sa vie, Moussa guida son peuple avec patience, porteur de la Torah, guidance et lumière.$tk$, $tk$Personne n'est trop faible pour être choisi pour une grande chose, et aucun tyran n'est trop fort pour tomber. Quand tu te sens acculé, souviens-toi qu'Allah est avec ceux qui Lui font confiance.$tk$, $tk$["28:3-35","20:9-79","26:52-68","7:142-145","2:51-54"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$harun$tk$, 'fr', 15, $tk$Haroun (Aaron)$tk$, $tk$هارون$tk$, $tk$Le frère et le soutien de Moussa$tk$, $tk$Haroun, paix sur lui, est le frère de Moussa, paix sur lui. Dans le Coran, son histoire est toujours liée à celle de Moussa : il est le prophète donné comme soutien à un autre prophète.

Quand Allah parla à Moussa et l'envoya vers Pharaon, Moussa mesura le poids de la mission. Il demanda à son Seigneur d'ouvrir son cœur, de lui faciliter sa tâche et de dénouer sa langue pour que les gens le comprennent. Puis il demanda un assistant issu de sa propre famille : Haroun, son frère, pour le fortifier et partager sa tâche. Moussa dit que Haroun s'exprimait mieux que lui. Allah exauça sa demande, et le Coran dit que, par miséricorde, Allah donna à Moussa son frère Haroun comme prophète.

Les deux frères allèrent ensemble trouver Pharaon. Allah leur avait dit de lui parler avec douceur, dans l'espoir qu'il se souvienne ou qu'il craigne. Ils lui demandèrent de laisser partir les Enfants d'Israël et de cesser de les tourmenter. Quand les magiciens de Pharaon virent la vérité, ils déclarèrent qu'ils croyaient au Seigneur de Haroun et de Moussa.

Après la traversée de la mer, Moussa partit à la rencontre de son Seigneur pendant quarante nuits. Avant de partir, il demanda à Haroun de le remplacer auprès du peuple, d'agir avec droiture et de ne pas suivre le chemin de ceux qui sèment la corruption.

C'est pendant cette absence que survint l'épreuve du veau. Un homme que le Coran appelle le Samiri égara le peuple avec un veau fait de leurs bijoux. Le Coran établit clairement que Haroun n'y prit aucune part. Il leur dit qu'ils étaient mis à l'épreuve par ce veau, que leur Seigneur était le Tout Miséricordieux, et qu'ils devaient le suivre et lui obéir. Ils refusèrent d'écouter tant que Moussa ne serait pas revenu.

Quand Moussa revint, en colère et affligé, il saisit son frère par la tête. Haroun lui répondit avec tendresse, en l'appelant fils de ma mère. Il expliqua que le peuple avait pris le dessus sur lui et avait failli le tuer, et qu'il avait craint de diviser les Enfants d'Israël. Moussa demanda alors à Allah de lui pardonner, à lui et à son frère, et de les faire entrer tous deux dans Sa miséricorde.

Ailleurs, le Coran compte Haroun parmi les prophètes guidés et récompensés pour leur bienfaisance.$tk$, $tk$Soutenir la mission de quelqu'un d'autre est aussi noble que la diriger. Dans un conflit avec un proche, réponds avec douceur et explique-toi calmement.$tk$, $tk$["20:29-36","28:34-35","20:90-94","7:150-151","19:53"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$dhul-kifl$tk$, 'fr', 16, $tk$Dhoul-Kifl$tk$, $tk$ذو الكفل$tk$, $tk$Compté parmi les patients et les meilleurs$tk$, $tk$Dhoul-Kifl, paix sur lui, est l'une des figures dont le Coran nous dit le moins. Il n'est mentionné que deux fois, chaque fois dans un seul verset et aux côtés d'autres prophètes.

Dans la sourate Al-Anbiya, il est nommé avec Ismaïl et Idris. Le Coran dit que tous étaient du nombre des patients, qu'Allah les a fait entrer dans Sa miséricorde, et qu'ils étaient du nombre des vertueux. Dans la sourate Sad, il est nommé avec Ismaïl et Al-Yasa, et le Coran dit que tous étaient parmi les meilleurs.

C'est tout ce que dit le Coran. Il ne nous apprend ni où ni quand il a vécu, ni vers quel peuple il a été envoyé, ni ce qu'il a fait. Aucun hadith authentique n'ajoute non plus de détail clair sur sa vie.

Son nom lui-même a été discuté. En arabe, Dhoul-Kifl signifie à peu près celui qui a la part, ou celui qui s'est porté garant. Certains commentateurs ont avancé qu'il reçut ce nom parce qu'il s'était chargé d'un lourd engagement et l'avait tenu fidèlement, mais c'est une explication du mot, pas un récit établi. D'autres ont tenté de l'identifier à des personnages connus d'autres traditions ; ce sont des hypothèses, et aucune ne repose sur une preuve solide.

La plupart des savants considèrent qu'il était prophète, puisque le Coran le nomme et le loue parmi des prophètes. Quelques savants ont estimé qu'il était un homme vertueux plutôt qu'un prophète. Dans les deux cas, c'est un serviteur qu'Allah Lui-même a loué, et c'est le plus grand des honneurs.

Il vaut la peine de remarquer quelles qualités le Coran a choisi de mentionner. La première est la patience : la capacité de continuer à bien agir dans la durée, sans abandonner quand c'est difficile. La deuxième est la droiture. La troisième est d'être compté parmi les meilleurs, en compagnie de prophètes comme Ismaïl et Al-Yasa.

Mieux vaut donc dire honnêtement que nous ne connaissons pas les détails de sa vie que de remplir ce silence avec des légendes. Le Coran n'enseigne pas toujours par de longs récits. Parfois, un nom et une parole d'éloge suffisent à rappeler qu'Allah connaît Ses serviteurs fidèles, y compris ceux dont personne n'a écrit l'histoire.$tk$, $tk$Il n'est pas nécessaire d'être célèbre pour être précieux aux yeux d'Allah. Il voit la patience et la fidélité à tes engagements, même quand personne d'autre ne les remarque.$tk$, $tk$["21:85-86","38:48"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$dawud$tk$, 'fr', 17, $tk$Dawoud (David)$tk$, $tk$داود$tk$, $tk$Prophète, roi et fervent adorateur$tk$, $tk$Dawoud, paix sur lui, apparaît pour la première fois dans le Coran sur un champ de bataille. Les Enfants d'Israël, conduits par leur roi Talout, faisaient face à l'armée de Jalout. Les croyants étaient peu nombreux, mais ils demandèrent à Allah la patience et la fermeté. Ils vainquirent leurs ennemis par la permission d'Allah, et Dawoud tua Jalout. Allah lui donna alors la royauté et la sagesse, et lui enseigna ce qu'Il voulut.

Dawoud devint à la fois prophète et roi. Allah lui donna le Zabour, les Psaumes. Le Coran le décrit comme un serviteur doué de force qui revenait sans cesse vers son Seigneur. Quand il glorifiait Allah, les montagnes et les oiseaux reprenaient les louanges avec lui, le soir et au lever du soleil.

Allah rendit aussi le fer malléable pour lui et lui apprit à fabriquer des cottes de mailles aux anneaux bien mesurés, pour protéger les gens au combat. Bien qu'il fût roi, un hadith nous apprend que Dawoud ne mangeait que de ce qu'il gagnait par le travail de ses propres mains (Sahih al-Boukhari, n° 2072).

Son royaume fut affermi, et il reçut la sagesse et un jugement sûr dans la parole. Mais le Coran raconte aussi un moment où il fut éprouvé. Deux hommes en litige entrèrent dans son oratoire et lui demandèrent de juger entre eux. Dawoud donna sa réponse, puis comprit qu'Allah le mettait à l'épreuve. Il demanda pardon à son Seigneur, tomba prosterné et se repentit, et Allah lui pardonna. Le Coran ne donne pas d'autres détails sur cette épreuve, et les récits qu'on y a ajoutés ne sont pas dignes d'un prophète.

Après cela, Allah s'adressa directement à lui : Il avait fait de lui un successeur sur terre, il devait donc juger entre les gens selon la vérité et ne pas suivre ses propres désirs.

Le Coran mentionne aussi l'affaire d'un champ abîmé de nuit par les moutons de certaines gens. Dawoud et son fils Soulayman rendirent chacun un jugement, et Allah donna à Soulayman la meilleure compréhension de l'affaire, alors que tous deux avaient reçu sagesse et savoir.

Dawoud réunit ainsi des choses que l'on croit souvent séparées : le pouvoir et l'humilité, l'habileté de l'artisan et la ferveur de l'adorateur.$tk$, $tk$Quelle que soit ta responsabilité, sois juste et ne laisse pas tes envies décider à ta place. Quand tu comprends que tu as eu tort, reviens vers Allah sans attendre.$tk$, $tk$["2:251","38:17-26","21:78-80","34:10-11","17:55"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$sulayman$tk$, 'fr', 18, $tk$Soulayman (Salomon)$tk$, $tk$سليمان$tk$, $tk$Le roi qui répondait à chaque don par la gratitude$tk$, $tk$Soulayman, paix sur lui, était le fils de Dawoud, paix sur lui, et hérita de lui la prophétie et la royauté. Le Coran l'appelle un excellent serviteur, qui revenait sans cesse vers Allah. Il demanda à son Seigneur de lui pardonner et de lui accorder un royaume tel que nul n'en aurait après lui.

Allah exauça sa prière. Il mit le vent à son service, soufflant sur son ordre là où il le dirigeait. Des djinns travaillaient pour lui comme bâtisseurs et plongeurs, et fabriquaient ce qu'il voulait. Il avait appris le langage des oiseaux, et son armée était composée de djinns, d'hommes et d'oiseaux, placés en rangs.

Un jour, cette armée arriva dans une vallée de fourmis. Une fourmi avertit les autres de rentrer dans leurs demeures pour que Soulayman et ses soldats ne les écrasent pas sans s'en rendre compte. Soulayman la comprit et sourit. Au lieu d'en tirer de l'orgueil, il demanda à Allah de l'aider à être reconnaissant de Ses bienfaits et à faire le bien qu'Il agrée.

Une autre fois, il remarqua l'absence de la huppe. L'oiseau revint avec des nouvelles de Saba : une reine y régnait, avec un trône immense, et elle et son peuple se prosternaient devant le soleil au lieu d'Allah. Soulayman lui envoya une lettre, qui commençait par le nom d'Allah, le Tout Miséricordieux, le Très Miséricordieux, les invitant à venir à lui en soumission.

La reine consulta ses conseillers et choisit la prudence. Elle envoya un présent, que Soulayman refusa : ce qu'Allah lui avait donné valait mieux. Avant son arrivée, son trône lui fut apporté en un clin d'œil par quelqu'un qui avait une science du Livre. En le voyant devant lui, Soulayman dit que c'était une faveur de son Seigneur, pour éprouver s'il serait reconnaissant ou ingrat.

Quand la reine entra dans son palais, elle prit le sol de verre lisse pour de l'eau. Elle comprit qu'elle s'était trompée sur bien des choses, et déclara qu'elle se soumettait, avec Soulayman, à Allah, le Seigneur des mondes.

Le Coran raconte aussi sa mort. Il mourut appuyé sur son bâton, et les djinns continuèrent à travailler sans le savoir, jusqu'à ce qu'une petite bête de la terre ronge le bâton et qu'il tombe. Ils comprirent alors qu'ils ne connaissaient pas l'invisible.$tk$, $tk$Plus tu reçois, plus tu dois être reconnaissant et humble. Chaque capacité est une épreuve : que vas-tu en faire ?$tk$, $tk$["27:15-44","21:81-82","34:12-14","38:30-40"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$ilyas$tk$, 'fr', 19, $tk$Ilyas (Élie)$tk$, $tk$إلياس$tk$, $tk$Le messager qui détourna son peuple de Baal$tk$, $tk$Ilyas, paix sur lui, est mentionné dans deux passages du Coran. Le premier, dans la sourate Al-An'am, le cite dans une liste de prophètes, avec Zakariyya, Yahya et Issa, et dit que tous étaient du nombre des vertueux. Le second, dans la sourate As-Saffat, donne le bref récit de sa mission.

Le Coran dit qu'Ilyas était du nombre des messagers. Il s'adressa à son peuple et leur demanda s'ils n'allaient pas craindre Allah. Ils adoraient une idole appelée Baal. Ilyas leur demanda comment ils pouvaient invoquer Baal et délaisser le meilleur des créateurs : Allah, leur Seigneur et le Seigneur de leurs ancêtres.

Son argument était simple et clair. Celui qui les a créés, et qui a créé leurs ancêtres avant eux, est le seul qui mérite d'être adoré. Une idole n'a rien créé, et se tourner vers elle, c'est oublier Celui à qui l'on doit l'existence.

Le Coran nous apprend ensuite que son peuple le traita de menteur. Ils auront pour cela à rendre des comptes, à l'exception des serviteurs sincères d'Allah. Cela signifie que certains, même peu nombreux, l'ont écouté et sont restés fidèles.

Le passage se termine par un hommage. Allah a laissé à Ilyas une mention honorable parmi les générations suivantes et lui a adressé un salut de paix. Il dit que c'est ainsi qu'Il récompense les bienfaisants, et qu'Ilyas était l'un de Ses serviteurs croyants.

Le Coran ne donne pas d'autres détails. Il ne nomme ni le peuple d'Ilyas, ni le pays où il vécut, ni l'époque de sa mission, et il ne décrit pas la fin de sa vie. Les commentateurs le placent généralement parmi les prophètes envoyés aux Enfants d'Israël, et d'autres traditions racontent de longs récits à son sujet, mais ces récits ne sont confirmés ni par le Coran ni par les hadiths authentiques. Nous nous en tenons donc à ce qui est certain.

Ce qui est certain est déjà riche de sens. Ilyas s'est dressé devant son peuple et lui a posé une question que chaque génération peut se poser : que mettons-nous à la place de notre Créateur ? L'idole n'est peut-être plus une statue ; ce peut être tout ce à quoi l'on obéit ou que l'on aime plus qu'Allah. Bien des siècles plus tard, son nom est toujours prononcé avec un salut de paix.$tk$, $tk$Demande-toi honnêtement ce qui occupe la première place dans ton cœur. Dire la vérité en vaut la peine, même si peu de gens écoutent.$tk$, $tk$["37:123-132","6:85"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$al-yasa$tk$, 'fr', 20, $tk$Al-Yasa (Élisée)$tk$, $tk$اليسع$tk$, $tk$Compté parmi les élus et les meilleurs$tk$, $tk$Al-Yasa, paix sur lui, est un prophète que le Coran ne mentionne que deux fois, et chaque fois très brièvement. Il n'y a pas de récit à son sujet dans le Coran : seulement son nom, placé parmi d'autres prophètes, et des paroles d'éloge.

La première mention se trouve dans la sourate Al-An'am. Après avoir parlé d'Ibrahim, Allah énumère beaucoup des prophètes qu'Il a guidés. Parmi eux viennent Ismaïl, Al-Yasa, Younous et Lout, et le Coran dit qu'Allah les a tous favorisés au-dessus des mondes. Un peu plus loin, le passage dit au Prophète Muhammad ﷺ que ce sont ceux-là qu'Allah a guidés, et qu'il doit suivre leur guidance.

La seconde mention se trouve dans la sourate Sad. Allah dit au Prophète ﷺ de se rappeler Ismaïl, Al-Yasa et Dhoul-Kifl, et dit que tous étaient parmi les meilleurs.

C'est tout ce que le Coran dit de lui. Nous ne savons pas vers quel peuple il a été envoyé, quand il a vécu, ce qu'il a dit à son peuple ni ce qu'on lui a répondu. Aucun hadith authentique ne raconte sa vie. Les commentateurs le placent généralement parmi les prophètes des Enfants d'Israël, après Ilyas, et d'autres traditions rapportent des récits sur lui. Comme ces récits ne sont confirmés ni par le Coran ni par les hadiths authentiques, nous ne les présentons pas comme des faits.

Mieux vaut accepter ce silence que le combler. Le Coran dit lui-même ailleurs qu'il y a des messagers dont Allah a raconté l'histoire et d'autres dont Il ne l'a pas racontée. Croire en tous les prophètes ne demande pas de connaître le détail de chaque vie.

Ces deux versets nous apprennent pourtant quelque chose. Al-Yasa est décrit comme choisi, guidé, favorisé et parmi les meilleurs. Ces mots viennent d'Allah Lui-même, qui sait ce que contient chaque cœur. Et il est dit au Prophète Muhammad ﷺ de suivre la guidance de ces prophètes, ce qui signifie que la voie d'Al-Yasa était celle de tous les autres : adorer Allah seul et faire le bien.

Son nom dans le Coran est comme un signe discret. Il rappelle que la chaîne des prophètes est longue, que chacun d'eux a porté le même message, et que beaucoup de ceux qui ont fait un grand bien ne sont pleinement connus que d'Allah.$tk$, $tk$Fais le bien sans attendre que l'on raconte ton histoire. Être parmi les meilleurs aux yeux d'Allah compte plus qu'être connu des gens.$tk$, $tk$["6:86","38:48"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$yunus$tk$, 'fr', 21, $tk$Younous (Jonas)$tk$, $tk$يونس$tk$, $tk$L'homme au poisson$tk$, $tk$Younous, paix sur lui, fut envoyé comme messager à un peuple qui, selon le Coran, comptait cent mille personnes ou davantage. Le Coran l'appelle aussi Dhoun-Noun, l'homme au poisson.

Son histoire commence par un départ. Younous partit en colère. Le Coran ne le cache pas : il dit qu'il s'en alla en pensant qu'Allah ne le mettrait pas dans la gêne. Les prophètes sont les meilleurs des hommes, et le Coran nous montre comment ils reviennent vers Allah lorsqu'ils trébuchent.

Younous monta sur un bateau chargé. On tira au sort à bord, et il fut de ceux qui perdirent. Il fut jeté à la mer, et un grand poisson l'avala. Le Coran dit qu'à ce moment-là il était blâmable.

Dans les ténèbres, Younous appela son Seigneur. Il proclama qu'il n'y a de dieu qu'Allah, Le glorifia, et reconnut qu'il avait été du nombre des injustes. Il ne chercha aucune excuse et n'accusa personne d'autre. Cette courte invocation est l'une des plus célèbres du Coran.

Allah l'exauça et le sauva de sa détresse, et le Coran ajoute que c'est ainsi qu'Il sauve les croyants. Il dit aussi que si Younous n'avait pas été de ceux qui glorifient Allah, il serait resté dans le ventre du poisson jusqu'au Jour de la Résurrection.

Il fut rejeté sur un rivage nu, malade et affaibli, et Allah fit pousser au-dessus de lui un plant de courge. Le Coran dit ensuite qu'il fut envoyé à son peuple, qu'ils crurent, et qu'Allah leur accorda de jouir de la vie pour un temps. Le Coran souligne que le peuple de Younous est un cas à part : ils crurent à temps, et leur foi les préserva d'un châtiment humiliant.

Le Coran ne nomme pas la cité de Younous et ne dit pas combien de temps il resta dans le poisson, et nous n'avons pas besoin de le savoir.

Allah dit au Prophète Muhammad ﷺ d'être patient et de ne pas être comme l'homme au poisson lorsqu'il appela dans sa détresse. Il dit aussi que son Seigneur a choisi Younous et l'a placé parmi les vertueux. Son histoire porte donc à la fois une mise en garde contre l'impatience et un grand espoir.$tk$, $tk$N'abandonne pas un devoir par découragement. Et si tu as trébuché, aucune obscurité n'est trop profonde : reconnais ta faute et invoque Allah.$tk$, $tk$["37:139-148","21:87-88","10:98","68:48-50"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$zakariyya$tk$, 'fr', 22, $tk$Zakariyya (Zacharie)$tk$, $tk$زكريا$tk$, $tk$Le serviteur qui n'a jamais désespéré de sa prière$tk$, $tk$Zakariyya, paix sur lui, était un prophète parmi les Enfants d'Israël. Le Coran le présente d'abord comme le tuteur de Maryam, la mère d'Issa, paix sur eux. Elle avait été consacrée au service d'Allah, et c'est à Zakariyya qu'elle fut confiée.

Chaque fois qu'il venait la voir dans le lieu où elle priait, il trouvait de la nourriture auprès d'elle. Il lui demanda d'où cela venait. Elle répondit que cela venait d'Allah, qui donne à qui Il veut sans compter.

Cette réponse le toucha profondément. Zakariyya était un homme très âgé, et son épouse n'avait jamais pu avoir d'enfant. Pourtant, en voyant la générosité d'Allah envers Maryam, il se tourna sur-le-champ vers son Seigneur et Lui demanda une bonne descendance.

Le Coran rapporte cette prière avec tendresse. Zakariyya invoqua son Seigneur à voix basse. Il dit que ses os s'étaient affaiblis et que ses cheveux avaient blanchi, mais qu'il n'avait jamais été déçu en Le priant. Il s'inquiétait pour ceux qui viendraient après lui, et il demanda un héritier qui soit agréé d'Allah. Il dit aussi : Seigneur, ne me laisse pas seul, et Tu es le meilleur des héritiers.

La réponse arriva alors qu'il se tenait debout en prière. Les anges lui annoncèrent un fils nommé Yahya, un nom qu'Allah n'avait donné à personne auparavant. Il serait noble, chaste et prophète, du nombre des vertueux.

Zakariyya s'étonna : comment aurait-il un fils à son âge, avec une épouse qui ne pouvait pas enfanter ? Il lui fut répondu que cela est facile pour Allah, qui fait ce qu'Il veut. Il demanda un signe. Le signe fut qu'il ne parlerait pas aux gens pendant trois jours, sinon par gestes, alors qu'il était en bonne santé. Il sortit vers son peuple et leur fit signe de glorifier Allah matin et soir.

Le Coran dit qu'Allah l'exauça, lui donna Yahya et guérit son épouse. Il explique ensuite pourquoi cette famille fut honorée : ils s'empressaient de faire le bien, ils invoquaient Allah avec espoir et crainte, et ils étaient humbles devant Lui.$tk$, $tk$Continue à demander à Allah avec espoir, quels que soient ton âge ou ta situation. Le bien que tu vois chez les autres peut être une invitation à demander davantage pour toi.$tk$, $tk$["3:37-41","19:2-11","21:89-90"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$yahya$tk$, 'fr', 23, $tk$Yahya (Jean)$tk$, $tk$يحيى$tk$, $tk$Sage dès l'enfance, doux et pur$tk$, $tk$Yahya, paix sur lui, est le fils de Zakariyya, paix sur lui. Il naquit en réponse à la prière de son père âgé, alors que rien ne semblait le permettre. Allah Lui-même choisit son nom, et le Coran dit que personne n'avait porté ce nom avant lui.

Le Coran ne raconte pas les événements de sa vie sous forme de récit. Il donne plutôt un portrait de son caractère, en quelques versets de la sourate Maryam, et ce portrait est l'un des plus beaux du Livre.

Allah lui ordonna de tenir fermement l'Écriture, et lui donna la sagesse alors qu'il était encore enfant. Yahya n'attendit pas d'être âgé pour prendre sa foi au sérieux : dès ses premières années, il comprit ce qu'Allah lui demandait et s'y attacha avec force.

Allah lui donna aussi de la tendresse de Sa part et de la pureté. Il craignait Allah. Il était bon envers ses parents, et il n'était ni arrogant ni désobéissant. Dans la sourate Al Imran, les anges l'annoncent à Zakariyya comme celui qui confirmerait une parole venant d'Allah, et comme noble, chaste et prophète, du nombre des vertueux. Les commentateurs expliquent que cette parole venant d'Allah désigne Issa, paix sur lui, dont Yahya confirma la mission.

Ailleurs, le Coran nomme Yahya avec Zakariyya, Issa et Ilyas, et dit que tous étaient du nombre des vertueux. Dans le hadith du Voyage nocturne, le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ rapporte qu'il a rencontré Yahya et Issa ensemble au deuxième ciel, et qu'ils étaient cousins (Sahih al-Boukhari, n° 3887).

Le portrait s'achève par un salut qu'Allah lui adresse : paix sur lui le jour où il naquit, le jour où il mourra et le jour où il sera ressuscité vivant. Ce sont les trois moments où l'être humain est le plus vulnérable, et la sécurité est promise à Yahya dans chacun d'eux.

Le Coran ne décrit pas comment Yahya appela son peuple ni comment il mourut. Des récits de sa mort existent dans d'autres sources, mais ils ne se trouvent pas dans le Coran, et nous ne les présentons pas comme certains.

Yahya montre que la force et la douceur vont ensemble. Il était ferme avec l'Écriture et doux avec les gens, sérieux dans sa foi et bon envers ses parents.$tk$, $tk$On n'est jamais trop jeune pour prendre sa foi et ses valeurs au sérieux. Sois ferme sur ce qui est juste et doux avec les gens, à commencer par tes parents.$tk$, $tk$["19:12-15","3:39","21:90","6:85"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$isa$tk$, 'fr', 24, $tk$Issa (Jésus)$tk$, $tk$عيسى$tk$, $tk$Le Messie, fils de Maryam$tk$, $tk$Issa, paix sur lui, est l'un des plus grands messagers d'Allah. Le Coran l'appelle le Messie, fils de Maryam (Marie), et accorde à sa mère une place unique : une sourate entière porte son nom.

Maryam était une jeune femme pure et pieuse. Un jour qu'elle s'était retirée loin des siens pour adorer, un ange lui apparut et lui annonça qu'elle aurait un fils pur. Elle s'étonna : comment aurait-elle un enfant alors qu'aucun homme ne l'avait touchée ? L'ange répondit que cela est facile pour Allah, qui crée ce qu'Il veut. Quand Il décide une chose, Il lui dit seulement : Sois, et elle est.

Maryam accoucha seule, près du tronc d'un palmier, dans la douleur et la détresse. Elle fut réconfortée : un ruisseau coulait à ses pieds, et des dattes fraîches tombaient de l'arbre lorsqu'elle le secouait.

Quand elle revint vers les siens en portant l'enfant, ils l'accusèrent. Elle se contenta de le désigner. Le nouveau-né parla alors, par la permission d'Allah. Il dit qu'il était le serviteur d'Allah, qu'Allah lui avait donné l'Écriture et avait fait de lui un prophète, l'avait rendu béni où qu'il soit, et lui avait prescrit la prière, l'aumône et la bonté envers sa mère.

Issa fut envoyé comme messager aux Enfants d'Israël. Il confirma la Torah venue avant lui et reçut l'Évangile. Allah le soutint par des signes clairs : par la permission d'Allah, il guérissait l'aveugle et le lépreux, rendait la vie aux morts, et façonnait dans l'argile un oiseau qui devenait un oiseau vivant. Le Coran répète chaque fois que ces miracles se produisaient par la permission d'Allah.

Son message était celui de tous les prophètes : Allah est mon Seigneur et votre Seigneur, adorez-Le donc ; voilà le droit chemin. Ses disciples crurent en lui et déclarèrent qu'ils se soumettaient à Allah. À leur demande, Issa pria pour qu'une table servie descende du ciel comme un signe.

Le Coran dit aussi qu'Issa annonça un messager qui viendrait après lui. Lorsque ses ennemis complotèrent contre lui, le Coran enseigne qu'ils ne l'ont ni tué ni crucifié, bien que cela leur ait semblé ainsi, et qu'Allah l'a élevé vers Lui.

Les musulmans aiment et honorent Issa et sa mère.$tk$, $tk$Honore ta mère et défends la vérité avec douceur. La vraie grandeur consiste à être un serviteur fidèle d'Allah et à apporter le bien partout où l'on se trouve.$tk$, $tk$["3:45-51","19:16-36","5:110-115","4:157-158","61:6"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$muhammad$tk$, 'fr', 25, $tk$Muhammad ﷺ$tk$, $tk$محمد$tk$, $tk$Le Sceau des prophètes, une miséricorde pour les mondes$tk$, $tk$Le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ est né à La Mecque vers l'an 570 de l'ère chrétienne, dans la tribu de Quraych. Son père mourut avant sa naissance et sa mère alors qu'il était tout petit ; il fut donc élevé par son grand-père, puis par son oncle Abou Talib. Jeune homme, il était si honnête qu'on le surnommait al-Amin, le digne de confiance. Il épousa Khadija, qu'Allah l'agrée, une commerçante respectée de La Mecque.

Il avait l'habitude de se retirer dans la grotte de Hira, sur une montagne proche de La Mecque, pour méditer et adorer. C'est là, alors qu'il avait environ quarante ans, que l'ange Jibril (Gabriel) lui apporta les premières paroles du Coran, lui ordonnant de lire au nom de son Seigneur qui a créé. Khadija fut la première à croire en lui.

Pendant treize ans, il appela les habitants de La Mecque à adorer Allah seul, à être honnêtes, à prendre soin des orphelins et des pauvres, et à abandonner les idoles. Quelques personnes le suivirent, mais les chefs de Quraych s'opposèrent à lui, et les croyants subirent insultes, boycott et persécutions. Il répondit par la patience.

En 622, il émigra vers la ville de Yathrib, qui devint Médine. Ce voyage, l'Hégire, marque le début du calendrier musulman. Le Coran rappelle comment, caché dans une grotte avec son compagnon Abou Bakr, il lui dit de ne pas s'attrister, car Allah était avec eux.

À Médine, il construisit une mosquée, unit par la fraternité les émigrés de La Mecque et les habitants de Médine, et organisa la vie d'une communauté. Les musulmans durent se défendre à plusieurs reprises, et le Prophète ﷺ conclut aussi une trêve avec Quraych. Lorsqu'elle fut rompue, il revint à La Mecque en l'an 8 de l'Hégire, presque sans combat. Il pardonna à ceux qui l'avaient combattu pendant des années et débarrassa la Kaaba de ses idoles. Les gens entrèrent alors en foule dans la religion d'Allah.

En l'an 10, il accomplit son pèlerinage d'adieu. À Arafat, il s'adressa à une foule immense : il rappela que la vie, les biens et l'honneur sont sacrés, recommanda de bien traiter les femmes, et déclara abolies les anciennes vengeances de sang et l'usure. C'est pendant ce pèlerinage que fut révélé le verset annonçant que la religion avait été parachevée.

Il mourut à Médine en l'an 11 de l'Hégire, en 632 de l'ère chrétienne. Le Coran l'appelle le Messager d'Allah et le dernier des prophètes, envoyé comme miséricorde pour les mondes.$tk$, $tk$Sois digne de confiance avant de parler de foi : c'est le caractère qui ouvre les cœurs. Quand tu es en position de force, choisis la miséricorde et le pardon.$tk$, $tk$["96:1-5","9:40","110:1-3","5:3","33:40","21:107"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.premium_content (kind, lang, payload) values ('hajj', 'fr', $tk${"umrah":{"title":"La Omra pas à pas","intro":"La Omra est la visite de la Maison sacrée à La Mecque. Elle peut s'accomplir presque toute l'année et ne prend que quelques heures. Elle se compose de quatre actes, toujours dans le même ordre : l'entrée en ihram au miqat, le tawaf autour de la Kaaba, le sa'y entre Safa et Marwa, puis le rasage ou le raccourcissement des cheveux.\n\nCe guide est un résumé. Il ne remplace ni un enseignant qualifié ni les consignes des autorités sur place, qu'il faut toujours suivre. Quand les écoles juridiques divergent sur un détail, nous indiquons la pratique courante : demandez à un savant de confiance pour votre cas.","chapters":[{"id":"preparation","title":"Avant le départ","body":"Un bon voyage commence à la maison. Commencez par votre intention : vous partez pour Allah seul, non pour être vu ou félicité. Repentez-vous sincèrement, remboursez vos dettes autant que possible et demandez pardon aux personnes que vous avez pu léser. Financez le voyage avec de l'argent licite.\n\nEnsuite, apprenez les rites avant de partir. Le pèlerin qui sait ce qu'il doit faire est serein et peut donner son cœur à l'adoration au lieu de s'inquiéter. Relisez ce guide plusieurs fois et, si possible, suivez un cours avec un enseignant.\n\nEnfin, préparez votre corps et vos papiers : marchez régulièrement dans les semaines qui précèdent, vérifiez visa, permis et vaccins, et voyagez léger. Vous marcherez beaucoup, souvent sous la chaleur et dans la foule.","steps":["Rendez votre intention sincère : ce voyage est pour Allah seul.","Repentez-vous, réglez vos dettes et demandez pardon à votre entourage.","Apprenez les quatre actes de la Omra et leur ordre.","Vérifiez passeport, visa, permis et vaccins exigés.","Renseignez-vous sur le miqat de votre trajet et le moment où vous y arriverez.","Entraînez-vous à marcher et préparez un bagage léger et confortable."],"duas":[]},{"id":"miqat-ihram","title":"Le miqat et l'ihram","body":"Le miqat est la limite à ne pas franchir sans être en ihram. Il y en a cinq : Dhul-Hulayfah pour ceux qui viennent de Médine, al-Juhfah, Qarn al-Manazil, Yalamlam et Dhat Irq. En avion, l'équipage annonce en général le miqat : soyez prêt avant.\n\nUn bain complet (ghusl) est recommandé avant l'ihram. Les hommes portent deux étoffes non cousues, l'une autour de la taille, l'autre sur les épaules ; les femmes gardent leurs vêtements pudiques habituels. Formulez ensuite l'intention dans votre cœur et prononcez la talbiyah, à voix haute pour les hommes, à voix basse pour les femmes.\n\nL'ihram est un état, pas un tissu. Sont alors interdits : couper cheveux ou ongles, se parfumer, chasser, conclure un mariage, avoir des relations conjugales. Les hommes ne portent pas de vêtements ajustés au corps et gardent la tête découverte ; les femmes ne portent ni voile de visage (niqab) ni gants (Sahih al-Bukhari 1838). Évitez aussi les disputes (Coran 2:197).","steps":["Avant le miqat, coupez vos ongles et prenez un bain (ghusl) si vous le pouvez.","Hommes : mettez les deux étoffes de l'ihram. Femmes : gardez une tenue pudique ordinaire.","Les hommes peuvent parfumer leur corps, pas les étoffes, avant l'intention ; jamais après.","Au miqat, formulez l'intention de la Omra dans votre cœur et dites : Labbayka Allahumma 'umrah.","Répétez souvent la talbiyah sur le chemin de La Mecque.","À partir de là, respectez tous les interdits de l'ihram.","Cessez la talbiyah au moment de commencer le tawaf."],"duas":[{"ar":"لَبَّيْكَ اللَّهُمَّ لَبَّيْكَ، لَبَّيْكَ لاَ شَرِيكَ لَكَ لَبَّيْكَ، إِنَّ الْحَمْدَ وَالنِّعْمَةَ لَكَ وَالْمُلْكَ، لاَ شَرِيكَ لَكَ","tr":"Labbayka Allahumma labbayk, labbayka la sharika laka labbayk, inna al-hamda wa an-ni'mata laka wa al-mulk, la sharika lak","t":"Me voici, ô Allah, me voici. Me voici, Tu n'as pas d'associé, me voici. Certes la louange et le bienfait T'appartiennent, ainsi que la royauté. Tu n'as pas d'associé.","source":"La talbiyah — Sahih al-Bukhari 1549 ; Sahih Muslim 1184"}]},{"id":"tawaf","title":"Le tawaf autour de la Kaaba","body":"Le tawaf, ce sont sept tours autour de la Kaaba, gardée à sa gauche. Les ablutions sont requises. Chaque tour commence et s'achève à la Pierre noire : faites-lui face, prononcez le takbir, et ne l'embrassez ou ne la touchez que si c'est facile ; sinon, faites-lui simplement signe. Beaucoup y disent « Bismillah, Allahu akbar », comme rapporté du compagnon Ibn Umar. Contournez le muret en demi-cercle (le Hijr), sans le traverser.\n\nLors de ce tawaf d'arrivée, les hommes découvrent l'épaule droite (idtiba) et pressent le pas, à petites foulées, durant les trois premiers tours (raml). Les femmes marchent normalement.\n\nIl n'existe aucune invocation fixe et obligatoire pour chaque tour : vous pouvez faire l'invocation de votre choix, dans n'importe quelle langue, évoquer Allah ou réciter le Coran. Une seule est rapportée, entre le coin yéménite et la Pierre noire : Coran 2:201. Ensuite, priez deux rak'at derrière le Maqam Ibrahim (Coran 2:125), puis buvez de l'eau de Zamzam.","steps":["Assurez-vous d'avoir vos ablutions. Les hommes découvrent l'épaule droite (idtiba).","Commencez à hauteur de la Pierre noire : faites-lui face, dites le takbir et faites un signe vers elle si vous ne pouvez l'atteindre.","Faites sept tours, la Kaaba à votre gauche, en passant à l'extérieur du Hijr.","Les hommes marchent d'un pas rapide pendant les trois premiers tours (raml), puis normalement.","Touchez le coin yéménite si c'est facile ; entre lui et la Pierre noire, récitez Coran 2:201.","Pendant les tours, faites les invocations de votre choix.","Après le septième tour, les hommes recouvrent l'épaule ; priez deux rak'at derrière le Maqam Ibrahim, ou ailleurs dans la mosquée s'il y a foule.","Buvez de l'eau de Zamzam et invoquez Allah."],"duas":[{"ar":"اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ","tr":"Allahu akbar","t":"Allah est le Plus Grand.","source":"Takbir à hauteur de la Pierre noire — Sahih al-Bukhari 1613"},{"ar":"رَبَّنَآ ءَاتِنَا فِي ٱلدُّنۡيَا حَسَنَةࣰ وَفِي ٱلۡأٓخِرَةِ حَسَنَةࣰ وَقِنَا عَذَابَ ٱلنَّارِ","tr":"Rabbana atina fi ad-dunya hasanatan wa fi al-akhirati hasanatan wa qina 'adhab an-nar","t":"Notre Seigneur, accorde-nous un bien ici-bas et un bien dans l'au-delà, et préserve-nous du châtiment du Feu.","source":"Coran 2:201 — récité entre le coin yéménite et la Pierre noire : Sunan Abi Dawud 1892 (hasan)"}]},{"id":"say","title":"Le sa'y entre Safa et Marwa","body":"Après le tawaf, rendez-vous à Safa. Le sa'y compte sept trajets entre les deux collines : de Safa à Marwa, c'est un trajet ; de Marwa à Safa, le deuxième, et ainsi de suite. On termine donc à Marwa.\n\nEn approchant de Safa pour la première fois, récitez le début de Coran 2:158, comme l'a fait le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ (Sahih Muslim 1218). Montez un peu, tournez-vous vers la Kaaba, levez les mains, proclamez la grandeur et l'unicité d'Allah avec les paroles ci-dessous, puis faites vos propres invocations. Cela se fait trois fois. Faites de même à Marwa à chaque arrivée.\n\nEntre les deux lumières vertes, les hommes pressent le pas ; les femmes marchent normalement. Comme pour le tawaf, aucune invocation particulière n'est imposée pour chaque trajet. Les ablutions sont recommandées, et les personnes âgées ou malades peuvent utiliser un fauteuil roulant.","steps":["Allez à Safa et récitez le début de Coran 2:158 en approchant, cette première fois seulement.","Sur Safa, face à la Kaaba, levez les mains, dites le dhikr de Safa et invoquez, trois fois.","Marchez vers Marwa ; les hommes pressent le pas entre les lumières vertes.","Sur Marwa, tournez-vous vers la Kaaba et répétez le dhikr et les invocations.","Revenez à Safa : c'est le deuxième trajet.","Continuez jusqu'au septième trajet, qui s'achève à Marwa."],"duas":[{"ar":"إِنَّ ٱلصَّفَا وَٱلۡمَرۡوَةَ مِن شَعَآئِرِ ٱللَّهِ","tr":"Inna as-Safa wa al-Marwata min sha'a'iri Allah","t":"Certes, Safa et Marwa sont parmi les rites sacrés d'Allah.","source":"Coran 2:158 (premiers mots) — récité en approchant de Safa : Sahih Muslim 1218"},{"ar":"لاَ إِلَهَ إِلاَّ اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لاَ شَرِيكَ لَهُ لَهُ الْمُلْكُ وَلَهُ الْحَمْدُ وَهُوَ عَلَى كُلِّ شَىْءٍ قَدِيرٌ لاَ إِلَهَ إِلاَّ اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ أَنْجَزَ وَعْدَهُ وَنَصَرَ عَبْدَهُ وَهَزَمَ الأَحْزَابَ وَحْدَهُ","tr":"La ilaha illa Allahu wahdahu la sharika lah, lahu al-mulku wa lahu al-hamd, wa huwa 'ala kulli shay'in qadir. La ilaha illa Allahu wahdah, anjaza wa'dah, wa nasara 'abdah, wa hazama al-ahzaba wahdah","t":"Il n'y a de divinité qu'Allah, Seul, sans associé. À Lui la royauté, à Lui la louange, et Il est capable de toute chose. Il n'y a de divinité qu'Allah, Seul ; Il a tenu Sa promesse, secouru Son serviteur et vaincu Seul les coalisés.","source":"Dhikr sur Safa et Marwa — Sahih Muslim 1218"}]},{"id":"halq-taqsir","title":"Rasage ou raccourcissement des cheveux","body":"Le dernier acte de la Omra consiste à se raser la tête (halq) ou à raccourcir les cheveux (taqsir). Pour les hommes, raser toute la tête est préférable ; s'ils raccourcissent, la pratique courante est de raccourcir les cheveux de toute la tête, et pas seulement quelques mèches. Les femmes ne se rasent jamais : elles rassemblent leurs cheveux et en coupent l'extrémité sur la longueur d'un bout de doigt environ.\n\nL'homme qui accomplit la Omra peu avant le Hajj (tamattu) choisit souvent de raccourcir à ce moment, afin qu'il reste des cheveux à raser le dixième jour.\n\nAllez chez les coiffeurs à l'extérieur de la mosquée plutôt que de couper des cheveux dans l'espace du sa'y, et les femmes coupent leurs cheveux à l'abri des regards. Par cet acte, la Omra est achevée et tous les interdits de l'ihram prennent fin.","steps":["Quittez l'espace du sa'y une fois le septième trajet terminé.","Hommes : rasez la tête, ou raccourcissez les cheveux de toute la tête.","Femmes : coupez la longueur d'un bout de doigt à l'extrémité des cheveux, à l'abri des regards.","Vous êtes désormais sorti de l'ihram : votre Omra est achevée."],"duas":[]},{"id":"after","title":"Après la Omra","body":"Votre Omra est terminée : remerciez Allah, qui vous a permis d'atteindre Sa Maison. Tout ce que l'ihram interdisait redevient permis, et vous pouvez remettre vos vêtements habituels.\n\nProfitez bien des jours qui vous restent à La Mecque. Accomplissez les cinq prières à la Mosquée sacrée aussi souvent que possible, faites des tawaf supplémentaires quand l'affluence le permet, lisez le Coran et invoquez Allah pour vous, votre famille et tous les musulmans. Soyez doux avec les autres pèlerins et patient face à la fatigue et à la foule.\n\nBeaucoup de pèlerins se rendent aussi à Médine pour prier dans la Mosquée du Prophète. Cette visite est une œuvre bénie en elle-même, mais elle ne fait pas partie de la Omra. De retour chez vous, essayez de garder les bonnes habitudes acquises là-bas.","steps":["Remerciez Allah et remettez vos vêtements habituels.","Priez à la Mosquée sacrée aussi souvent que possible.","Faites des tawaf supplémentaires, lisez le Coran et multipliez les invocations.","Gardez les bonnes habitudes du voyage une fois rentré."],"duas":[]}]},"hajj":{"title":"Le Hajj jour après jour","intro":"Le Hajj est le cinquième pilier de l'islam. Il est obligatoire une fois dans la vie pour tout musulman adulte qui a les moyens de faire le voyage (Coran 3:97). Ses rites principaux se déroulent du 8 au 13 Dhul-Hijja, entre La Mecque, Mina, Arafat et Muzdalifah.\n\nIl existe trois manières de l'accomplir. Dans le tamattu, on accomplit une Omra complète pendant les mois du Hajj, on sort de l'ihram, puis on entre de nouveau en ihram pour le Hajj le 8. Dans le qiran, on réunit Omra et Hajj dans un seul ihram, sans en sortir entre les deux. Dans l'ifrad, on entre en ihram pour le Hajj seul. Un sacrifice est dû pour le tamattu et le qiran (Coran 2:196), pas pour l'ifrad. Les trois sont valides ; les savants divergent sur la meilleure, et la plupart des pèlerins venus de loin font le tamattu.\n\nCe guide est un résumé. Il ne remplace ni un enseignant qualifié ni les consignes des autorités sur place et de votre groupe, qui organisent les déplacements et les horaires pour la sécurité de tous. Quand les écoles juridiques divergent sur un détail, nous indiquons la pratique courante : demandez à un savant de confiance pour votre cas.","chapters":[{"id":"day-8-tarwiyah","title":"8 Dhul-Hijja : le jour de Tarwiyah, à Mina","body":"Le Hajj commence le 8 Dhul-Hijja, appelé jour de Tarwiyah. Le pèlerin qui accomplit le tamattu entre de nouveau en ihram, cette fois pour le Hajj, depuis l'endroit où il loge à La Mecque : bain, vêtements de l'ihram, intention, puis talbiyah. Ceux qui accomplissent le qiran ou l'ifrad sont toujours en ihram depuis le miqat.\n\nTous se rendent ensuite à Mina, une vallée de tentes à quelques kilomètres de La Mecque. On y prie Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib, Isha puis le Fajr du 9, chacune à son heure ; les prières de quatre rak'at sont raccourcies à deux, sans être regroupées.\n\nCette journée et cette nuit à Mina sont une sunna et un temps de préparation. Reposez-vous, répétez la talbiyah, lisez le Coran et préparez votre cœur pour le lendemain, le plus grand jour du Hajj.","steps":["Tamattu : prenez un bain et mettez-vous en ihram là où vous logez à La Mecque.","Formulez l'intention du Hajj dans votre cœur et dites : Labbayka Allahumma hajjan.","Rendez-vous à Mina avec votre groupe en répétant la talbiyah.","Priez Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib et Isha à Mina, chacune à son heure, en raccourcissant les prières de quatre rak'at.","Passez la nuit à Mina et priez-y le Fajr."],"duas":[{"ar":"لَبَّيْكَ اللَّهُمَّ لَبَّيْكَ، لَبَّيْكَ لاَ شَرِيكَ لَكَ لَبَّيْكَ، إِنَّ الْحَمْدَ وَالنِّعْمَةَ لَكَ وَالْمُلْكَ، لاَ شَرِيكَ لَكَ","tr":"Labbayka Allahumma labbayk, labbayka la sharika laka labbayk, inna al-hamda wa an-ni'mata laka wa al-mulk, la sharika lak","t":"Me voici, ô Allah, me voici. Me voici, Tu n'as pas d'associé, me voici. Certes la louange et le bienfait T'appartiennent, ainsi que la royauté. Tu n'as pas d'associé.","source":"La talbiyah — Sahih al-Bukhari 1549 ; Sahih Muslim 1184"}]},{"id":"day-9-arafat","title":"9 Dhul-Hijja : Arafat, puis Muzdalifah","body":"La station à Arafat est le pilier du Hajj : celui qui la manque a manqué le Hajj. Le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ a enseigné que le Hajj, c'est Arafat (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 889).\n\nAprès le lever du soleil, quittez Mina pour Arafat et vérifiez que vous êtes bien à l'intérieur de ses limites, indiquées par des panneaux. Après le sermon, Dhuhr et Asr sont priées ensemble et raccourcies, à l'heure du Dhuhr. Vient alors le cœur du Hajj : de midi jusqu'au coucher du soleil, tournez-vous vers la qibla et invoquez Allah, demandez pardon, priez pour ceux que vous aimez. Aucun texte n'est imposé. Ne partez pas avant le coucher du soleil.\n\nAprès le coucher du soleil, rendez-vous calmement à Muzdalifah. Vous y priez Maghrib et Isha ensemble, Isha raccourcie, puis vous vous reposez pour la nuit à ciel ouvert. Vous pouvez y ramasser les petits cailloux de la lapidation.","steps":["Après le lever du soleil, allez de Mina à Arafat en répétant la talbiyah.","Vérifiez que vous êtes à l'intérieur des limites d'Arafat.","Priez Dhuhr et Asr regroupées et raccourcies à l'heure du Dhuhr.","Passez l'après-midi en invocations, dhikr et repentir, tourné vers la qibla.","Restez à Arafat jusqu'au coucher complet du soleil.","Rendez-vous calmement à Muzdalifah et priez-y Maghrib et Isha regroupées.","Ramassez de petits cailloux, de la taille d'un pois chiche, puis dormez.","Priez le Fajr, invoquez face à la qibla et partez vers Mina avant le lever du soleil."],"duas":[]},{"id":"day-10-nahr","title":"10 Dhul-Hijja : le jour du sacrifice","body":"Le 10, jour de l'Aïd, est la journée la plus chargée du Hajj. Quatre actes s'enchaînent, idéalement ainsi. D'abord, à Mina, lancez sept cailloux un à un sur Jamrat al-Aqaba, la plus grande stèle, avec le takbir à chaque jet ; la talbiyah s'arrête ici (Sahih al-Bukhari 1685). Ensuite, le sacrifice, dû pour le tamattu et le qiran, souvent réglé par un bon officiel. Puis les hommes se rasent ou raccourcissent leurs cheveux ; les femmes en coupent un bout de doigt. Enfin, à La Mecque, le tawaf al-ifadah, pilier du Hajj, suivi du sa'y s'il est encore dû.\n\nAprès la lapidation et le rasage, les interdits de l'ihram sont levés, sauf les relations conjugales, permises après le tawaf al-ifadah. Les écoles divergent sur les détails : demandez à un savant de confiance pour votre cas.\n\nSi l'ordre change, aucun mal : le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ l'a dit à ceux qui l'interrogeaient ce jour-là (Sahih al-Bukhari 83).","steps":["À Mina, lancez sept cailloux un à un sur Jamrat al-Aqaba en disant Allahu akbar à chaque jet.","Cessez la talbiyah en commençant la lapidation.","Offrez le sacrifice, ou assurez-vous qu'il est fait en votre nom (tamattu et qiran).","Les hommes rasent ou raccourcissent toute la tête ; les femmes coupent la longueur d'un bout de doigt.","Remettez des vêtements ordinaires : tout redevient permis, sauf les relations conjugales.","Allez à La Mecque pour le tawaf al-ifadah, sans idtiba ni raml, et priez deux rak'at.","Accomplissez le sa'y du Hajj si vous le devez encore.","Retournez à Mina pour y passer la nuit."],"duas":[{"ar":"اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ","tr":"Allahu akbar","t":"Allah est le Plus Grand.","source":"Takbir à chaque caillou — Sahih al-Bukhari 1751"}]},{"id":"days-11-13-tashriq","title":"11–13 Dhul-Hijja : les jours de Tashriq","body":"Les jours de Tashriq se passent à Mina. Ce sont des jours d'évocation d'Allah (Coran 2:203), et l'on y passe aussi les nuits.\n\nChaque jour, après midi, on lapide les trois jamarat dans l'ordre : la petite, la moyenne, puis Jamrat al-Aqaba, avec sept cailloux chacune, en disant le takbir à chaque jet. Après la première et après la deuxième, mettez-vous à l'écart, tournez-vous vers la qibla et invoquez longuement, les mains levées ; après la troisième, repartez sans vous arrêter (Sahih al-Bukhari 1751).\n\nVous pouvez quitter Mina le 12 après la lapidation, avant le coucher du soleil ; celui qui reste lapide de nouveau le 13. Les deux sont permis (Coran 2:203). Suivez l'horaire donné à votre groupe. Si vous êtes faible ou malade, ou pour l'heure exacte de la lapidation, demandez à un savant de confiance pour votre cas.","steps":["Passez les nuits du 11 et du 12 à Mina.","Chaque jour après midi, emportez 21 cailloux aux jamarat.","Lapidez la petite jamrah de sept cailloux, puis mettez-vous à l'écart et invoquez.","Lapidez la jamrah moyenne de sept cailloux, puis mettez-vous à l'écart et invoquez.","Lapidez Jamrat al-Aqaba de sept cailloux et repartez sans vous arrêter.","Le 12, quittez Mina avant le coucher du soleil si vous souhaitez partir plus tôt.","Sinon, restez la nuit et lapidez de nouveau les trois jamarat le 13."],"duas":[]},{"id":"farewell-tawaf","title":"Le tawaf d'adieu","body":"Quand tous les rites sont terminés et que vous êtes sur le point de quitter La Mecque, la dernière chose à faire est le tawaf d'adieu (tawaf al-wada). Il a été ordonné aux gens de faire de la Maison leur dernière étape ; seule la femme en période de règles en a été dispensée (Sahih al-Bukhari 1755 ; Sahih Muslim 1328).\n\nC'est un tawaf ordinaire de sept tours, en vêtements de tous les jours, sans idtiba ni raml, suivi de deux rak'at. Il n'y a pas de sa'y après. Faites-le une fois vos bagages prêts et votre départ proche ; une courte attente pour le transport ou pour vos compagnons ne pose pas de problème.\n\nQuittez la Mosquée sacrée le cœur reconnaissant. Demandez à Allah d'accepter votre Hajj, de vous pardonner et de vous permettre de revenir. Puis efforcez-vous de rentrer chez vous meilleur que vous n'êtes parti.","steps":["Terminez d'abord tous vos rites et préparez vos bagages.","Faites sept tours autour de la Kaaba en vêtements ordinaires.","Priez deux rak'at après le tawaf.","Demandez à Allah d'accepter votre Hajj, puis quittez La Mecque sans tarder.","La femme en période de règles part sans faire ce tawaf."],"duas":[]}]},"mistakes":[{"title":"Bousculer pour embrasser la Pierre noire","body":"Embrasser la Pierre noire est une sunna ; faire du mal à un musulman est interdit. Si la foule est dense, faites simplement un signe vers la Pierre en disant le takbir : votre tawaf est complet et personne n'est blessé."},{"title":"Croire que chaque tour a son invocation obligatoire","body":"Certains livrets donnent une invocation par tour. Rien de tel n'est exigé : il n'y a pas d'invocation fixe et obligatoire pour les tours du tawaf ni pour les trajets du sa'y. Parlez à Allah avec vos propres mots, dans votre langue."},{"title":"Quitter Arafat avant le coucher du soleil","body":"Le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ est resté à Arafat jusqu'au coucher du soleil (Sahih Muslim 1218). Ne vous précipitez pas vers les bus en fin d'après-midi : ce sont les heures les plus précieuses de tout le Hajj."},{"title":"Gravir le Jabal ar-Rahmah en pensant que c'est requis","body":"Toute la plaine d'Arafat est un lieu de station. Gravir la colline ne fait pas partie du Hajj et n'apporte aucune récompense supplémentaire ; la bousculade y est épuisante et dangereuse. Restez là où vous pouvez invoquer en paix."},{"title":"Stationner hors des limites d'Arafat","body":"Certains pèlerins s'installent juste à l'extérieur d'Arafat sans s'en rendre compte. Comme la station est le pilier du Hajj, repérez les panneaux de limite et interrogez votre guide au moindre doute."},{"title":"Ramasser des cailloux trop gros","body":"Les cailloux sont petits, à peu près de la taille d'un pois chiche. Les grosses pierres, les chaussures ou d'autres objets ne font pas partie du rite et peuvent blesser. La lapidation est un acte d'obéissance à Allah, accompli calmement, pas un accès de colère."},{"title":"Photographier au lieu d'adorer","body":"Une photo souvenir prend quelques secondes ; filmer tout le tawaf ou publier en direct depuis Arafat éloigne votre cœur. Rangez le téléphone pendant les rites et protégez la sincérité de votre intention."},{"title":"Négliger le miqat en avion","body":"L'avion franchit le miqat en plein vol. Mettez les étoffes de l'ihram avant d'embarquer ou en début de vol, et formulez l'intention à l'annonce du miqat, ou un peu avant. Si vous l'avez franchi sans ihram, demandez à un savant de confiance pour votre cas."},{"title":"Garder l'épaule droite découverte en permanence","body":"L'idtiba ne concerne que les hommes et seulement pendant le tawaf d'arrivée. Avant et après, y compris pendant la prière et le sa'y, les deux épaules sont couvertes."},{"title":"Compter un aller-retour pour un seul trajet du sa'y","body":"De Safa à Marwa, c'est un trajet ; de Marwa à Safa, un autre. Le sa'y compte sept trajets et s'achève à Marwa. Compter chaque aller-retour pour un seul reviendrait à faire quatorze trajets."},{"title":"Vouloir à tout prix prier juste derrière le Maqam Ibrahim","body":"Prier sur le passage de ceux qui font le tawaf les bloque et vous met en danger. En cas d'affluence, les deux rak'at peuvent être priées n'importe où dans la mosquée."},{"title":"Se disputer et perdre patience","body":"La chaleur, les files d'attente et la fatigue éprouvent tout le monde. Le Coran interdit les propos indécents, la désobéissance et les disputes pendant le Hajj (2:197). Cédez le passage, souriez, pardonnez : le bon comportement fait partie de votre pèlerinage."}],"checklist":[{"id":"passport","group":"documents","label":"Passeport valable encore au moins six mois"},{"id":"visa-permit","group":"documents","label":"Visa Hajj ou Omra et permis officiel"},{"id":"tickets-bookings","group":"documents","label":"Billets d'avion et réservations d'hébergement"},{"id":"id-copies","group":"documents","label":"Copies papier et numériques de tous les documents"},{"id":"emergency-contacts","group":"documents","label":"Carte avec les coordonnées du groupe, de l'hôtel et des urgences"},{"id":"money-card","group":"documents","label":"Un peu d'argent liquide local et une carte bancaire"},{"id":"ihram-garments","group":"clothing","label":"Hommes : deux jeux d'étoffes d'ihram"},{"id":"modest-clothing","group":"clothing","label":"Femmes : vêtements amples, pudiques et confortables"},{"id":"sandals","group":"clothing","label":"Sandales confortables déjà portées"},{"id":"ihram-belt","group":"clothing","label":"Ceinture d'ihram ou pochette pour l'argent et les papiers"},{"id":"small-bag","group":"clothing","label":"Petit sac pour les chaussures et les cailloux"},{"id":"umbrella","group":"clothing","label":"Parapluie clair contre le soleil"},{"id":"vaccinations","group":"health","label":"Vaccins exigés et certificat"},{"id":"medication","group":"health","label":"Médicaments habituels avec ordonnance"},{"id":"unscented-toiletries","group":"health","label":"Savon et produits de toilette sans parfum pour l'ihram"},{"id":"water-bottle","group":"health","label":"Gourde d'eau réutilisable"},{"id":"masks-sanitizer","group":"health","label":"Masques et gel désinfectant sans parfum"},{"id":"first-aid","group":"health","label":"Petite trousse de secours avec pansements pour ampoules"},{"id":"sincere-intention","group":"spiritual","label":"Renouveler son intention : pour Allah seul"},{"id":"repentance-debts","group":"spiritual","label":"Se repentir, régler ses dettes et demander pardon"},{"id":"learn-rites","group":"spiritual","label":"Apprendre les rites et leur ordre"},{"id":"talbiyah-memorised","group":"spiritual","label":"Mémoriser la talbiyah"},{"id":"dua-list","group":"spiritual","label":"Écrire une liste d'invocations personnelles pour Arafat"},{"id":"quran-dhikr","group":"spiritual","label":"Coran de poche ou application pour la lecture et le dhikr"}]}$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (kind, lang) do update set payload = excluded.payload, updated_at = now();
insert into public.premium_content (kind, lang, payload) values ('quiz', 'fr', $tk$[{"id":"q001","cat":"prophets","q":"Selon le Coran, qui est le premier être humain créé par Dieu ?","options":["Nouh (Noé)","Adam","Ibrahim (Abraham)","Idris"],"answer":1,"explain":"Adam (paix sur lui) est le premier être humain et le père de l'humanité. Le Coran raconte sa création en 2:30-37."},{"id":"q002","cat":"prophets","q":"Quel prophète (paix sur lui) a construit l'arche sur l'ordre de Dieu ?","options":["Moussa (Moïse)","Younous (Jonas)","Nouh (Noé)","Houd"],"answer":2,"explain":"Nouh (paix sur lui) a reçu l'ordre de construire l'arche avant le déluge, comme le raconte le Coran (11:37)."},{"id":"q003","cat":"prophets","q":"Quel prophète (paix sur lui) a été avalé par un grand poisson ?","options":["Ayyoub (Job)","Youssouf (Joseph)","Ilyas (Élie)","Younous (Jonas)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Younous (paix sur lui) a été avalé par un grand poisson, a invoqué Dieu du fond des ténèbres et a été sauvé (37:139-144)."},{"id":"q004","cat":"prophets","q":"Quel fils a aidé Ibrahim (paix sur lui) à élever les fondations de la Kaaba ?","options":["Ishaq (Isaac)","Yaqoub (Jacob)","Ismaïl (Ismaël)","Lout (Loth)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Le Coran dit qu'Ibrahim et Ismaïl (paix sur eux) ont élevé ensemble les fondations de la Maison (2:127)."},{"id":"q005","cat":"prophets","q":"Quel prophète (paix sur lui) a été jeté dans un puits par ses frères lorsqu'il était enfant ?","options":["Yahya (Jean)","Youssouf (Joseph)","Haroun (Aaron)","Dawoud (David)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Youssouf (paix sur lui) a été jeté dans un puits par ses frères jaloux. Son histoire occupe la sourate Youssouf, la 12e sourate (12:15)."},{"id":"q006","cat":"prophets","q":"À quel prophète (paix sur lui) la Tawrat (Torah) a-t-elle été révélée ?","options":["Issa (Jésus)","Dawoud (David)","Ibrahim (Abraham)","Moussa (Moïse)"],"answer":3,"explain":"La Tawrat a été donnée à Moussa (paix sur lui), qui a fait sortir d'Égypte les Enfants d'Israël."},{"id":"q007","cat":"prophets","q":"Quel prophète (paix sur lui) a reçu le Zabour (les Psaumes) ?","options":["Dawoud (David)","Soulayman (Salomon)","Moussa (Moïse)","Zakariya (Zacharie)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Le Coran affirme que Dieu a donné le Zabour à Dawoud (paix sur lui) (4:163 et 17:55)."},{"id":"q008","cat":"prophets","q":"Quel prophète (paix sur lui) a reçu l'Injil (l'Évangile) ?","options":["Issa (Jésus)","Yahya (Jean)","Moussa (Moïse)","Younous (Jonas)"],"answer":0,"explain":"L'Injil a été donné à Issa, fils de Maryam (paix sur eux), comme le dit le Coran en 5:46."},{"id":"q009","cat":"prophets","q":"Quel prophète était le frère de Moussa (paix sur lui) et l'a aidé dans sa mission ?","options":["Youcha (Josué)","Chouayb","Ilyas (Élie)","Haroun (Aaron)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Moussa a demandé à Dieu de lui donner son frère Haroun comme soutien (20:29-32). Paix sur eux deux."},{"id":"q010","cat":"prophets","q":"Quel prophète (paix sur lui), éprouvé par une longue épreuve, est célèbre pour sa patience et pour avoir invoqué son Seigneur, « le plus Miséricordieux des miséricordieux » ?","options":["Ayyoub (Job)","Haroun (Aaron)","Lout (Loth)","Salih"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ayyoub (paix sur lui) est resté patient dans l'épreuve et a invoqué son Seigneur, qui l'a soulagé (21:83-84)."},{"id":"q011","cat":"prophets","q":"Quel prophète (paix sur lui) a été envoyé au peuple de 'Ad ?","options":["Salih","Chouayb","Houd","Lout (Loth)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Houd (paix sur lui) a été envoyé au peuple de 'Ad pour l'appeler à n'adorer que Dieu (7:65)."},{"id":"q012","cat":"prophets","q":"La chamelle donnée comme signe au peuple de Thamoud est liée à quel prophète (paix sur lui) ?","options":["Houd","Salih","Nouh (Noé)","Younous (Jonas)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Salih (paix sur lui) a été envoyé aux Thamoud, et la chamelle était pour eux un signe de Dieu (7:73)."},{"id":"q013","cat":"prophets","q":"Quel prophète (paix sur lui) a été jeté dans un feu par son peuple, feu que Dieu a rendu frais et sans danger pour lui ?","options":["Moussa (Moïse)","Ibrahim (Abraham)","Yahya (Jean)","Houd"],"answer":1,"explain":"Quand son peuple a jeté Ibrahim (paix sur lui) dans le feu, Dieu a ordonné au feu d'être pour lui fraîcheur et salut (21:68-69)."},{"id":"q014","cat":"prophets","q":"Selon le Coran, quel prophète (paix sur lui) a compris les paroles d'une fourmi qui avertissait les autres fourmis ?","options":["Younous (Jonas)","Issa (Jésus)","Soulayman (Salomon)","Youssouf (Joseph)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Soulayman (paix sur lui) a entendu une fourmi avertir les autres à l'approche de son armée ; il a souri et remercié Dieu (27:18-19)."},{"id":"q015","cat":"prophets","q":"Quel prophète (paix sur lui) était le père de Yahya (Jean) ?","options":["Imran","Yaqoub (Jacob)","Ayyoub (Job)","Zakariya (Zacharie)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Zakariya (paix sur lui) a demandé un enfant dans sa vieillesse et a reçu la bonne nouvelle de la naissance de Yahya (19:7)."},{"id":"q016","cat":"seerah","q":"Dans quelle ville le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ est-il né ?","options":["Médine","La Mecque","Taïf","Jérusalem"],"answer":1,"explain":"Le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ est né à La Mecque, dans la tribu de Quraych."},{"id":"q017","cat":"seerah","q":"Où le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ a-t-il reçu la première révélation ?","options":["Dans la grotte de Thawr","Dans la grotte de Hira","Sur le mont Ouhoud","Dans la plaine d'Arafat"],"answer":1,"explain":"La première révélation est venue dans la grotte de Hira, sur la Montagne de la Lumière, près de La Mecque, où il avait l'habitude de se retirer."},{"id":"q018","cat":"seerah","q":"Quel ange a apporté la révélation au Prophète Muhammad ﷺ ?","options":["Mikaïl (Michel)","Jibril (Gabriel)","Israfil","Malik"],"answer":1,"explain":"L'ange Jibril (paix sur lui) a apporté le Coran au Prophète Muhammad ﷺ."},{"id":"q019","cat":"seerah","q":"Quel est le premier mot du Coran révélé au Prophète Muhammad ﷺ ?","options":["Qoul (Dis)","Qoum (Lève-toi)","Iqra (Lis)","Sabbih (Glorifie)"],"answer":2,"explain":"La première révélation commence par le mot « Iqra », qui signifie lis ou récite (96:1)."},{"id":"q020","cat":"seerah","q":"Qui était la première épouse du Prophète Muhammad ﷺ ?","options":["Khadija bint Khouwaylid","Aïcha bint Abi Bakr","Hafsa bint Omar","Sawda bint Zam'a"],"answer":0,"explain":"Khadija, commerçante respectée de La Mecque, a été sa première épouse. Il n'a épousé aucune autre femme tant qu'elle était en vie."},{"id":"q021","cat":"seerah","q":"En quelle année de l'ère commune l'Hégire, l'émigration de La Mecque à Médine, a-t-elle eu lieu ?","options":["622","570","610","632"],"answer":0,"explain":"L'Hégire a eu lieu en 622. Cette année est devenue plus tard l'an 1 du calendrier musulman."},{"id":"q022","cat":"seerah","q":"Comment s'appelait la ville de Médine avant l'Hégire ?","options":["Khaybar","Tabouk","Taïf","Yathrib"],"answer":3,"explain":"La ville s'appelait Yathrib. Après l'installation du Prophète Muhammad ﷺ, elle a été connue sous le nom de Médine, « la Ville »."},{"id":"q023","cat":"seerah","q":"Dans quelle grotte le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ s'est-il abrité pendant l'Hégire ?","options":["La grotte de Thawr","La grotte de Hira","Une grotte du mont Ouhoud","Une grotte du mont Safa"],"answer":0,"explain":"Il s'est caché dans la grotte de Thawr avec son compagnon Abou Bakr pendant que les Quraych les cherchaient. Le Coran évoque ce moment en 9:40."},{"id":"q024","cat":"seerah","q":"En quelle année de l'Hégire la bataille de Badr a-t-elle eu lieu ?","options":["L'an 2","L'an 1","L'an 5","L'an 8"],"answer":0,"explain":"La bataille de Badr a eu lieu pendant le Ramadan de l'an 2 de l'Hégire. C'est la première grande bataille des musulmans."},{"id":"q025","cat":"seerah","q":"Comment s'appelait le grand-père qui a pris soin du Prophète Muhammad ﷺ après la mort de sa mère ?","options":["Abou Lahab","Abou Soufyan","Abd al-Mouttalib","Waraqa ibn Nawfal"],"answer":2,"explain":"Son grand-père Abd al-Mouttalib, l'un des chefs de La Mecque, s'est occupé de lui. Après la mort de ce grand-père, son oncle Abou Talib l'a recueilli."},{"id":"q026","cat":"seerah","q":"Comment s'appelait la mère du Prophète Muhammad ﷺ ?","options":["Halima as-Sa'diyya","Fatima bint Asad","Oumm Ayman","Amina bint Wahb"],"answer":3,"explain":"Sa mère s'appelait Amina bint Wahb. Elle est morte alors qu'il était encore un jeune enfant. Halima as-Sa'diyya était sa nourrice."},{"id":"q027","cat":"seerah","q":"À quelle tribu appartenait le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ ?","options":["Aws","Khazraj","Thaqif","Quraych"],"answer":3,"explain":"Il appartenait à Quraych, la tribu de La Mecque, dans le clan des Banou Hachim."},{"id":"q028","cat":"seerah","q":"Comment s'appelle la trêve conclue entre les musulmans et les Quraych en l'an 6 de l'Hégire ?","options":["Le serment d'Aqaba","La charte de Médine","Le traité de Houdaybiya","Le pacte des Vertueux (Hilf al-Foudoul)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Le traité de Houdaybiya a été conclu en l'an 6 de l'Hégire, près de La Mecque. Il a ouvert une période de paix pendant laquelle l'islam s'est largement répandu."},{"id":"q029","cat":"seerah","q":"En quelle année de l'Hégire la conquête de La Mecque a-t-elle eu lieu ?","options":["L'an 2","L'an 4","L'an 8","L'an 6"],"answer":2,"explain":"Les musulmans sont entrés à La Mecque en l'an 8 de l'Hégire (630). Le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ a pardonné à ses habitants."},{"id":"q030","cat":"seerah","q":"Avant la prophétie, quel surnom les habitants de La Mecque donnaient-ils au Prophète Muhammad ﷺ ?","options":["Al-Hakim (le Sage)","Al-Karim (le Généreux)","Ach-Chouja (le Courageux)","Al-Amin (le Digne de confiance)"],"answer":3,"explain":"On l'appelait al-Amin, le Digne de confiance, en raison de son honnêteté. Les gens lui confiaient leurs objets de valeur."},{"id":"q031","cat":"companions","q":"Qui a été le premier muezzin de l'islam ?","options":["Salman al-Farisi","Anas ibn Malik","Bilal ibn Rabah","Zayd ibn Haritha"],"answer":2,"explain":"Bilal ibn Rabah, esclave affranchi d'origine abyssine, a été choisi par le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ pour lancer l'appel à la prière."},{"id":"q032","cat":"companions","q":"Quel compagnon a voyagé avec le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ pendant l'Hégire ?","options":["Bilal ibn Rabah","Mous'ab ibn Oumayr","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Abou Bakr"],"answer":3,"explain":"Abou Bakr a été son compagnon de route de La Mecque à Médine et il est resté avec lui dans la grotte de Thawr."},{"id":"q033","cat":"companions","q":"Quel compagnon a proposé de creuser un fossé pour défendre Médine ?","options":["Bilal ibn Rabah","Abou Ayyoub al-Ansari","Salman al-Farisi","Mous'ab ibn Oumayr"],"answer":2,"explain":"Salman al-Farisi, originaire de Perse, a proposé de creuser un fossé. C'est pourquoi la bataille de l'an 5 de l'Hégire s'appelle la bataille du Fossé."},{"id":"q034","cat":"companions","q":"Quel compagnon est connu sous le titre d'« Épée de Dieu » (Sayf Allah) ?","options":["Khalid ibn al-Walid","Bilal ibn Rabah","Zayd ibn Thabit","Anas ibn Malik"],"answer":0,"explain":"Khalid ibn al-Walid, célèbre chef militaire, est connu sous le nom de Sayf Allah, l'Épée de Dieu."},{"id":"q035","cat":"companions","q":"Qui a été la première personne à croire au message du Prophète Muhammad ﷺ ?","options":["Omar ibn al-Khattab","Khadija bint Khouwaylid","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Abou Soufyan"],"answer":1,"explain":"Son épouse Khadija a été la première à croire en lui. Elle l'a réconforté et soutenu dès la toute première révélation."},{"id":"q036","cat":"companions","q":"Sous quel calife le calendrier hégirien a-t-il été adopté, avec l'année de l'Hégire comme point de départ ?","options":["Omar ibn al-Khattab","Haroun ar-Rachid","Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan","Omar ibn Abd al-Aziz"],"answer":0,"explain":"Le calendrier hégirien a été mis en place pendant le califat d'Omar ibn al-Khattab, quelques années après la mort du Prophète Muhammad ﷺ."},{"id":"q037","cat":"companions","q":"Quel compagnon, scribe de la révélation, a été chargé de rassembler le Coran en un seul recueil écrit ?","options":["Bilal ibn Rabah","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Salman al-Farisi","Zayd ibn Thabit"],"answer":3,"explain":"Zayd ibn Thabit, l'un des scribes du Prophète Muhammad ﷺ, a rassemblé le Coran écrit en un seul recueil pendant le califat d'Abou Bakr."},{"id":"q038","cat":"companions","q":"Quel compagnon a été envoyé à Yathrib avant l'Hégire pour enseigner l'islam à ses habitants ?","options":["Mous'ab ibn Oumayr","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Anas ibn Malik","Abou Ayyoub al-Ansari"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mous'ab ibn Oumayr a été envoyé à Yathrib après le premier serment d'Aqaba. Beaucoup de ses habitants ont embrassé l'islam grâce à lui."},{"id":"q039","cat":"companions","q":"Lors de quelle bataille Hamza, l'oncle du Prophète Muhammad ﷺ, a-t-il été tué ?","options":["Badr","Le Fossé","Hounayn","Ouhoud"],"answer":3,"explain":"Hamza ibn Abd al-Mouttalib est tombé en martyr à la bataille d'Ouhoud, en l'an 3 de l'Hégire."},{"id":"q040","cat":"companions","q":"Qui est considérée comme la première martyre de l'islam ?","options":["Oumm Ayman","Soumayya bint Khayyat","Halima as-Sa'diyya","Oumm Salama"],"answer":1,"explain":"Soumayya bint Khayyat, la mère de Ammar ibn Yasir, a été tuée à La Mecque parce qu'elle refusait de renoncer à sa foi."},{"id":"q041","cat":"companions","q":"Quel compagnon a pris la parole au nom des émigrés musulmans devant le Négus, le roi d'Abyssinie ?","options":["Bilal ibn Rabah","Ja'far ibn Abi Talib","Zayd ibn Thabit","Anas ibn Malik"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ja'far ibn Abi Talib a présenté l'islam au Négus, qui a ensuite laissé les musulmans vivre en sécurité sur sa terre."},{"id":"q042","cat":"companions","q":"Quelle fille du Prophète Muhammad ﷺ a épousé Ali ibn Abi Talib ?","options":["Fatima","Zaynab","Rouqayya","Oumm Koulthoum"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fatima a épousé Ali ibn Abi Talib à Médine. Ils sont les parents d'al-Hasan et d'al-Housayn."},{"id":"q043","cat":"companions","q":"Quel nom donne-t-on aux habitants de Médine qui ont accueilli et aidé les émigrés de La Mecque ?","options":["Les Mouhajiroun (les Émigrés)","Les Quraych","Les Tabi'oun (les Successeurs)","Les Ansar (les Auxiliaires)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Les musulmans de Médine sont appelés les Ansar, les Auxiliaires. Ceux qui ont émigré de La Mecque sont appelés les Mouhajiroun."},{"id":"q044","cat":"companions","q":"Qui a dormi dans le lit du Prophète Muhammad ﷺ la nuit où il a quitté La Mecque pour l'Hégire ?","options":["Zayd ibn Haritha","Bilal ibn Rabah","Ali ibn Abi Talib","Anas ibn Malik"],"answer":2,"explain":"Le jeune Ali ibn Abi Talib a pris sa place dans le lit cette nuit-là, puis il est resté pour rendre les biens que les gens lui avaient confiés."},{"id":"q045","cat":"companions","q":"Chez qui le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ a-t-il habité à Médine pendant la construction de sa mosquée ?","options":["Salman al-Farisi","Abou Ayyoub al-Ansari","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Mous'ab ibn Oumayr"],"answer":1,"explain":"Il a habité chez Abou Ayyoub al-Ansari jusqu'à ce que sa mosquée et ses chambres soient construites."},{"id":"q046","cat":"quran","q":"Combien de sourates le Coran contient-il ?","options":["114","99","110","120"],"answer":0,"explain":"Le Coran compte 114 sourates, d'al-Fatiha à an-Nas."},{"id":"q047","cat":"quran","q":"Quelle est la plus longue sourate du Coran ?","options":["Al-Imran","An-Nisa","Ya-Sin","Al-Baqara"],"answer":3,"explain":"Al-Baqara, la 2e sourate, est la plus longue du Coran."},{"id":"q048","cat":"quran","q":"Quelle est la plus courte sourate du Coran ?","options":["Al-Ikhlas","Al-Kawthar","Al-Falaq","Al-Fatiha"],"answer":1,"explain":"Al-Kawthar, la 108e sourate, est la plus courte : elle ne compte que trois courts versets."},{"id":"q049","cat":"quran","q":"Quelle sourate ouvre le Coran ?","options":["Al-Baqara","Al-Ikhlas","Al-Fatiha","Al-Alaq"],"answer":2,"explain":"Al-Fatiha, « l'Ouverture », est la première sourate du Coran ; elle est récitée dans chaque prière."},{"id":"q050","cat":"quran","q":"Quelle sourate est placée en dernier dans le Coran ?","options":["Al-Falaq","Al-Ikhlas","An-Nas","An-Nasr"],"answer":2,"explain":"An-Nas, la 114e sourate, clôt le Coran."},{"id":"q051","cat":"quran","q":"En combien de parties (juz') le Coran est-il divisé ?","options":["7","30","60","114"],"answer":1,"explain":"Le Coran est divisé en 30 juz', ce qui permet de le lire facilement en entier en un mois."},{"id":"q052","cat":"quran","q":"Durant quel mois la révélation du Coran a-t-elle commencé ?","options":["Ramadan","Rajab","Mouharram","Dhoul-Hijja"],"answer":0,"explain":"Le Coran dit qu'il a été descendu pendant le mois de Ramadan (2:185)."},{"id":"q053","cat":"quran","q":"Dans quelle sourate se trouve Ayat al-Koursi, le verset du Trône ?","options":["Al-Imran","Al-Baqara","Al-Kahf","Al-Moulk"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ayat al-Koursi est le verset 255 de la sourate al-Baqara (2:255)."},{"id":"q054","cat":"quran","q":"Quelle est la seule sourate qui ne commence pas par la basmala ?","options":["At-Tawba","Al-Anfal","Younous","Al-Moulk"],"answer":0,"explain":"At-Tawba, la 9e sourate, est la seule qui ne s'ouvre pas par « Au nom de Dieu, le Tout Miséricordieux, le Très Miséricordieux »."},{"id":"q055","cat":"quran","q":"Dans quelle sourate la basmala apparaît-elle deux fois ?","options":["An-Nahl","Al-Ankabout","Saba","An-Naml"],"answer":3,"explain":"Dans la sourate an-Naml, la basmala figure en ouverture, puis dans la lettre de Soulayman, paix sur lui (27:30)."},{"id":"q056","cat":"quran","q":"Quelle sourate du Coran porte le nom de la mère de Issa (paix sur lui) ?","options":["An-Nisa","Maryam","Al-Imran","An-Nour"],"answer":1,"explain":"La 19e sourate porte le nom de Maryam, la mère de Issa (paix sur eux). Elle est la seule femme citée par son nom dans le Coran."},{"id":"q057","cat":"quran","q":"Sur combien d'années environ le Coran a-t-il été révélé ?","options":["Environ 23 ans","Environ 3 ans","Environ 10 ans","Environ 40 ans"],"answer":0,"explain":"Le Coran a été révélé peu à peu pendant environ 23 ans, d'abord à La Mecque puis à Médine."},{"id":"q058","cat":"quran","q":"Combien de versets la sourate al-Fatiha compte-t-elle ?","options":["3","5","10","7"],"answer":3,"explain":"Al-Fatiha compte sept versets. Elle est récitée dans chaque unité de la prière."},{"id":"q059","cat":"quran","q":"Comment appelle-t-on les sourates révélées avant l'Hégire ?","options":["Médinoises (madani)","Mouhkam","Mecquoises (makki)","Moufassal"],"answer":2,"explain":"Les sourates révélées avant l'Hégire sont dites mecquoises, et celles révélées après sont dites médinoises."},{"id":"q060","cat":"quran","q":"Quel est le plus long verset du Coran ?","options":["Ayat al-Koursi (2:255)","Le verset de la lumière (24:35)","Le verset de la dette (2:282)","Le premier verset d'al-Fatiha"],"answer":2,"explain":"Le plus long verset est le verset 282 d'al-Baqara. Il apprend à mettre ses dettes par écrit et à prendre des témoins."},{"id":"q061","cat":"history","q":"Quelle ville était la capitale du califat omeyyade ?","options":["Bagdad","Damas","Le Caire","Médine"],"answer":1,"explain":"Les Omeyyades ont gouverné depuis Damas, en Syrie, de 661 à 750."},{"id":"q062","cat":"history","q":"Quel calife abbasside a fondé la ville de Bagdad en 762 ?","options":["Haroun ar-Rachid","Al-Mansour","Al-Ma'moun","Al-Mou'tasim"],"answer":1,"explain":"Le calife al-Mansour a fondé Bagdad en 762 sur les rives du Tigre. Elle est devenue l'une des plus grandes villes du monde."},{"id":"q063","cat":"history","q":"Qui a pris et détruit Bagdad en 1258 ?","options":["Les Mongols","Les Croisés","Les Seldjoukides","Les Ottomans"],"answer":0,"explain":"L'armée mongole conduite par Houlagou a pris Bagdad en 1258, ce qui a mis fin au califat abbasside de Bagdad."},{"id":"q064","cat":"history","q":"Quel sultan ottoman a pris Constantinople en 1453 ?","options":["Mehmed II","Soliman le Magnifique","Sélim Ier","Osman Ier"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mehmed II, surnommé « le Conquérant », a pris Constantinople en 1453. La ville est devenue la capitale ottomane, Istanbul."},{"id":"q065","cat":"history","q":"Qui commandait l'armée musulmane qui est passée d'Afrique du Nord à la péninsule Ibérique en 711 ?","options":["Oqba ibn Nafi","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Salah ad-Din (Saladin)","Tariq ibn Ziyad"],"answer":3,"explain":"Tariq ibn Ziyad a traversé le détroit en 711. Le rocher de Gibraltar porte son nom : Jabal Tariq, « la montagne de Tariq »."},{"id":"q066","cat":"history","q":"Qui a repris Jérusalem aux Croisés en 1187 ?","options":["Baybars","Nour ad-Din Zangi","Salah ad-Din (Saladin)","Mehmed II"],"answer":2,"explain":"Salah ad-Din al-Ayyoubi a repris Jérusalem en 1187, après sa victoire à la bataille de Hattin."},{"id":"q067","cat":"history","q":"Quelle ville, dernier royaume musulman d'al-Andalus, est tombée en 1492 ?","options":["Cordoue","Grenade","Séville","Tolède"],"answer":1,"explain":"Le royaume nasride de Grenade est tombé en 1492, ce qui a mis fin à près de huit siècles de présence musulmane dans une partie de la péninsule Ibérique."},{"id":"q068","cat":"history","q":"Qui a vaincu les Mongols à la bataille d'Aïn Jalout en 1260 ?","options":["Les Mamelouks d'Égypte","Les Ottomans","Les Seldjoukides","Les Almohades"],"answer":0,"explain":"Les Mamelouks d'Égypte ont arrêté l'avancée des Mongols à Aïn Jalout, en Palestine, en 1260."},{"id":"q069","cat":"history","q":"Quelle dynastie a pris la tête du califat en 750, puis a fait de Bagdad sa capitale ?","options":["Les Ottomans","Les Mamelouks","Les Almoravides","Les Abbassides"],"answer":3,"explain":"Les Abbassides ont remplacé les Omeyyades en 750 et ont fondé Bagdad en 762."},{"id":"q070","cat":"history","q":"Quel prince omeyyade s'est réfugié en al-Andalus et a fondé l'émirat de Cordoue en 756 ?","options":["Tariq ibn Ziyad","Haroun ar-Rachid","Salah ad-Din (Saladin)","Abd al-Rahman Ier"],"answer":3,"explain":"Abd al-Rahman Ier, survivant de la famille omeyyade, a gagné al-Andalus et a fondé l'émirat de Cordoue en 756."},{"id":"q071","cat":"history","q":"La dynastie ottomane tire son nom de son fondateur. Qui était-il ?","options":["Mehmed II","Timour (Tamerlan)","Alp Arslan","Osman Ier"],"answer":3,"explain":"Osman Ier a fondé l'État ottoman en Anatolie vers 1300. Le mot « ottoman » vient de son nom."},{"id":"q072","cat":"history","q":"Qui a fondé l'Empire moghol en Inde en 1526 ?","options":["Akbar","Shah Jahan","Babour","Aurangzeb"],"answer":2,"explain":"Babour a fondé l'Empire moghol après sa victoire à la bataille de Panipat en 1526."},{"id":"q073","cat":"history","q":"Quel sultan ottoman du XVIe siècle est surnommé « le Magnifique » et « le Législateur » ?","options":["Mehmed II","Soliman Ier","Osman Ier","Abdülhamid II"],"answer":1,"explain":"Soliman Ier a régné de 1520 à 1566, à l'apogée de l'Empire ottoman. On l'appelle « Kanouni », le Législateur."},{"id":"q074","cat":"history","q":"Mansa Moussa, célèbre pour son pèlerinage à La Mecque en 1324, régnait sur quel empire ?","options":["L'empire songhaï","L'empire du Ghana","L'empire du Mali","L'empire du Kanem"],"answer":2,"explain":"Mansa Moussa régnait sur l'empire du Mali, en Afrique de l'Ouest. Son pèlerinage de 1324 a fait connaître au loin la richesse du Mali."},{"id":"q075","cat":"history","q":"Quel sultan seldjoukide a remporté la bataille de Manzikert contre les Byzantins en 1071 ?","options":["Salah ad-Din (Saladin)","Baybars","Alp Arslan","Osman Ier"],"answer":2,"explain":"Alp Arslan a battu l'armée byzantine à Manzikert en 1071, ce qui a ouvert l'Anatolie aux Turcs."},{"id":"q076","cat":"sciences","q":"Quelle branche des mathématiques tire son nom du livre « al-Jabr » d'al-Khwarizmi ?","options":["La géométrie","L'algèbre","La trigonométrie","L'arithmétique"],"answer":1,"explain":"Le mot algèbre vient de « al-jabr », dans le titre du livre qu'al-Khwarizmi a écrit à Bagdad au IXe siècle."},{"id":"q077","cat":"sciences","q":"Le mot « algorithme » vient de la forme latine du nom de quel savant ?","options":["Al-Khwarizmi","Al-Kindi","Al-Birouni","Al-Battani"],"answer":0,"explain":"« Algorithme » vient de « Algoritmi », la forme latine du nom du mathématicien al-Khwarizmi."},{"id":"q078","cat":"sciences","q":"Qui a écrit le « Canon de la médecine » (al-Qanoun fi al-Tibb) ?","options":["Al-Razi (Rhazès)","Al-Zahrawi","Ibn Sina (Avicenne)","Ibn al-Nafis"],"answer":2,"explain":"Ibn Sina a écrit le Canon de la médecine au XIe siècle. Il a servi à enseigner la médecine pendant des siècles, dans le monde musulman comme en Europe."},{"id":"q079","cat":"sciences","q":"Quel savant a écrit le « Livre de l'optique » et expliqué que nous voyons parce que la lumière entre dans l'œil ?","options":["Al-Khwarizmi","Ibn al-Haytham","Ibn Khaldoun","Al-Idrisi"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ibn al-Haytham, qui a vécu aux Xe et XIe siècles, a écrit le Livre de l'optique (Kitab al-Manazir) en s'appuyant sur des expériences rigoureuses."},{"id":"q080","cat":"sciences","q":"Le savant andalou al-Zahrawi est célèbre comme pionnier dans quel domaine ?","options":["L'astronomie","La chirurgie","La géographie","L'algèbre"],"answer":1,"explain":"Al-Zahrawi, qui vivait près de Cordoue au Xe siècle, a décrit des opérations chirurgicales et de nombreux instruments dans son encyclopédie al-Tasrif."},{"id":"q081","cat":"sciences","q":"Quel médecin a écrit un célèbre traité qui distingue la variole de la rougeole ?","options":["Al-Kindi","Al-Farabi","Al-Idrisi","Al-Razi (Rhazès)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Al-Razi, grand médecin des IXe et Xe siècles, a écrit un traité sur la variole et la rougeole qui décrit avec soin les deux maladies."},{"id":"q082","cat":"sciences","q":"Quel médecin du XIIIe siècle a décrit le passage du sang du cœur vers les poumons ?","options":["Al-Khwarizmi","Al-Birouni","Ibn al-Nafis","Ibn Khaldoun"],"answer":2,"explain":"Ibn al-Nafis a décrit la circulation pulmonaire du sang au XIIIe siècle, bien avant qu'elle ne soit connue en Europe."},{"id":"q083","cat":"sciences","q":"Quel savant du XIe siècle a calculé le rayon de la Terre et écrit une grande étude sur l'Inde ?","options":["Ibn Rochd (Averroès)","Jabir ibn Hayyan","Al-Zahrawi","Al-Birouni"],"answer":3,"explain":"Al-Birouni a calculé le rayon de la Terre par une méthode ingénieuse, avec un résultat proche de la valeur moderne, et a écrit un livre détaillé sur l'Inde."},{"id":"q084","cat":"sciences","q":"Quel géographe a dessiné au XIIe siècle une célèbre carte du monde pour le roi Roger II de Sicile ?","options":["Ibn Battouta","Ibn Khaldoun","Al-Idrisi","Al-Khwarizmi"],"answer":2,"explain":"Al-Idrisi a achevé sa carte du monde et le livre qui l'accompagne en 1154, à la cour de Roger II, en Sicile."},{"id":"q085","cat":"sciences","q":"Jabir ibn Hayyan est surtout connu pour ses travaux dans quel domaine ?","options":["L'optique","La chirurgie","La chimie","La géographie"],"answer":2,"explain":"Jabir ibn Hayyan est l'un des grands noms des débuts de la chimie (alchimie) ; de nombreux ouvrages lui sont attribués."},{"id":"q086","cat":"sciences","q":"Dans quelle ville se trouvait la Maison de la Sagesse (Bayt al-Hikma), la célèbre bibliothèque abbasside liée au mouvement de traduction ?","options":["Bagdad","Cordoue","Le Caire","Damas"],"answer":0,"explain":"La Maison de la Sagesse se trouvait dans la Bagdad abbasside, où des savants traduisaient et étudiaient des ouvrages des traditions grecque, perse et indienne."},{"id":"q087","cat":"sciences","q":"Qui a écrit la « Mouqaddima », célèbre ouvrage sur l'histoire et la société ?","options":["Ibn Khaldoun","Ibn Battouta","Ibn Rochd (Averroès)","Al-Ghazali"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ibn Khaldoun, né à Tunis en 1332, a écrit la Mouqaddima. Il est considéré comme un précurseur de la sociologie et de l'étude moderne de l'histoire."},{"id":"q088","cat":"sciences","q":"À quoi servait l'astrolabe, instrument perfectionné par les savants musulmans ?","options":["À mesurer la température","À peser l'or et l'argent","À moudre le grain","À observer la position du soleil et des étoiles"],"answer":3,"explain":"L'astrolabe servait à observer le soleil et les étoiles, ce qui aidait à connaître l'heure et à s'orienter."},{"id":"q089","cat":"sciences","q":"Pour quoi l'ingénieur al-Jazari, qui a terminé son célèbre livre en 1206, est-il connu ?","options":["Des machines ingénieuses et des horloges à eau","Une carte du monde","Un traité sur la variole","Un livre d'algèbre"],"answer":0,"explain":"Al-Jazari a écrit le Livre de la connaissance des procédés mécaniques ingénieux, qui décrit des horloges à eau, des pompes et des automates."},{"id":"q090","cat":"sciences","q":"Sous quel nom latin le savant andalou Ibn Rochd est-il connu en Europe ?","options":["Avicenne","Alhazen","Rhazès","Averroès"],"answer":3,"explain":"Ibn Rochd de Cordoue est connu sous le nom d'Averroès. Ses commentaires d'Aristote ont été très lus dans l'Europe médiévale."},{"id":"q091","cat":"pillars","q":"Combien de piliers l'islam compte-t-il ?","options":["Trois","Cinq","Quatre","Sept"],"answer":1,"explain":"L'islam compte cinq piliers : l'attestation de foi, la prière, la zakat, le jeûne du Ramadan et le pèlerinage à La Mecque."},{"id":"q092","cat":"pillars","q":"Quel est le premier pilier de l'islam ?","options":["La prière (salat)","Le jeûne (sawm)","Le pèlerinage (hajj)","L'attestation de foi (chahada)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Le premier pilier est la chahada : attester qu'il n'y a de dieu que Dieu et que Muhammad ﷺ est Son Messager."},{"id":"q093","cat":"pillars","q":"Combien y a-t-il de prières obligatoires par jour ?","options":["Deux","Trois","Cinq","Sept"],"answer":2,"explain":"Il y a cinq prières obligatoires par jour : Fajr, Dhouhr, Asr, Maghrib et Icha."},{"id":"q094","cat":"pillars","q":"Comment s'appelle la prière de l'aube ?","options":["Dhouhr","Fajr","Asr","Icha"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fajr est la prière de l'aube, la première des cinq prières quotidiennes."},{"id":"q095","cat":"pillars","q":"Combien d'unités obligatoires (rak'at) la prière du Fajr compte-t-elle ?","options":["Une","Deux","Trois","Quatre"],"answer":1,"explain":"La prière obligatoire du Fajr compte deux rak'at."},{"id":"q096","cat":"pillars","q":"Quel mois du calendrier musulman est le Ramadan ?","options":["Le neuvième","Le premier","Le septième","Le douzième"],"answer":0,"explain":"Le Ramadan est le neuvième mois du calendrier hégirien. C'est le mois du jeûne."},{"id":"q097","cat":"pillars","q":"Pendant le Ramadan, à quel moment le jeûne de chaque journée commence-t-il ?","options":["À l'aube","Au lever du soleil","À midi","À minuit"],"answer":0,"explain":"Le jeûne commence à l'aube, quand apparaît la première lueur, comme l'explique le Coran en 2:187."},{"id":"q098","cat":"pillars","q":"Comment s'appelle le repas qui rompt le jeûne, le soir ?","options":["Le souhour","La walima","La aqiqa","L'iftar"],"answer":3,"explain":"L'iftar est le repas de rupture du jeûne. Le souhour est le repas pris avant l'aube."},{"id":"q099","cat":"pillars","q":"Quel est le taux habituel de la zakat sur l'épargne, comme l'argent, l'or et l'argent métal ?","options":["2,5 %","1 %","5 %","15 %"],"answer":0,"explain":"La zakat sur l'épargne est de 2,5 %, soit un quarantième, lorsque le montant minimum a été détenu pendant une année lunaire complète."},{"id":"q100","cat":"pillars","q":"Durant quel mois le Hajj a-t-il lieu ?","options":["Ramadan","Dhoul-Hijja","Mouharram","Chawwal"],"answer":1,"explain":"Le Hajj a lieu en Dhoul-Hijja, le douzième et dernier mois du calendrier hégirien."},{"id":"q101","cat":"pillars","q":"Combien de fois les pèlerins tournent-ils autour de la Kaaba lors d'un tawaf ?","options":["Trois","Cinq","Sept","Neuf"],"answer":2,"explain":"Un tawaf se compose de sept tours autour de la Kaaba."},{"id":"q102","cat":"pillars","q":"Entre quelles deux collines les pèlerins marchent-ils pendant le sa'i ?","options":["Ouhoud et Thawr","Arafat et Mina","Safa et Marwa","Hira et Thawr"],"answer":2,"explain":"Les pèlerins font sept trajets entre Safa et Marwa, que le Coran compte parmi les symboles de Dieu (2:158)."},{"id":"q103","cat":"pillars","q":"Quel jour de Dhoul-Hijja les pèlerins se tiennent-ils à Arafat ?","options":["Le 9","Le 1er","Le 10","Le 13"],"answer":0,"explain":"La station à Arafat a lieu le 9 Dhoul-Hijja. C'est le cœur du Hajj."},{"id":"q104","cat":"pillars","q":"Combien de fois dans sa vie le Hajj est-il obligatoire pour le musulman qui en a la capacité ?","options":["Trois fois","Chaque année","Tous les cinq ans","Une fois"],"answer":3,"explain":"Le Hajj est obligatoire une fois dans la vie pour ceux qui ont la capacité de faire le voyage (3:97)."},{"id":"q105","cat":"pillars","q":"Vers quelle ville les premiers musulmans priaient-ils avant que la qibla ne devienne la Kaaba ?","options":["Médine","Damas","Taïf","Jérusalem"],"answer":3,"explain":"Les premiers musulmans priaient en direction de Jérusalem. La qibla a ensuite été tournée vers la Mosquée sacrée de La Mecque (2:144)."},{"id":"q106","cat":"civilisation","q":"Qui a fondé à Fès, au IXe siècle, la mosquée al-Qarawiyyin, devenue une université célèbre ?","options":["Rabia al-Adawiyya","Fatima al-Fihri","Zoubayda bint Ja'far","Chajar ad-Dourr"],"answer":1,"explain":"Selon la tradition, Fatima al-Fihri a fondé al-Qarawiyyin à Fès, au Maroc, en 859. Elle est souvent présentée comme la plus ancienne université encore en activité."},{"id":"q107","cat":"civilisation","q":"Dans quelle ville d'al-Andalus se trouve la grande mosquée célèbre pour sa forêt de colonnes et ses arcs rouges et blancs ?","options":["Séville","Cordoue","Grenade","Tolède"],"answer":1,"explain":"La Grande Mosquée de Cordoue a été commencée au VIIIe siècle, quand Cordoue était la capitale d'al-Andalus."},{"id":"q108","cat":"civilisation","q":"Dans quelle ville se trouve le palais de l'Alhambra ?","options":["Grenade","Cordoue","Marrakech","Fès"],"answer":0,"explain":"L'Alhambra se dresse à Grenade, dans le sud de l'Espagne. C'était le palais des souverains nasrides."},{"id":"q109","cat":"civilisation","q":"Quel empereur moghol a fait construire le Taj Mahal ?","options":["Akbar","Babour","Aurangzeb","Shah Jahan"],"answer":3,"explain":"Shah Jahan a fait construire le Taj Mahal à Agra au XVIIe siècle, en mémoire de son épouse Mumtaz Mahal."},{"id":"q110","cat":"civilisation","q":"Dans quelle ville le grand voyageur Ibn Battouta est-il né ?","options":["Fès","Tunis","Le Caire","Tanger"],"answer":3,"explain":"Ibn Battouta est né à Tanger, au Maroc, en 1304. Il a voyagé pendant près de trente ans à travers l'Afrique, l'Asie et l'Europe."},{"id":"q111","cat":"civilisation","q":"Tombouctou, célèbre centre de savoir et de manuscrits, se trouve dans quel pays actuel ?","options":["L'Égypte","Le Maroc","Le Mali","Le Soudan"],"answer":2,"explain":"Tombouctou se trouve au Mali. Ses mosquées et ses écoles attiraient des étudiants et des savants de toute l'Afrique de l'Ouest."},{"id":"q112","cat":"civilisation","q":"Dans quelle ville se trouve al-Azhar, l'une des plus anciennes universités du monde ?","options":["Bagdad","Le Caire","Damas","Kairouan"],"answer":1,"explain":"Al-Azhar a été fondée au Caire au Xe siècle et reste aujourd'hui un grand centre de savoir."},{"id":"q113","cat":"civilisation","q":"Dans quelle ville se trouve le Dôme du Rocher ?","options":["Jérusalem","Damas","Médine","Le Caire"],"answer":0,"explain":"Le Dôme du Rocher se trouve à Jérusalem. Il a été achevé à la fin du VIIe siècle sous le calife omeyyade Abd al-Malik."},{"id":"q114","cat":"civilisation","q":"Quel architecte ottoman a conçu la mosquée Süleymaniye d'Istanbul ?","options":["Al-Jazari","Ulugh Beg","Mimar Sinan","Ibn Khaldoun"],"answer":2,"explain":"Mimar Sinan, architecte en chef de l'Empire ottoman au XVIe siècle, a conçu la Süleymaniye à Istanbul et la Selimiye à Edirne."},{"id":"q115","cat":"civilisation","q":"Dans le monde musulman médiéval, qu'était un « bimaristan » ?","options":["Une bibliothèque","Un observatoire","Un marché couvert","Un hôpital"],"answer":3,"explain":"Un bimaristan était un hôpital. Des villes comme Bagdad, Damas et Le Caire avaient de grands hôpitaux où l'on soignait les malades."},{"id":"q116","cat":"civilisation","q":"Quel est le premier mois du calendrier hégirien ?","options":["Mouharram","Ramadan","Rajab","Chawwal"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mouharram est le premier des douze mois du calendrier hégirien, qui suit la lune."},{"id":"q117","cat":"civilisation","q":"Dans une mosquée, qu'est-ce que le mihrab ?","options":["La chaire utilisée pour le sermon du vendredi","La niche qui indique la direction de La Mecque","La tour utilisée pour l'appel à la prière","La fontaine utilisée pour les ablutions"],"answer":1,"explain":"Le mihrab est une niche dans le mur qui indique la qibla, la direction de La Mecque. La chaire s'appelle le minbar et la tour le minaret."},{"id":"q118","cat":"civilisation","q":"La Grande Mosquée de Kairouan, l'une des plus anciennes d'Afrique, se trouve dans quel pays ?","options":["La Tunisie","Le Maroc","L'Égypte","La Syrie"],"answer":0,"explain":"Kairouan se trouve en Tunisie. Sa Grande Mosquée remonte à la fondation de la ville par Oqba ibn Nafi en 670."},{"id":"q119","cat":"civilisation","q":"L'écriture coufique, l'un des plus anciens styles de la calligraphie arabe, tire son nom de quelle ville ?","options":["Cordoue","Konya","Koufa","Kaboul"],"answer":2,"explain":"L'écriture coufique tire son nom de Koufa, en Irak. Beaucoup de copies anciennes du Coran ont été écrites dans ce style anguleux."},{"id":"q120","cat":"civilisation","q":"Dans la civilisation islamique, qu'est-ce qu'un « waqf » ?","options":["Une caravane de commerce","Un style de calligraphie","Une fondation pieuse de bienfaisance","Un impôt sur les récoltes"],"answer":2,"explain":"Un waqf est un bien réservé au bien commun, comme une école, un hôpital ou une fontaine. Ses revenus continuent de servir les gens au fil du temps."}]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (kind, lang) do update set payload = excluded.payload, updated_at = now();
insert into public.premium_content (kind, lang, payload) values ('days', 'fr', $tk${"new-year":{"detail":"Le premier jour de Muharram ouvre l'année hégirienne. Le calendrier islamique est lunaire : chaque mois commence avec le nouveau croissant, si bien que l'année compte environ onze jours de moins que l'année solaire.\n\nCe calendrier a été organisé sous le calife Omar ibn al-Khattab, quelques années après la mort du Prophète Muhammad ﷺ. Les Compagnons ont choisi de compter les années à partir de l'Hégire, l'émigration du Prophète ﷺ de La Mecque à Médine en 622, et de garder Muharram comme premier mois. L'Hégire elle-même a eu lieu plus tard dans l'année, au mois de Rabi' al-Awwal.\n\nMuharram est l'un des quatre mois sacrés mentionnés dans le Coran (9:36). Le Prophète ﷺ a enseigné que le meilleur jeûne après Ramadan est celui du mois d'Allah, Muharram (Sahih Muslim 1163).\n\nAucune prière ni célébration particulière n'est prescrite pour ce jour. Beaucoup de musulmans en profitent simplement pour se rappeler l'Hégire, faire le bilan de l'année écoulée et prendre de bonnes résolutions.","sources":["Coran 9:36","Sahih Muslim 1163"]},"tasua":{"detail":"Tasu'a est le neuvième jour de Muharram, la veille d'Achoura.\n\nLorsque le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ a jeûné Achoura et demandé aux musulmans de le jeûner, ses Compagnons lui ont fait remarquer que les juifs et les chrétiens honoraient aussi ce jour. Il a répondu que l'année suivante, si Allah le voulait, ils jeûneraient également le neuvième. Il est décédé avant le Muharram suivant (Sahih Muslim 1134).\n\nC'est pourquoi les savants recommandent de jeûner le neuvième jour avec le dixième. Le jeûne des musulmans a ainsi sa forme propre, tout en gardant le lien avec le jour où Allah a sauvé Moïse (paix sur lui).\n\nCe jeûne est facultatif. Celui qui ne peut jeûner que le dixième jeûne tout de même Achoura, et personne ne doit culpabiliser s'il est malade, en voyage ou incapable de jeûner. Muharram tout entier est un mois sacré, et le Prophète ﷺ a enseigné que le jeûne de ce mois est le meilleur après celui de Ramadan (Sahih Muslim 1163).","sources":["Sahih Muslim 1134","Sahih Muslim 1163"]},"ashura":{"detail":"Achoura est le dixième jour de Muharram. Son histoire remonte à Moïse (paix sur lui) : ce jour-là, Allah l'a sauvé, lui et les Enfants d'Israël, de leur ennemi.\n\nQuand le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ est arrivé à Médine, il a trouvé les juifs en train de jeûner ce jour en souvenir de cette délivrance. Il a dit que les musulmans avaient plus de droit sur Moïse ; il l'a donc jeûné et a demandé aux musulmans de le jeûner (Sahih al-Bukhari 2004). Lorsque le jeûne de Ramadan est devenu obligatoire, celui d'Achoura est resté un acte facultatif et recommandé (Sahih al-Bukhari 2002).\n\nInterrogé sur sa récompense, le Prophète ﷺ a dit qu'il expie les péchés de l'année écoulée (Sahih Muslim 1162). Il est recommandé de jeûner aussi le neuvième jour (Sahih Muslim 1134).","sources":["Sahih al-Bukhari 2004","Sahih al-Bukhari 2002","Sahih Muslim 1162","Sahih Muslim 1134"]},"mawlid":{"detail":"Le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ est né à La Mecque, dans le clan des Banu Hachim de la tribu de Quraych, l'année appelée l'année de l'Éléphant, vers 570. Son père Abdallah est mort avant sa naissance, et sa mère Amina est morte alors qu'il était encore un petit enfant.\n\nIl est établi qu'il est né un lundi : interrogé sur le jeûne du lundi, il a dit que c'était le jour où il était né et le jour où la révélation lui était venue (Sahih Muslim 1162). La plupart des historiens situent sa naissance au mois de Rabi' al-Awwal. Le 12 est la date la plus connue, mais d'autres jours ont été proposés et aucun n'est certain.\n\nLes musulmans ont des avis différents sur le fait de marquer ce jour. Beaucoup se réunissent pour évoquer sa vie, prier sur lui et partager un repas. D'autres ne distinguent pas ce jour, car le Prophète ﷺ et ses Compagnons ne l'ont pas fait. Tous s'accordent sur l'amour qu'on lui doit et sur le fait de suivre son exemple chaque jour ; le Coran le décrit comme une miséricorde pour les mondes (21:107).","sources":["Sahih Muslim 1162","Coran 21:107"]},"isra-miraj":{"detail":"Al-Isra est le voyage nocturne du Prophète Muhammad ﷺ de la Mosquée sacrée de La Mecque à la mosquée al-Aqsa de Jérusalem. Le Coran le mentionne au début de la sourate al-Isra (17:1). Al-Mi'raj est son ascension, de là, à travers les cieux, où il a rencontré des prophètes antérieurs (paix sur eux) et où lui ont été montrés de grands signes de son Seigneur ; la sourate an-Najm y fait allusion (53:13-18).\n\nCela s'est passé à La Mecque, avant l'Hégire, dans une période d'épreuve pour le Prophète ﷺ. Le don le plus durable de cette nuit, ce sont les cinq prières quotidiennes, rendues obligatoires à ce moment-là (Sahih al-Bukhari 349).\n\nLa date exacte n'est pas connue avec certitude. Le 27 Rajab est la date la plus couramment retenue, mais les premiers historiens mentionnent d'autres dates.\n\nAucune prière ni aucun jeûne particulier n'est établi pour cette nuit. Beaucoup de musulmans profitent de l'occasion pour raconter l'événement et se rappeler la valeur de la prière ; d'autres ne marquent pas cette date. Rajab est l'un des quatre mois sacrés (9:36).","sources":["Coran 17:1","Coran 53:13-18","Sahih al-Bukhari 349","Coran 9:36"]},"mid-shaban":{"detail":"Cha'ban est le huitième mois, juste avant Ramadan. Aïcha (qu'Allah l'agrée) a rapporté qu'elle n'avait jamais vu le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ jeûner, en dehors de Ramadan, autant qu'en Cha'ban (Sahih al-Bukhari 1969). C'est un mois de préparation : jeûnes facultatifs, rattrapage des jours manqués du Ramadan précédent et lecture du Coran.\n\nLa nuit du 15, au milieu du mois, est connue dans de nombreux pays sous des noms comme Laylat al-Bara'a, Shab-e-Barat ou Nisfu Sya'ban. Plusieurs récits parlent du pardon d'Allah en cette nuit. Les savants ont des avis différents sur la solidité de ces récits, et donc sur la pratique : beaucoup de musulmans passent une partie de la nuit en prière et en invocation et jeûnent la journée ; d'autres la considèrent comme n'importe quelle nuit de Cha'ban et ne lui réservent pas d'adoration particulière.\n\nCe qui fait l'accord de tous, c'est que demander pardon, se réconcilier avec les autres et jeûner de façon facultative sont de bonnes choses durant tout Cha'ban, la porte d'entrée de Ramadan.","sources":["Sahih al-Bukhari 1969"]},"ramadan-start":{"detail":"Ramadan est le neuvième mois de l'année hégirienne, le mois durant lequel le Coran a été révélé comme guide pour les gens (2:185). Son jeûne est l'un des cinq piliers de l'islam (Sahih al-Bukhari 8). Il a été prescrit à Médine, la deuxième année après l'Hégire.\n\nLe mois commence lorsque le nouveau croissant est aperçu ; s'il ne peut pas être vu, on complète Cha'ban à trente jours (Sahih al-Bukhari 1909). C'est pourquoi le début peut différer d'un jour d'un pays à l'autre.\n\nDe l'aube au coucher du soleil, ceux qui jeûnent s'abstiennent de manger, de boire et de relations conjugales. Les malades et les voyageurs en sont dispensés et rattrapent les jours plus tard, et ceux qui ne peuvent pas du tout jeûner nourrissent un pauvre à la place (2:184-185). Le Coran donne le but du jeûne : grandir en piété (2:183).\n\nLe Prophète Muhammad ﷺ a dit que lorsque Ramadan commence, les portes du ciel sont ouvertes, les portes de l'Enfer sont fermées et les démons sont enchaînés (Sahih al-Bukhari 1899). C'est un mois de prière, de Coran et de générosité.","sources":["Coran 2:183-185","Sahih al-Bukhari 8","Sahih al-Bukhari 1909","Sahih al-Bukhari 1899"]},"last-ten-nights":{"detail":"Les dix dernières nuits de Ramadan commencent la veille au soir du 21e jour et vont jusqu'à la fin du mois, qui compte 29 ou 30 jours. C'est la partie la plus précieuse de Ramadan, car Laylat al-Qadr se trouve parmi elles.\n\nAïcha (qu'Allah l'agrée) a rapporté que lorsque ces dix nuits commençaient, le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ resserrait sa ceinture, passait la nuit en prière et réveillait sa famille (Sahih al-Bukhari 2024). Il s'appliquait dans l'adoration durant ces nuits plus qu'à tout autre moment (Sahih Muslim 1175).\n\nIl pratiquait aussi l'i'tikaf, la retraite à la mosquée pour se consacrer à l'adoration, pendant les dix derniers jours de Ramadan, jusqu'à sa mort (Sahih al-Bukhari 2026).\n\nCe qui est recommandé : la prière de nuit, la lecture du Coran, beaucoup d'invocations, la demande de pardon et l'aumône. Ceux qui ne peuvent pas veiller longtemps peuvent quand même prier un peu chaque nuit, pour ne pas manquer la nuit qui vaut mieux que mille mois (97:3). C'est aussi durant ces jours que l'on prépare la zakat al-fitr, avant la prière de l'Aïd.","sources":["Sahih al-Bukhari 2024","Sahih Muslim 1175","Sahih al-Bukhari 2026","Coran 97:3"]},"laylat-al-qadr":{"detail":"Laylat al-Qadr, la Nuit du Destin, est la nuit durant laquelle le Coran a été révélé. Une sourate entière lui est consacrée : elle est meilleure que mille mois, les anges et l'Esprit y descendent avec la permission de leur Seigneur, et elle est paix jusqu'au lever de l'aube (97:1-5). Le Coran l'appelle aussi une nuit bénie (44:3).\n\nSa date exacte est restée cachée. Le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ a demandé aux croyants de la rechercher dans les nuits impaires des dix dernières de Ramadan (Sahih al-Bukhari 2017) : la 21e, la 23e, la 25e, la 27e ou la 29e. La 27e est la nuit la plus souvent citée, et beaucoup de communautés se rassemblent ce soir-là, mais ce n'est pas certain, et les savants ont des avis différents. Le plus sage est de faire des efforts pendant les dix nuits.\n\nLe Prophète ﷺ a dit que celui qui veille en prière durant Laylat al-Qadr, avec foi et en espérant la récompense, verra ses péchés passés pardonnés (Sahih al-Bukhari 2014). Les actes recommandés sont la prière de nuit, la récitation du Coran, l'invocation et la demande de pardon à Allah.","sources":["Coran 97:1-5","Coran 44:3","Sahih al-Bukhari 2017","Sahih al-Bukhari 2014"]},"eid-al-fitr":{"detail":"L'Aïd al-Fitr, la fête de la rupture du jeûne, tombe le premier jour de Chawwal et clôt le mois de Ramadan. Le Coran demande aux croyants de compléter le nombre de jours et de proclamer la grandeur d'Allah pour les avoir guidés (2:185).\n\nAvant la prière de l'Aïd, on donne la zakat al-fitr : une petite aumône en nourriture de base, due pour chaque membre du foyer, afin que les pauvres puissent prendre part à la fête. Le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ a ordonné qu'elle soit versée avant que les gens ne sortent pour la prière (Sahih al-Bukhari 1503).\n\nLe matin, les musulmans prononcent le takbir, mettent leurs plus beaux vêtements et se rassemblent pour la prière de l'Aïd, suivie d'un sermon (Sahih al-Bukhari 956). Ce jour-là, le Prophète ﷺ ne partait pas à la prière avant d'avoir mangé quelques dattes (Sahih al-Bukhari 953).\n\nIl n'est pas permis de jeûner le jour de l'Aïd (Sahih al-Bukhari 1991). C'est un jour de gratitude, de visites familiales et de bonté envers les voisins. Les jeûnes facultatifs, comme les six jours de Chawwal, peuvent commencer dès le deuxième jour.","sources":["Coran 2:185","Sahih al-Bukhari 1503","Sahih al-Bukhari 956","Sahih al-Bukhari 953","Sahih al-Bukhari 1991"]},"six-shawwal":{"detail":"Chawwal est le mois qui suit Ramadan. Le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ a dit que celui qui jeûne Ramadan puis le fait suivre de six jours de Chawwal, c'est comme s'il avait jeûné toute l'année (Sahih Muslim 1164).\n\nLes savants l'expliquent par la règle selon laquelle une bonne action est récompensée dix fois (6:160) : le mois de Ramadan compte pour dix mois, et six jours de plus comptent pour deux mois, ce qui fait une année entière.\n\nN'importe quels six jours du mois comptent. L'application affiche du 2 au 7 uniquement comme période pratique : les jours peuvent être jeûnés à la suite ou séparément, au début, au milieu ou à la fin de Chawwal. Le seul jour exclu est le 1er, le jour de l'Aïd, où le jeûne n'est pas permis.\n\nCe jeûne est facultatif, pas obligatoire. Celui à qui il reste des jours de Ramadan à rattraper doit aussi les prévoir : beaucoup de savants conseillent de les rattraper d'abord, tandis que d'autres permettent de commencer par les six jours. C'est une manière douce de garder vivantes les bonnes habitudes de Ramadan.","sources":["Sahih Muslim 1164","Coran 6:160"]},"dhul-hijja-nine":{"detail":"Dhul-Hijja est le douzième mois, le mois du Hajj. Ses dix premiers jours comptent parmi les meilleurs de l'année : le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ a dit qu'aucune bonne œuvre n'est meilleure que celles accomplies durant ces jours (Sahih al-Bukhari 969). Beaucoup de commentateurs comprennent aussi que les dix nuits par lesquelles Allah jure dans le Coran (89:2) désignent ces jours.\n\nComme le jeûne fait partie des meilleures œuvres, il est recommandé de jeûner pendant les neuf premiers jours, autant qu'on le peut. Le neuvième, le jour d'Arafat, est le plus recommandé. Le dixième est l'Aïd al-Adha, où le jeûne n'est pas permis.\n\nIl est aussi recommandé de répéter le takbir, de louer souvent Allah, de prier, de faire l'aumône et de lire le Coran. Le Coran parle d'évoquer le nom d'Allah durant des jours connus (22:28).\n\nCelui qui a l'intention d'offrir un sacrifice est invité à ne pas se couper les cheveux ni les ongles du début du mois jusqu'à ce que le sacrifice soit accompli (Sahih Muslim 1977). Pendant ces jours, des pèlerins venus du monde entier se rassemblent à La Mecque.","sources":["Sahih al-Bukhari 969","Coran 89:2","Coran 22:28","Sahih Muslim 1977"]},"arafat":{"detail":"Le 9 Dhul-Hijja est le jour d'Arafat, le cœur du Hajj. De midi jusqu'au coucher du soleil, les pèlerins se tiennent dans la plaine d'Arafat, près de La Mecque, en prière et en invocation. La station à Arafat est le pilier essentiel du pèlerinage.\n\nC'est ce jour-là, un vendredi, pendant le pèlerinage d'adieu du Prophète Muhammad ﷺ, qu'a été révélé le verset annonçant qu'Allah avait parachevé la religion (5:3), comme l'a rapporté Omar ibn al-Khattab (Sahih al-Bukhari 45).\n\nLe Prophète ﷺ a dit qu'il n'y a pas de jour où Allah affranchit du Feu plus de serviteurs que le jour d'Arafat (Sahih Muslim 1348).\n\nPour ceux qui ne sont pas au Hajj, jeûner ce jour est fortement recommandé : le Prophète ﷺ a dit qu'il expie les péchés de l'année précédente et de l'année à venir (Sahih Muslim 1162). Il n'est pas demandé aux pèlerins de jeûner ; le Prophète ﷺ ne jeûnait pas lorsqu'il se tenait à Arafat (Sahih al-Bukhari 1988).\n\nC'est un jour d'invocation, de rappel d'Allah et de demande de pardon, où que l'on soit.","sources":["Coran 5:3","Sahih al-Bukhari 45","Sahih Muslim 1348","Sahih Muslim 1162","Sahih al-Bukhari 1988"]},"eid-al-adha":{"detail":"L'Aïd al-Adha, la fête du Sacrifice, tombe le 10 Dhul-Hijja, le lendemain d'Arafat. Il coïncide avec les principaux rites du Hajj à Mina et à La Mecque.\n\nIl rappelle l'épreuve d'Abraham (paix sur lui). Il a vu en rêve qu'il sacrifiait son fils ; tous deux se sont soumis à l'ordre d'Allah, et Allah a racheté le fils par un sacrifice immense (37:102-107).\n\nLa journée commence par la prière de l'Aïd, suivie d'un sermon (Sahih al-Bukhari 956). Après la prière, ceux qui en ont les moyens sacrifient un mouton, une chèvre, une vache ou un chameau. La viande est mangée par la famille, offerte aux proches et aux voisins et donnée aux pauvres. Le Coran rappelle aux croyants que ni la viande ni le sang n'atteignent Allah ; ce qui L'atteint, c'est leur piété (22:37).\n\nIl n'est pas permis de jeûner ce jour-là (Sahih al-Bukhari 1991). Les musulmans prononcent le takbir, mettent leurs plus beaux vêtements, se rendent visite et partagent des repas. Le sacrifice peut aussi être accompli les jours suivants.","sources":["Coran 37:102-107","Sahih al-Bukhari 956","Coran 22:37","Sahih al-Bukhari 1991"]},"tashriq":{"detail":"Les jours de Tachriq sont les 11, 12 et 13 Dhul-Hijja, les trois jours qui suivent l'Aïd al-Adha. Le Prophète Muhammad ﷺ les a décrits comme des jours où l'on mange, où l'on boit et où l'on se rappelle Allah (Sahih Muslim 1141).\n\nDurant ces jours, les pèlerins séjournent à Mina et lancent chaque jour des cailloux sur les trois stèles appelées jamarat. Le Coran leur demande d'invoquer Allah pendant ces jours comptés, et dit qu'il n'y a pas de péché pour celui qui se hâte de partir au bout de deux jours, ni pour celui qui reste plus longtemps (2:203). Les pèlerins peuvent donc partir le 12 ou rester jusqu'au 13.\n\nIl n'est pas permis de jeûner ces jours-là. La seule exception rapportée concerne les pèlerins qui doivent une offrande et n'en trouvent pas (Sahih al-Bukhari 1997).\n\nPour tous les autres, ce sont des jours de joie et de gratitude : partager la viande du sacrifice, rendre visite à ses proches et prononcer le takbir, surtout après les prières quotidiennes. Le sacrifice de l'Aïd peut encore être accompli durant ces jours, avec des détails qui varient selon les écoles juridiques.","sources":["Sahih Muslim 1141","Coran 2:203","Sahih al-Bukhari 1997"]}}$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (kind, lang) do update set payload = excluded.payload, updated_at = now();

-- ===== 10-premium-en =====
-- Contenu Premium — en
-- Fichier GÉNÉRÉ par scripts/build-seed.mjs : ne pas modifier à la main. Idempotent (peut être relancé).

insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$adam$tk$, 'en', 1, $tk$Adam$tk$, $tk$آدم$tk$, $tk$The first human being and the first prophet$tk$, $tk$Adam, peace be upon him, is the first human being and the first prophet. The Qur'an tells us that Allah announced to the angels that He would place on earth a being entrusted with it. The angels wondered about a creature that might spread corruption and shed blood. Allah answered that He knew what they did not know.

Allah created Adam from clay and taught him the names of all things. When the angels were asked to give these names, they admitted that they knew only what Allah had taught them. Adam then named them. Knowledge was the first honour given to humankind.

Allah commanded the angels to prostrate before Adam. All obeyed except Iblis, who refused out of pride, claiming that he, created from fire, was better than a being made of clay. He was expelled, and he swore that he would try to mislead the children of Adam.

Adam and his wife were settled in the Garden. They could eat freely of everything, except from one tree. Satan whispered to them, swearing that he was a sincere adviser and promising them eternal life. They both ate from the tree. The Qur'an does not put the blame on the wife alone: it says that both of them slipped. Their nakedness became apparent to them, and they began to cover themselves with leaves of the Garden.

What followed is the heart of the story. Adam and his wife did not argue or make excuses, as Iblis had done. They turned to their Lord, admitted that they had wronged themselves and asked for His forgiveness and mercy. Adam received words from his Lord, and Allah accepted his repentance. The Qur'an says that his Lord then chose him, forgave him and guided him.

Adam and his wife were sent down to earth, where human beings would live, die and be raised again. Allah promised that guidance would come to them, and that those who follow it will have nothing to fear and will not grieve.

The Qur'an does not say where the Garden was, what kind of tree it was, or how long Adam lived, and there is no need to guess. What it does teach is that all human beings come from the same father and mother, and that the door of repentance has been open since the very first day.$tk$, $tk$Everyone makes mistakes; what matters is to admit them without excuses and to come back to Allah quickly. Pride closes the door that humility opens.$tk$, $tk$["2:30-39","7:11-25","20:115-123"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$idris$tk$, 'en', 2, $tk$Idris$tk$, $tk$إدريس$tk$, $tk$A man of truth raised to a high station$tk$, $tk$Idris, peace be upon him, is one of the prophets about whom the Qur'an says very little. He is mentioned by name in only two places, and each time in a few words.

In Surah Maryam, after the stories of several prophets, Allah tells the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ to mention Idris in the Book. He is described as a man of truth and a prophet, and the Qur'an adds that Allah raised him to a high station. In Surah al-Anbiya, he is named together with Ismail and Dhul-Kifl: all three were among the patient, Allah admitted them into His mercy, and they were among the righteous.

That is all the Qur'an tells us. It does not say when Idris lived, to which people he was sent, or what happened during his mission. Scholars have explained the high station in different ways: some understand it as a high rank and honour with Allah, others as a raised place in the heavens. In the sound hadith about the Night Journey, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ relates that he met Idris in the fourth heaven, and that Idris welcomed him (Sahih al-Bukhari, no. 3887).

Many later books add details: that he was the first to write with a pen, to sew clothes or to study the stars, or that he is the same person as a figure known from older scriptures. These reports do not come from the Qur'an or from sound hadith. They cannot be presented as established facts, and it is wiser to leave them aside.

What remains is short but precious. Out of a whole life, the Qur'an has kept three qualities: truthfulness, patience and righteousness. The Arabic word used for him, siddiq, describes someone who is deeply truthful, whose actions match his words and whose words match his faith. No account of power, wealth or fame is attached to his name; only his character has been preserved.

The few verses about Idris also remind us that a person's worth with Allah does not depend on how much people know about him. A prophet may be honoured in the Qur'an in two lines, and those two lines are enough to make him an example.$tk$, $tk$Be truthful and patient even when nobody notices: with Allah, character counts for more than fame.$tk$, $tk$["19:56-57","21:85-86"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$nuh$tk$, 'en', 3, $tk$Nuh (Noah)$tk$, $tk$نوح$tk$, $tk$The tireless caller and builder of the Ark$tk$, $tk$Nuh, peace be upon him, was sent to a people who had fallen into the worship of idols. The Qur'an even gives the names of these idols: Wadd, Suwa, Yaghuth, Yauq and Nasr. Nuh came to them with a simple message: worship Allah alone, ask His forgiveness, and He will forgive you.

The Qur'an says that Nuh remained among his people for a thousand years less fifty. He called them night and day, in public and in private. He reminded them that Allah is ever forgiving and that He would send them rain in abundance. But the more he called, the more they turned away. They put their fingers in their ears and covered themselves with their clothes so as not to hear him.

The leaders of his people looked down on him. They said he was only a man like them, and that only the lowest people followed him. Nuh answered that he asked them for no wealth, that his reward was with Allah alone, and that he would never drive away those who believed.

When it was revealed to Nuh that no one else among his people would believe, Allah ordered him to build the Ark under His watch. The chiefs mocked him each time they passed by. Then the command of Allah came, and the flood began. Nuh was told to take on board a pair of each kind of creature, his family and those who believed, and they were only a few.

The Ark sailed through waves like mountains. Nuh saw one of his sons standing apart and called him to come aboard. The son answered that he would take refuge on a mountain. A wave came between them, and he was among the drowned. Nuh spoke to his Lord about his son, and Allah told him that this son was not truly of his family, because his conduct was not righteous. Nuh accepted this with humility.

Then the earth was ordered to swallow its water and the sky to hold back its rain. The Ark came to rest on Mount Judi, and Nuh was told to disembark in peace, with blessings upon him and those with him. The Qur'an says that the Ark was left as a sign for all people.$tk$, $tk$Keep doing what is right even when results are slow to come. Success is measured by faithfulness, not by the number of people who follow you.$tk$, $tk$["11:25-48","71:1-28","29:14-15","54:9-15"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$hud$tk$, 'en', 4, $tk$Hud$tk$, $tk$هود$tk$, $tk$The messenger sent to the people of Ad$tk$, $tk$Hud, peace be upon him, was sent to the people of Ad, who lived in a region the Qur'an calls al-Ahqaf, the sand dunes. They came after the people of Nuh and had been given great strength. Hud was one of them: the Qur'an calls him their brother.

His message was the same as that of every prophet: worship Allah alone, you have no other god. He told them that he asked them for no reward. He invited them to ask forgiveness of their Lord and to turn back to Him, promising that Allah would then send them abundant rain and add strength to their strength.

The leaders of Ad answered harshly. They accused him of foolishness and of lying. Some even said that one of their gods must have struck him with evil. Hud replied calmly that there was no foolishness in him, that he was a messenger from the Lord of the worlds and a trustworthy adviser to them. He reminded them that Allah had made them successors after the people of Nuh, and he asked them to remember His favours.

Then he spoke words of great courage. He declared that he had nothing to do with the false gods they worshipped, and he challenged them to plot against him all together, without giving him any respite. He said he relied on Allah, his Lord and their Lord, who has power over every living creature.

The people of Ad did not change. They even asked Hud to bring the punishment he warned them about. One day they saw a cloud approaching their valleys and rejoiced, thinking it would bring rain. It was in fact a violent wind carrying a painful punishment. The Qur'an says that it blew against them for seven nights and eight days in a row, leaving the people lying like hollow trunks of palm trees. Afterwards nothing could be seen except their dwellings.

Hud and those who believed with him were saved by the mercy of Allah. The Qur'an does not tell us how many they were or where they went afterwards.

The story of Ad is told several times in the Qur'an as a warning to every powerful people: strength and prosperity are gifts, and they protect no one who uses them to reject the truth and to act with arrogance.$tk$, $tk$Strength, talent and wealth are gifts to be thankful for, not reasons to feel above others. When someone gives you sincere advice, listen before you judge.$tk$, $tk$["7:65-72","11:50-60","46:21-25","69:6-8"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$salih$tk$, 'en', 5, $tk$Salih$tk$, $tk$صالح$tk$, $tk$The messenger sent to Thamud$tk$, $tk$Salih, peace be upon him, was sent to the people of Thamud, who came after the people of Ad. They were skilled builders: the Qur'an says that they built palaces on the plains and carved homes out of the mountains. Salih was one of them, and before his mission his people had placed great hopes in him.

He called them to worship Allah alone. He reminded them that it was Allah who had brought them out of the earth and settled them on it, and he asked them to seek His forgiveness and not to spread corruption in the land. Their answer was disappointment: how could he forbid them to worship what their fathers had worshipped? They said they were in serious doubt about his message.

A few people believed, mostly among the weak. The proud leaders asked them, with contempt, whether they really thought Salih had been sent by his Lord. The believers answered plainly that they believed in what he had been sent with.

Allah then gave Thamud a clear sign: a she-camel. The Qur'an calls her the she-camel of Allah. Salih asked his people to let her graze freely on Allah's earth, to respect her turn to drink, and not to harm her, or else a punishment would seize them. The Qur'an does not describe how the she-camel appeared, and the detailed stories told about this are not established.

The test was simple, and they failed it. The Qur'an also mentions nine men in the city who spread corruption and who swore together to attack Salih and his family by night. The most wretched among them rose up, and the she-camel was hamstrung and killed. Then they defied Salih to bring the punishment he had promised.

Salih told them that they had three days left to enjoy in their homes, a promise that would not be denied. Then the punishment came: a mighty blast and an earthquake seized them, and they were left lifeless in their homes, as if they had never lived there. Salih and those who believed with him were saved by the mercy of Allah.

The Qur'an reports the last words of Salih as he turned away from them: he had conveyed to them the message of his Lord and given them sincere advice, but they did not like those who give advice.$tk$, $tk$A blessing is also a responsibility: respect what has been entrusted to you. Welcome sincere advice, even when it is not what you wanted to hear.$tk$, $tk$["7:73-79","11:61-68","27:45-53","91:11-15"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$ibrahim$tk$, 'en', 6, $tk$Ibrahim (Abraham)$tk$, $tk$إبراهيم$tk$, $tk$The friend of Allah and father of the prophets$tk$, $tk$Ibrahim, peace be upon him, grew up among people who worshipped idols and heavenly bodies. His own father, whom the Qur'an calls Azar, was one of them. From his youth, Allah had given Ibrahim sound judgement, and he could not accept that people should bow to statues they had made with their own hands.

The Qur'an tells how he reasoned with his people. He looked at a star, then at the moon, then at the sun. Each one set and disappeared, and each time he showed that something which disappears cannot be the Lord. He then declared that he turned his face towards the One who created the heavens and the earth.

One day, when the people had gone away, Ibrahim broke their idols into pieces, except the largest. When they questioned him, he told them to ask the big idol, if idols could speak. For a moment they realised how absurd their worship was. But pride won, and they decided to burn him. Allah commanded the fire to be cool and safe for Ibrahim, and he came out unharmed.

Ibrahim then left his people for the sake of his Lord and asked Him for a righteous child. In his old age, Allah granted him Ismail and Ishaq, and he gave thanks to the One who hears every prayer.

By the command of Allah, he settled some of his family in a valley without crops, near the Sacred House, and prayed that hearts would lean towards them and that they would be given fruits. Later, he raised the foundations of the Kaaba with his son Ismail. While they built, they asked Allah to accept their work and to send to the people of that land a messenger from among themselves.

Ibrahim was also tested in the hardest way: he saw in a dream that he was sacrificing his son. He told his son about it, and the son answered that he should do what he was commanded. When both had submitted, Allah called to Ibrahim that he had fulfilled the vision, and the son was ransomed with a great sacrifice. Muslims remember this every year at Eid al-Adha.

The Qur'an says that Ibrahim was tried by his Lord with commands and fulfilled them all, and that Allah made him a leader for people.$tk$, $tk$Use your mind to look for the truth, and have the courage to follow it even if you stand alone. What we give up for Allah is never lost.$tk$, $tk$["6:74-83","21:51-70","2:124-129","37:99-111","14:35-41"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$lut$tk$, 'en', 7, $tk$Lut (Lot)$tk$, $tk$لوط$tk$, $tk$The prophet who stood firm among a corrupt people$tk$, $tk$Lut, peace be upon him, lived in the time of Ibrahim, peace be upon him. The Qur'an says that he believed in Ibrahim's message. He was then sent as a prophet to a people whose city the Qur'an does not name.

These people had fallen into grave wrongs. The Qur'an says that they committed an indecency that no people before them had committed: the men approached men with desire instead of women. They also attacked travellers on the road and did evil openly in their gatherings. Lut called them to fear Allah and to give up these acts. He told them that he asked for no payment and that he was a trustworthy messenger to them.

Their only answer was to mock him and to threaten him. They said: drive Lut and his followers out of the city, for they are people who want to stay pure. They even challenged him to bring the punishment of Allah if he was telling the truth. Lut then asked his Lord for help against those who spread corruption.

Angels were sent with a mission. They first visited Ibrahim, to whom they brought good news of a son, and told him that they were going to the people of Lut. Then they came to Lut in the form of guests. He did not know who they were, and he was distressed, because he feared what his people would do to them. Indeed, the men of the city came rushing to his house. Lut begged them to fear Allah and not to shame him in front of his guests. He wished he had the strength to stop them.

The angels then revealed who they were: messengers of his Lord, whom the people would never reach. They told Lut to leave during the night with his family, and that no one should look back. His wife, however, would be among those who stayed behind and would share the fate of the others.

In the morning, the punishment fell. The city was turned upside down and stones of hard clay rained upon it. Lut and those of his family who followed him were saved.

The Qur'an says that the remains of that place were left as a clear sign for people who use their reason.$tk$, $tk$Doing right does not depend on what the majority does. Stay true to your values, even when you are mocked for wanting to stay pure.$tk$, $tk$["7:80-84","11:77-83","15:57-77","29:26-35"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$ismail$tk$, 'en', 8, $tk$Ismail (Ishmael)$tk$, $tk$إسماعيل$tk$, $tk$True to his promise, builder of the Kaaba with his father$tk$, $tk$Ismail, peace be upon him, is the son of Ibrahim, peace be upon him. Ibrahim had asked his Lord for a righteous child, and he thanked Allah for granting him Ismail and Ishaq in his old age.

The Qur'an reports the prayer of Ibrahim saying that he had settled some of his descendants in a valley without crops, near the Sacred House, so that they would establish prayer. A long hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari (no. 3364) relates how Ibrahim left Ismail, still a baby, with his mother Hajar in that empty valley of Mecca, how she ran between the hills of Safa and Marwa looking for water, and how the spring of Zamzam appeared. Pilgrims still walk between these two hills in memory of her trust in Allah.

The Qur'an then tells of a great test. Ibrahim saw in a dream that he was sacrificing his son, and he asked him what he thought. The son, described as gentle and forbearing, answered that his father should do as he was commanded, and that he would find him, if Allah willed, among the patient. When both had submitted, Allah called to Ibrahim that he had fulfilled the vision, and He ransomed the son with a great sacrifice. The Qur'an does not name the son in this passage; most Muslim scholars hold that it was Ismail.

Father and son then worked together on a task that still marks the life of Muslims. Allah charged Ibrahim and Ismail to purify His House for those who walk around it, stay there in worship, bow and prostrate. Together they raised the foundations of the Kaaba, asking Allah to accept this from them, to make them and their descendants people who submit to Him, and to send to the people of that land a messenger from among themselves. Muslims see in the coming of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, a descendant of Ismail, the answer to this prayer.

The Qur'an sums up the character of Ismail in a few words. He was true to his promise, a messenger and a prophet. He used to enjoin prayer and charity on his people, and his Lord was pleased with him. Elsewhere he is counted among the patient and among those whom Allah favoured.$tk$, $tk$Keep your promises, even small ones, and care about the faith of those close to you. Trust in Allah grows through patience in hard moments.$tk$, $tk$["2:125-129","19:54-55","37:101-107","14:37-39","21:85-86"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$ishaq$tk$, 'en', 9, $tk$Ishaq (Isaac)$tk$, $tk$إسحاق$tk$, $tk$The good news given to Ibrahim in his old age$tk$, $tk$Ishaq, peace be upon him, is the second son of Ibrahim, peace be upon him. The Qur'an tells the story of his birth in some detail, but says little about the rest of his life.

One day, guests came to Ibrahim and greeted him with peace. He returned their greeting and, generous as he was, quickly brought them a roasted calf. But the guests did not reach for the food. Ibrahim felt uneasy. They told him not to be afraid: they were angels, sent to the people of Lut, and they brought him good news of a son full of knowledge.

Ibrahim's wife was standing nearby. The Qur'an says that she was given the good news of Ishaq and, after Ishaq, of Yaqub. She was astonished: how could she have a child when she was an old woman who had never been able to bear children, and her husband was an old man? The angels answered: was she surprised at the decree of Allah? They wished the mercy and blessings of Allah upon the people of the house.

The promise came true. Ibrahim praised Allah for giving him Ismail and Ishaq in his old age, and declared that his Lord hears every prayer.

The Qur'an describes Ishaq as a prophet from among the righteous, and says that Allah blessed Ibrahim and Ishaq. It adds that Allah made Ishaq and Yaqub leaders who guided by His command, and inspired them to do good deeds, to establish prayer and to give charity; they were devoted worshippers of Allah. When Yaqub was close to death, his sons promised to worship the God of his fathers, Ibrahim, Ismail and Ishaq, one God.

Beyond this, the Qur'an gives no account of Ishaq's mission or of the events of his life. The many details found in other sources are not confirmed by the Qur'an or by sound hadith, so we do not repeat them here.

From Ishaq came Yaqub, and from Yaqub a long line of prophets among the Children of Israel, including Yusuf, Musa, Dawud and Isa, peace be upon them all. The birth of Ishaq shows that Allah keeps His promises in His own time, even when every visible cause seems to say that it is too late.$tk$, $tk$Never think it is too late for a prayer to be answered. Generosity towards guests and trust in Allah open doors that seemed closed.$tk$, $tk$["11:69-73","37:112-113","51:24-30","14:39","21:72-73"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$yaqub$tk$, 'en', 10, $tk$Yaqub (Jacob)$tk$, $tk$يعقوب$tk$, $tk$The father whose patience was beautiful$tk$, $tk$Yaqub, peace be upon him, is the son of Ishaq and the grandson of Ibrahim, peace be upon them. He is also known as Israil, and his descendants are called the Children of Israel. The Qur'an speaks of him mainly as a father, in the story of his son Yusuf.

When young Yusuf told him about a dream in which eleven stars, the sun and the moon bowed to him, Yaqub understood that Allah had chosen this child. He advised him not to tell his brothers, for fear that jealousy would push them to plot against him.

The brothers did plot. They asked to take Yusuf out with them. Yaqub said that it saddened him to let him go and that he feared a wolf might eat him. In the evening they came back in tears, with false blood on Yusuf's shirt. Yaqub did not believe their story. He told them that their own souls had led them to do something, and he chose beautiful patience, asking Allah alone for help.

Years passed. When a second son was kept in Egypt, the pain came back. Yaqub turned away and grieved for Yusuf, and his eyes turned white with sorrow, which he kept inside. His sons blamed him for still thinking about Yusuf. He answered that he complained of his sadness and grief only to Allah, and that he knew from Allah what they did not know.

Then he gave them this advice: go and look for Yusuf and his brother, and do not despair of the mercy of Allah.

His hope was rewarded. Yusuf, now a high official in Egypt, sent his shirt to be placed over his father's face. As the caravan left Egypt, Yaqub said that he could sense the scent of Yusuf. When the shirt was laid on his face, he could see again. His sons asked him to pray for their forgiveness, and he promised to do so. The whole family was reunited in Egypt.

The Qur'an also records Yaqub's last concern. As death approached, he asked his sons what they would worship after him. They answered that they would worship his God and the God of his fathers, Ibrahim, Ismail and Ishaq, one God.$tk$, $tk$Sadness is not a lack of faith: bring your pain to Allah and keep hoping. Never despair of His mercy.$tk$, $tk$["2:132-133","12:4-6","12:83-87","12:93-100"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$yusuf$tk$, 'en', 11, $tk$Yusuf (Joseph)$tk$, $tk$يوسف$tk$, $tk$The truthful one, from the well to the storehouses of Egypt$tk$, $tk$The story of Yusuf, peace be upon him, fills a whole surah of the Qur'an. As a boy, he dreamed that eleven stars, the sun and the moon bowed to him. His father Yaqub, peace be upon him, told him to keep the dream from his brothers.

The brothers were jealous of their father's love for Yusuf. They took him out and threw him to the bottom of a well, then told their father that a wolf had eaten him. Travellers passing by drew Yusuf out of the well, and he was sold in Egypt for a few coins.

He grew up in the house of a high Egyptian official. When he became a man, the wife of this official tried to seduce him. Yusuf sought refuge in Allah and ran to the door; she tore his shirt from behind, which proved his innocence. Yet he was still sent to prison. He had even said that prison was dearer to him than what he was being called to do.

In prison, Yusuf was known for his goodness. He interpreted the dreams of two fellow prisoners and took the opportunity to speak to them about the one God. One of them was freed, but forgot to mention Yusuf to his master, and Yusuf remained in prison for several years.

Then the king had a dream: seven fat cows eaten by seven lean ones, and seven green ears of grain with others that were dry. Yusuf explained it: seven years of good harvests, then seven hard years, so the grain had to be stored. Before leaving prison, he asked that his innocence be established, and the wife of the official admitted the truth. Yusuf asked to be put in charge of the storehouses of the land, and he managed them carefully.

When famine came, his brothers travelled to Egypt for food. Yusuf recognised them, but they did not recognise him. After several journeys, he revealed who he was. They admitted their wrong. He answered that there was no blame on them that day and that he hoped Allah would forgive them.

Yusuf then brought his parents and all his family to Egypt. His childhood dream had come true. He ended with a prayer, asking his Lord to let him die in submission to Him and to join him with the righteous.$tk$, $tk$Stay honest and pure whether you are at the bottom or at the top. When you finally have power over those who hurt you, forgiveness is the noblest choice.$tk$, $tk$["12:4-21","12:23-35","12:36-57","12:88-101"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$ayyub$tk$, 'en', 12, $tk$Ayyub (Job)$tk$, $tk$أيوب$tk$, $tk$The model of patience$tk$, $tk$Ayyub, peace be upon him, is the prophet whose name has become a symbol of patience. The Qur'an counts him among the prophets who received revelation and among the descendants of Ibrahim whom Allah guided.

The Qur'an tells his story in a few verses, without many details. Ayyub was struck by a severe trial that touched his health and his family. The Qur'an does not describe his illness, nor does it say how long it lasted. Many stories have been told about this, some of them exaggerated and unworthy of a prophet; they are not established and we leave them aside.

What the Qur'an has kept is the way Ayyub spoke to his Lord. He did not complain to people, and he did not accuse Allah. He simply called out that hardship had touched him, and that Allah is the Most Merciful of the merciful. In another passage, he says that Satan had afflicted him with distress and suffering. His prayer contains no demand: he describes his condition, he praises the mercy of his Lord, and he leaves the rest to Him.

Allah answered him. He told him to strike the ground with his foot: a spring of cool water appeared, to wash with and to drink. His suffering was removed. Allah gave him back his family and as many more with them, as a mercy from Him and a reminder for those who worship and for people of understanding.

The Qur'an also mentions that Ayyub had made an oath during his trial. Allah showed him a gentle way to keep his word without doing harm: to take a bundle of grass in his hand and strike with it. The Qur'an does not explain the circumstances of this oath in detail.

The passage ends with the praise of Allah Himself: He found Ayyub patient, an excellent servant, who always turned back to Him.

The patience of Ayyub was not silence or the absence of pain. He felt his suffering and said so, but he said it to Allah, with respect and hope. His story has comforted sick and grieving people for centuries, because it shows that a trial is not a sign of rejection by Allah, and that relief can come from where no one expects it.$tk$, $tk$In hardship, speak to Allah about your pain without losing respect or hope. Patience does not mean feeling nothing; it means staying faithful while it hurts.$tk$, $tk$["21:83-84","38:41-44","4:163","6:84"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$shuayb$tk$, 'en', 13, $tk$Shuayb$tk$, $tk$شعيب$tk$, $tk$The prophet of honest trade$tk$, $tk$Shuayb, peace be upon him, was sent to the people of Madyan. The Qur'an calls him their brother, for he was one of them. It also mentions his preaching to the people of the Thicket; scholars differ on whether these were the same people as Madyan or a neighbouring group.

The people of Madyan were prosperous, but their trade was built on cheating. They gave less than was due when they measured and weighed, and they deprived people of what belonged to them. They also sat on the roads to threaten people and to turn the believers away from the path of Allah.

Shuayb called them first to worship Allah alone. Then he spoke to them about their daily business: give full measure and full weight with justice, do not cheat people of their goods, and do not spread corruption on earth after it has been set in order. He told them that what Allah leaves them as honest profit is better for them. He reminded them that they had once been few and that Allah had multiplied them.

Their answer was mocking. Did his prayer command him that they should give up what their fathers worshipped, or stop doing what they pleased with their own money? Shuayb answered with words that have remained famous: he did not want to do himself what he forbade them; he only wanted to put things right as far as he could, and his success depended on Allah alone, in whom he trusted.

He warned them not to let their opposition to him bring upon them a fate like that of the peoples of Nuh, Hud, Salih or Lut. He invited them to ask forgiveness, saying that his Lord is merciful and loving.

The leaders turned to threats. They said that they did not understand much of what he said, that they saw him as weak among them, and that without his family they would have stoned him. They threatened to expel him and the believers unless they returned to their religion.

The punishment came. The Qur'an speaks of an earthquake and a mighty blast that left them lifeless in their homes, and of the punishment of the day of the dark cloud. Shuayb and those who believed with him were saved by the mercy of Allah.$tk$, $tk$Faith shows in the way we buy, sell and work: give everyone their due. Practise what you ask of others.$tk$, $tk$["7:85-93","11:84-95","26:176-191","29:36-37"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$musa$tk$, 'en', 14, $tk$Musa (Moses)$tk$, $tk$موسى$tk$, $tk$The one to whom Allah spoke$tk$, $tk$Musa, peace be upon him, is the prophet most often mentioned in the Qur'an. He was born in Egypt at a time when Pharaoh oppressed the Children of Israel and had their newborn sons killed. Allah inspired the mother of Musa to place her baby in the river, promising to return him to her. Pharaoh's household picked him up, and Pharaoh's wife asked that he be spared. Since the baby refused every nurse, he was given back to his own mother to be fed.

As a young man, Musa struck an Egyptian who was fighting with a man of his people, and the man died. Musa had not meant to kill him; he regretted it at once and asked Allah for forgiveness. He fled to Madyan. There he helped two women to water their flock, then sat down in the shade and prayed that he was in need of any good Allah would send him. Their father welcomed him, gave him one of his daughters in marriage, and Musa worked for him for years.

On his way back with his family, he saw a fire near Mount Tur. There Allah spoke to him, made him His messenger and gave him signs: his staff turned into a snake and his hand shone white. Musa asked that his brother Harun be sent with him. Allah told them to go to Pharaoh and to speak to him gently.

Pharaoh refused to listen and summoned his magicians. But when they saw the staff of Musa swallow their tricks, they recognised the truth and declared their faith, in spite of Pharaoh's threats.

Allah then ordered Musa to leave by night with his people. Pharaoh chased them with his army as far as the sea. The people panicked, but Musa answered that his Lord was with him and would guide him. He struck the sea with his staff; it parted, and they crossed. Pharaoh and his soldiers were drowned.

Musa then went to meet his Lord for forty nights and received the Tablets. In his absence, some of his people began to worship a calf made from their jewellery. Musa came back angry and grieved, and called them to repent.

For the rest of his life, Musa led his people with patience, carrying the Torah, a guidance and a light.$tk$, $tk$Nobody is too weak to be chosen for something great, and no tyrant is too strong to fall. When you feel trapped, remember that Allah is with those who trust Him.$tk$, $tk$["28:3-35","20:9-79","26:52-68","7:142-145","2:51-54"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$harun$tk$, 'en', 15, $tk$Harun (Aaron)$tk$, $tk$هارون$tk$, $tk$The brother and helper of Musa$tk$, $tk$Harun, peace be upon him, is the brother of Musa, peace be upon him. In the Qur'an his story is always linked to that of Musa: he is the prophet who was given as a support to another prophet.

When Allah spoke to Musa and sent him to Pharaoh, Musa measured the weight of the mission. He asked his Lord to open his heart, to make his task easy and to loosen his tongue so that people would understand him. Then he asked for a helper from his own family: Harun, his brother, to strengthen him and to share his task. Musa said that Harun was more fluent than he was. Allah granted the request, and the Qur'an says that, out of His mercy, Allah gave Musa his brother Harun as a prophet.

The two brothers went to Pharaoh together. Allah had told them to speak to him gently, in the hope that he would remember or fear. They asked him to let the Children of Israel go and to stop tormenting them. When the magicians of Pharaoh saw the truth, they declared that they believed in the Lord of Harun and Musa.

After the crossing of the sea, Musa went to meet his Lord for forty nights. Before leaving, he asked Harun to take his place among the people, to act rightly and not to follow the way of those who spread corruption.

During this absence came the test of the calf. A man the Qur'an calls the Samiri led the people astray with a calf made from their jewellery. The Qur'an makes it clear that Harun did not take part in this. He told them that they were being tested by it, that their Lord was the Most Merciful, and that they should follow him and obey him. They refused to listen until Musa returned.

When Musa came back, angry and grieved, he seized his brother by the head. Harun answered him tenderly, calling him son of my mother. He explained that the people had overpowered him and had almost killed him, and that he had feared to divide the Children of Israel. Musa then asked Allah to forgive him and his brother and to admit them both into His mercy.

Elsewhere the Qur'an counts Harun among the prophets who were guided and rewarded for doing good.$tk$, $tk$Supporting someone else's mission is as noble as leading it. In a conflict with someone close, answer with gentleness and explain yourself calmly.$tk$, $tk$["20:29-36","28:34-35","20:90-94","7:150-151","19:53"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$dhul-kifl$tk$, 'en', 16, $tk$Dhul-Kifl$tk$, $tk$ذو الكفل$tk$, $tk$Counted among the patient and the best$tk$, $tk$Dhul-Kifl, peace be upon him, is one of the figures about whom the Qur'an tells us the least. He is mentioned only twice, each time in a single verse and alongside other prophets.

In Surah al-Anbiya, he is named with Ismail and Idris. The Qur'an says that all of them were among the patient, that Allah admitted them into His mercy, and that they were among the righteous. In Surah Sad, he is named with Ismail and Al-Yasa, and the Qur'an says that all of them were among the best.

That is everything the Qur'an says. It does not tell us where or when he lived, to which people he was sent, or what he did. No sound hadith adds any clear detail about his life either.

His name itself has been discussed. In Arabic, Dhul-Kifl means something like the one with the portion, or the one who took on a commitment. Some commentators have suggested that he was given this name because he took on a heavy duty and fulfilled it faithfully, but this is an explanation of the word, not an established account. Others have tried to identify him with figures known from other traditions; these suggestions are guesses, and none of them rests on firm evidence.

Most scholars consider that he was a prophet, because the Qur'an names him and praises him among prophets. A few scholars have held that he was a righteous man rather than a prophet. In both views, he is a servant whom Allah Himself has praised, and that is the greatest honour.

It is worth noticing which qualities the Qur'an chose to mention. The first is patience: the ability to keep doing what is right over a long time, without giving up when things are hard. The second is righteousness. The third is to be counted among the best, in the company of prophets like Ismail and Al-Yasa.

So it is better to say honestly that we do not know the details of his life than to fill the silence with legends. The Qur'an does not always teach through long stories. Sometimes a name and a word of praise are enough to remind us that Allah knows His faithful servants, including those whose story no one has written down.$tk$, $tk$You do not need to be famous to be precious in the sight of Allah. Patience and faithfulness to your commitments are noticed by Him, even when nobody else sees them.$tk$, $tk$["21:85-86","38:48"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$dawud$tk$, 'en', 17, $tk$Dawud (David)$tk$, $tk$داود$tk$, $tk$Prophet, king and devoted worshipper$tk$, $tk$Dawud, peace be upon him, appears in the Qur'an for the first time on a battlefield. The Children of Israel, led by their king Talut, faced the army of Jalut. The believers were few, but they asked Allah for patience and firmness. They defeated their enemies by the permission of Allah, and Dawud killed Jalut. Allah then gave him kingship and wisdom and taught him what He willed.

Dawud became both a prophet and a king. Allah gave him the Zabur, the Psalms. The Qur'an describes him as a servant of great strength who constantly turned back to his Lord. When he glorified Allah, the mountains and the birds repeated the praises with him, in the evening and at sunrise.

Allah also made iron soft for him and taught him to make coats of armour with well-measured links, to protect people in battle. Although he was a king, a hadith tells us that Dawud ate only from what he earned by the work of his own hands (Sahih al-Bukhari, no. 2072).

His kingdom was made strong, and he was given wisdom and sound judgement in speech. But the Qur'an also tells of a moment when he was tested. Two men in dispute came into his prayer chamber and asked him to judge between them. Dawud gave his answer, then understood that Allah was testing him. He asked his Lord for forgiveness, fell down in prostration and repented, and Allah forgave him. The Qur'an gives no further details about this test, and the stories that have been added to it are not worthy of a prophet.

After this, Allah addressed him directly: He had made him a successor on earth, so he had to judge between people with truth and not follow his own desires.

The Qur'an also mentions a case about a field damaged at night by the sheep of some people. Dawud and his son Sulayman both gave a judgement, and Allah gave Sulayman the better understanding of the case, while both of them had received wisdom and knowledge.

Dawud thus brings together things we often think of as separate: power and humility, the skill of a craftsman and the devotion of a worshipper.$tk$, $tk$Whatever responsibility you hold, be fair and do not let your own wishes decide for you. When you realise you were wrong, turn back to Allah straight away.$tk$, $tk$["2:251","38:17-26","21:78-80","34:10-11","17:55"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$sulayman$tk$, 'en', 18, $tk$Sulayman (Solomon)$tk$, $tk$سليمان$tk$, $tk$The king who answered every gift with gratitude$tk$, $tk$Sulayman, peace be upon him, was the son of Dawud, peace be upon him, and inherited from him prophethood and kingship. The Qur'an calls him an excellent servant who constantly turned back to Allah. He asked his Lord to forgive him and to grant him a kingdom such as no one after him would have.

Allah answered his prayer. He put the wind at his service, blowing by his command wherever he directed it. Jinn worked for him as builders and divers and made what he wanted. He was taught the language of the birds, and his army was made up of jinn, men and birds, lined up in ranks.

One day this army came to a valley of ants. An ant warned the others to go into their homes so that Sulayman and his soldiers would not crush them without noticing. Sulayman understood her and smiled. Instead of feeling proud, he asked Allah to help him be grateful for His favours and to do good deeds that please Him.

Another time, he noticed that the hoopoe was missing. The bird returned with news from Sheba: a queen ruled there, with a great throne, and she and her people bowed to the sun instead of Allah. Sulayman sent her a letter, which began with the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, inviting them to come to him in submission.

The queen consulted her advisers and chose caution. She sent a gift, which Sulayman refused: what Allah had given him was better. Before she arrived, her throne was brought to him in the blink of an eye by one who had knowledge of the Scripture. Seeing it in front of him, Sulayman said that this was a favour from his Lord, to test whether he would be grateful or ungrateful.

When the queen entered his palace, she took the floor of smooth glass for water. She understood that she had been mistaken about many things, and declared that she submitted, with Sulayman, to Allah, the Lord of the worlds.

The Qur'an also tells of his death. He died leaning on his staff, and the jinn kept on working, unaware of it, until a small creature of the earth ate through the staff and he fell. They then understood that they did not know the unseen.$tk$, $tk$The more you receive, the more grateful and humble you should be. Every ability is a test of what you will do with it.$tk$, $tk$["27:15-44","21:81-82","34:12-14","38:30-40"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$ilyas$tk$, 'en', 19, $tk$Ilyas (Elijah)$tk$, $tk$إلياس$tk$, $tk$The messenger who called his people away from Baal$tk$, $tk$Ilyas, peace be upon him, is mentioned in two passages of the Qur'an. The first, in Surah al-An'am, names him in a list of prophets, together with Zakariyya, Yahya and Isa, and says that all of them were among the righteous. The second, in Surah as-Saffat, gives the short account of his mission.

The Qur'an says that Ilyas was one of the messengers. He spoke to his people and asked them whether they would not fear Allah. They worshipped an idol called Baal. Ilyas asked them how they could call upon Baal and abandon the best of creators: Allah, their Lord and the Lord of their forefathers.

His argument was simple and clear. The one who created them and created their ancestors before them is the only one who deserves to be worshipped. An idol has created nothing, and to turn to it is to forget the One to whom they owe their existence.

The Qur'an then tells us that his people rejected him. Because of this they will be brought to account, except the sincere servants of Allah. This means that some people, even if they were few, did listen to him and remained faithful.

The passage ends with a tribute. Allah left for Ilyas an honourable mention among later generations and sent peace upon him. He says that this is how He rewards those who do good, and that Ilyas was one of His believing servants.

The Qur'an gives no other details. It does not name the people of Ilyas, the land where he lived or the time of his mission, and it does not describe how his life ended. Commentators generally place him among the prophets sent to the Children of Israel, and other traditions tell long stories about him, but those accounts are not confirmed by the Qur'an or by sound hadith. We therefore keep to what is certain.

What is certain is already rich in meaning. Ilyas stood up in front of his people and asked them a question that every generation can ask itself: what do we put in the place of our Creator? The idol may no longer be a statue; it can be anything that we obey or love more than Allah. Many centuries later, his name is still spoken with a greeting of peace.$tk$, $tk$Ask yourself honestly what takes the first place in your heart. Speaking the truth is worth it even if only a few people listen.$tk$, $tk$["37:123-132","6:85"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$al-yasa$tk$, 'en', 20, $tk$Al-Yasa (Elisha)$tk$, $tk$اليسع$tk$, $tk$Counted among the chosen and the best$tk$, $tk$Al-Yasa, peace be upon him, is a prophet whom the Qur'an mentions only twice, and each time very briefly. There is no story about him in the Qur'an: only his name, placed among other prophets, and words of praise.

The first mention is in Surah al-An'am. After speaking of Ibrahim, Allah lists many of the prophets He guided. Among them come Ismail, Al-Yasa, Yunus and Lut, and the Qur'an says that Allah favoured all of them above all people. A little further on, the passage tells the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ that these are the ones whom Allah guided, and that he should follow their guidance.

The second mention is in Surah Sad. Allah tells the Prophet ﷺ to remember Ismail, Al-Yasa and Dhul-Kifl, and says that all of them were among the best.

This is all that the Qur'an says about him. We are not told to which people he was sent, when he lived, what he said to his people or how they answered him. No sound hadith gives an account of his life. Commentators generally place him among the prophets of the Children of Israel, after Ilyas, and other traditions tell stories about him. Since these stories are not confirmed by the Qur'an or by sound hadith, we do not present them as facts.

It is better to accept this silence than to fill it. The Qur'an itself says elsewhere that there are messengers whose stories Allah has told and others whose stories He has not told. Believing in all the prophets does not require knowing the details of each life.

Still, the two verses teach us something. Al-Yasa is described as chosen, guided, favoured and among the best. These words are given by Allah Himself, who knows what is in every heart. And the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ is told to follow the guidance of these prophets, which means that the way of Al-Yasa was the same as the way of all the others: to worship Allah alone and to do good.

His name in the Qur'an is like a quiet sign. It reminds us that the chain of prophets is long, that each of them carried the same message, and that many people who did great good are known fully only to Allah.$tk$, $tk$Do good without waiting for your story to be told. Being among the best in the sight of Allah matters more than being known by people.$tk$, $tk$["6:86","38:48"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$yunus$tk$, 'en', 21, $tk$Yunus (Jonah)$tk$, $tk$يونس$tk$, $tk$The man of the fish$tk$, $tk$Yunus, peace be upon him, was sent as a messenger to a people who, according to the Qur'an, numbered a hundred thousand or more. The Qur'an also calls him Dhun-Nun, the man of the fish.

His story begins with a departure. Yunus left in anger. The Qur'an does not hide this: it says that he went off thinking that Allah would not put him in difficulty. Prophets are the best of people, and the Qur'an shows us how they return to Allah when they slip.

Yunus boarded a loaded ship. Lots were drawn on board, and he was among those who lost. He was cast into the sea, and a great fish swallowed him. The Qur'an says that at that moment he was to blame.

In the darkness, Yunus called upon his Lord. He declared that there is no god but Allah, glorified Him, and admitted that he had been among the wrongdoers. He made no excuse and blamed no one else. This short prayer is one of the most famous in the Qur'an.

Allah answered him and saved him from his distress, and the Qur'an adds that this is how He saves the believers. It also says that if Yunus had not been one of those who glorify Allah, he would have stayed in the belly of the fish until the Day of Resurrection.

He was cast onto an open shore, sick and weak, and Allah made a gourd plant grow over him. The Qur'an then says that he was sent to his people, that they believed, and that Allah let them enjoy life for a time. The Qur'an points out that the people of Yunus are a special case: they believed in time, and their faith protected them from a humiliating punishment.

The Qur'an does not name the city of Yunus and does not say how long he remained inside the fish, and we do not need to know.

Allah tells the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ to be patient and not to be like the companion of the fish when he cried out in distress. He also says that his Lord chose Yunus and placed him among the righteous. His story therefore carries both a warning against impatience and great hope.$tk$, $tk$Do not give up on a duty out of frustration. And if you have slipped, no darkness is too deep: admit your mistake and call on Allah.$tk$, $tk$["37:139-148","21:87-88","10:98","68:48-50"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$zakariyya$tk$, 'en', 22, $tk$Zakariyya (Zechariah)$tk$, $tk$زكريا$tk$, $tk$The servant who never despaired of his prayer$tk$, $tk$Zakariyya, peace be upon him, was a prophet among the Children of Israel. The Qur'an presents him first as the guardian of Maryam, the mother of Isa, peace be upon them. She had been dedicated to the service of Allah, and Zakariyya was the one entrusted with her care.

Each time he came to see her in the place where she prayed, he found food with her. He asked her where it came from. She answered that it was from Allah, who provides for whom He wills without measure.

This answer touched him deeply. Zakariyya was a very old man, and his wife had never been able to have children. Yet, seeing the generosity of Allah towards Maryam, he turned to his Lord there and then and asked for a good child.

The Qur'an reports this prayer with tenderness. Zakariyya called upon his Lord in a low voice. He said that his bones had become weak and that his hair had turned white, but that he had never been disappointed when he prayed to Him. He was worried about those who would come after him, and he asked for an heir who would be pleasing to Allah. He also said: my Lord, do not leave me alone, and You are the best of inheritors.

The answer came while he was standing in prayer. The angels announced to him a son named Yahya, a name that Allah had not given to anyone before. He would be noble, chaste and a prophet from among the righteous.

Zakariyya was amazed: how could he have a son at his age, with a wife who could not bear children? He was told that this is easy for Allah, who does what He wills. He asked for a sign. The sign was that he would not speak to people for three days, except by gestures, although he was in good health. He came out to his people and signalled to them to glorify Allah morning and evening.

The Qur'an says that Allah answered him, gave him Yahya and cured his wife. It then explains why this family was honoured: they hastened to do good deeds, they called upon Allah with hope and fear, and they were humble before Him.$tk$, $tk$Keep asking Allah with hope, whatever your age or situation. The good you see in others can be an invitation to ask for more yourself.$tk$, $tk$["3:37-41","19:2-11","21:89-90"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$yahya$tk$, 'en', 23, $tk$Yahya (John)$tk$, $tk$يحيى$tk$, $tk$Wise from childhood, gentle and pure$tk$, $tk$Yahya, peace be upon him, is the son of Zakariyya, peace be upon him. He was born in answer to the prayer of his elderly father, when nothing seemed to make it possible. Allah Himself chose his name, and the Qur'an says that no one had been given this name before him.

The Qur'an does not tell the events of his life as a story. It gives instead a portrait of his character, in a few verses of Surah Maryam, and this portrait is one of the most beautiful in the Book.

Allah commanded him to hold firmly to the Scripture, and gave him wisdom while he was still a child. Yahya did not wait to grow old to take his faith seriously: from his earliest years, he understood what Allah asked of him and held on to it with strength.

Allah also gave him tenderness from Himself and purity. He feared Allah. He was dutiful to his parents, and he was neither arrogant nor disobedient. In Surah Al Imran, the angels announce him to Zakariyya as one who would confirm a word from Allah, and as noble, chaste and a prophet from among the righteous. Commentators explain that the word from Allah refers to Isa, peace be upon him, whose mission Yahya confirmed.

Elsewhere the Qur'an names Yahya with Zakariyya, Isa and Ilyas, and says that all of them were among the righteous. In the hadith about the Night Journey, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ relates that he met Yahya and Isa together in the second heaven, and that they were cousins (Sahih al-Bukhari, no. 3887).

The portrait ends with a greeting that Allah gives him: peace upon him the day he was born, the day he dies and the day he is raised alive. These are the three moments when a human being is most vulnerable, and Yahya is promised safety in each of them.

The Qur'an does not describe how Yahya called his people or how he died. Accounts of his death exist in other sources, but they are not found in the Qur'an, and we do not present them as certain.

Yahya shows that strength and gentleness go together. He was firm with the Scripture and soft with people, serious in his faith and kind to his parents.$tk$, $tk$You are never too young to take your faith and your values seriously. Be firm about what is right and gentle with people, starting with your parents.$tk$, $tk$["19:12-15","3:39","21:90","6:85"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$isa$tk$, 'en', 24, $tk$Isa (Jesus)$tk$, $tk$عيسى$tk$, $tk$The Messiah, son of Maryam$tk$, $tk$Isa, peace be upon him, is one of the greatest messengers of Allah. The Qur'an calls him the Messiah, son of Maryam (Mary), and gives his mother a unique place: a whole surah bears her name.

Maryam was a pure and devout young woman. One day, when she had withdrawn from her family to worship, an angel appeared to her and announced that she would have a pure son. She was amazed: how could she have a child when no man had touched her? The angel answered that this is easy for Allah, who creates what He wills. When He decides a matter, He only says to it: Be, and it is.

Maryam gave birth alone, near the trunk of a palm tree, in pain and distress. She was comforted: a stream flowed beneath her, and fresh dates fell from the tree when she shook it.

When she returned to her people carrying the child, they accused her. She simply pointed to him. The newborn then spoke, by the permission of Allah. He said that he was the servant of Allah, that Allah had given him the Scripture and made him a prophet, had made him blessed wherever he was, and had enjoined on him prayer, charity and kindness to his mother.

Isa was sent as a messenger to the Children of Israel. He confirmed the Torah that came before him and received the Gospel. Allah supported him with clear signs: by the permission of Allah, he healed the blind and the leper, gave life to the dead, and shaped a bird from clay which became a living bird. The Qur'an repeats each time that these miracles happened by the permission of Allah.

His message was the same as that of all the prophets: Allah is my Lord and your Lord, so worship Him; that is the straight path. His disciples believed in him and declared that they submitted to Allah. At their request, Isa prayed for a table spread with food to be sent down from heaven as a sign.

The Qur'an also says that Isa announced a messenger who would come after him. When his enemies plotted against him, the Qur'an teaches that they neither killed him nor crucified him, although it appeared so to them, and that Allah raised him to Himself.

Muslims love and honour Isa and his mother.$tk$, $tk$Honour your mother and stand by the truth with gentleness. True greatness lies in being a faithful servant of Allah and in bringing good wherever you are.$tk$, $tk$["3:45-51","19:16-36","5:110-115","4:157-158","61:6"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$muhammad$tk$, 'en', 25, $tk$Muhammad ﷺ$tk$, $tk$محمد$tk$, $tk$The Seal of the Prophets, a mercy to the worlds$tk$, $tk$The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ was born in Mecca around the year 570 CE, in the tribe of Quraysh. His father died before his birth and his mother when he was a small child, so he was raised by his grandfather, then by his uncle Abu Talib. As a young man, he was so honest that people called him al-Amin, the trustworthy. He married Khadija, may Allah be pleased with her, a respected merchant of Mecca.

He used to withdraw to the cave of Hira, on a mountain near Mecca, to reflect and to worship. There, when he was about forty years old, the angel Jibril (Gabriel) brought him the first words of the Qur'an, commanding him to read in the name of his Lord who created. Khadija was the first to believe in him.

For thirteen years he called the people of Mecca to worship Allah alone, to be honest, to care for orphans and the poor, and to give up idols. A few people followed him, but the leaders of Quraysh opposed him, and the believers suffered insults, boycott and persecution. He answered with patience.

In 622, he emigrated to the city of Yathrib, which became Medina. This journey, the Hijra, marks the beginning of the Islamic calendar. The Qur'an recalls how, hidden in a cave with his companion Abu Bakr, he told him not to grieve, for Allah was with them.

In Medina he built a mosque, joined the emigrants from Mecca and the people of Medina in brotherhood, and organised the life of a community. The Muslims had to defend themselves several times, and the Prophet ﷺ also concluded a truce with Quraysh. When it was broken, he returned to Mecca in the year 8 of the Hijra, almost without fighting. He forgave those who had fought him for years and removed the idols from the Kaaba. People then entered the religion of Allah in crowds.

In the year 10, he performed his farewell pilgrimage. At Arafat, he spoke to a great crowd: he reminded them that life, property and honour are sacred, called for the good treatment of women, and declared that old blood feuds and usury were abolished. It was during this pilgrimage that the verse announcing that the religion had been perfected was revealed.

He died in Medina in the year 11 of the Hijra, 632 CE. The Qur'an calls him the Messenger of Allah and the last of the prophets, sent as a mercy to the worlds.$tk$, $tk$Be trustworthy before you speak about faith: character opens hearts. When you have the upper hand, choose mercy and forgiveness.$tk$, $tk$["96:1-5","9:40","110:1-3","5:3","33:40","21:107"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.premium_content (kind, lang, payload) values ('hajj', 'en', $tk${"umrah":{"title":"Umrah step by step","intro":"Umrah is the visit to the Sacred House in Makkah. It can be performed at almost any time of the year and takes only a few hours. It is made of four acts, always in the same order: entering ihram at the miqat, tawaf around the Kaaba, sa'y between Safa and Marwa, then shaving or trimming the hair.\n\nThis guide is a summary. It does not replace a qualified teacher, nor the instructions of the authorities on site, which you should always follow. Where the schools of law differ on a detail, we give the common practice: ask a scholar you trust for your case.","chapters":[{"id":"preparation","title":"Before you leave","body":"A good journey starts at home. Begin with your intention: you are travelling for Allah alone, not to be seen or praised. Repent sincerely, pay back your debts as far as you can, and ask forgiveness from the people you may have wronged. Pay for the trip with lawful money.\n\nThen learn the rites before you go. A pilgrim who knows what to do is calm, and can give his heart to worship instead of worrying. Read this guide several times and, if possible, attend a class with a teacher.\n\nFinally, prepare your body and your papers: walk regularly in the weeks before, check your visa, permit and vaccinations, and pack lightly. You will walk a lot, often in heat and in a crowd.","steps":["Make your intention sincere: this journey is for Allah alone.","Repent, settle your debts and ask forgiveness from those around you.","Learn the four acts of Umrah and their order.","Check your passport, visa, permit and required vaccinations.","Find out which miqat is on your route and when you will reach it.","Train by walking and pack light, comfortable things."],"duas":[]},{"id":"miqat-ihram","title":"The miqat and ihram","body":"The miqat is the boundary that a pilgrim must not cross without being in ihram. There are five: Dhul-Hulayfah for those coming from Madinah, al-Juhfah, Qarn al-Manazil, Yalamlam and Dhat Irq. On a plane, the crew usually announces the miqat: be ready before that moment.\n\nIt is recommended to take a full bath (ghusl) before ihram. Men wear two unstitched cloths, one around the waist and one over the shoulders; women wear their ordinary modest clothes. Then make the intention in your heart and say the talbiyah. Men say it aloud, women quietly.\n\nIhram is the state, not the cloth. In it you must not cut hair or nails, use perfume, hunt, contract a marriage or have marital relations. Men do not wear clothes tailored to the body or cover their head; women do not wear the face veil (niqab) or gloves (Sahih al-Bukhari 1838). Avoid quarrels too (Qur'an 2:197).","steps":["Before the miqat, trim your nails and take a bath (ghusl) if you can.","Men: put on the two ihram cloths. Women: keep ordinary modest clothing.","Men may perfume the body, not the cloths, before the intention; never after.","At the miqat, intend Umrah in your heart and say: Labbayka Allahumma 'umrah.","Repeat the talbiyah often on the way to Makkah.","From now on, respect everything that ihram forbids.","Stop the talbiyah when you begin the tawaf."],"duas":[{"ar":"لَبَّيْكَ اللَّهُمَّ لَبَّيْكَ، لَبَّيْكَ لاَ شَرِيكَ لَكَ لَبَّيْكَ، إِنَّ الْحَمْدَ وَالنِّعْمَةَ لَكَ وَالْمُلْكَ، لاَ شَرِيكَ لَكَ","tr":"Labbayka Allahumma labbayk, labbayka la sharika laka labbayk, inna al-hamda wa an-ni'mata laka wa al-mulk, la sharika lak","t":"Here I am, O Allah, here I am. Here I am, You have no partner, here I am. Truly all praise and all blessing are Yours, and all sovereignty. You have no partner.","source":"The talbiyah — Sahih al-Bukhari 1549; Sahih Muslim 1184"}]},{"id":"tawaf","title":"Tawaf around the Kaaba","body":"Tawaf is seven circuits around the Kaaba, keeping it on your left. You need to be in a state of ablution. Each circuit begins and ends at the Black Stone: face it, say the takbir, and kiss or touch it only if this is easy; otherwise simply point towards it. Many pilgrims say « Bismillah, Allahu akbar » there, as reported from the companion Ibn Umar. Walk around the semicircular wall (the Hijr), not through it.\n\nIn this arrival tawaf, men uncover the right shoulder (idtiba) and walk briskly with short steps in the first three circuits (raml). Women walk normally.\n\nThere is no fixed obligatory supplication for each circuit: you may make any dua, in any language, remember Allah or recite the Qur'an. Only between the Yemeni corner and the Black Stone is a precise supplication reported: Qur'an 2:201. Afterwards, pray two rak'at behind Maqam Ibrahim (Qur'an 2:125), then drink Zamzam water.","steps":["Make sure you have ablution. Men uncover the right shoulder (idtiba).","Start level with the Black Stone: face it, say the takbir, point to it if you cannot reach it.","Walk seven circuits with the Kaaba on your left, passing outside the Hijr.","Men walk briskly in the first three circuits (raml), then normally.","Touch the Yemeni corner if it is easy; between it and the Black Stone recite Qur'an 2:201.","During the circuits, make any dua you wish.","After the seventh circuit, men cover the shoulder again; pray two rak'at behind Maqam Ibrahim, or anywhere in the mosque if it is crowded.","Drink Zamzam water and make dua."],"duas":[{"ar":"اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ","tr":"Allahu akbar","t":"Allah is the Greatest.","source":"Takbir when level with the Black Stone — Sahih al-Bukhari 1613"},{"ar":"رَبَّنَآ ءَاتِنَا فِي ٱلدُّنۡيَا حَسَنَةࣰ وَفِي ٱلۡأٓخِرَةِ حَسَنَةࣰ وَقِنَا عَذَابَ ٱلنَّارِ","tr":"Rabbana atina fi ad-dunya hasanatan wa fi al-akhirati hasanatan wa qina 'adhab an-nar","t":"Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and protect us from the punishment of the Fire.","source":"Qur'an 2:201 — recited between the Yemeni corner and the Black Stone: Sunan Abi Dawud 1892 (hasan)"}]},{"id":"say","title":"Sa'y between Safa and Marwa","body":"After the tawaf, go to Safa. Sa'y is seven legs between the two hills: from Safa to Marwa is one leg, from Marwa back to Safa is the second, and so on. You therefore finish at Marwa.\n\nWhen approaching Safa for the first time, recite the opening of Qur'an 2:158, as the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ did (Sahih Muslim 1218). Climb a little, face the Kaaba, raise your hands, proclaim the greatness and oneness of Allah with the words below, then make your own dua. This is done three times. Do the same at Marwa on each arrival.\n\nBetween the two green lights, men quicken their pace; women walk normally. As in tawaf, no particular supplication is imposed for each leg. Ablution is recommended, and the elderly or ill may use a wheelchair.","steps":["Go to Safa and recite the opening of Qur'an 2:158 as you approach, this first time only.","On Safa, face the Kaaba, raise your hands, say the dhikr of Safa and make dua, three times.","Walk to Marwa; men quicken their pace between the green lights.","On Marwa, face the Kaaba and repeat the dhikr and dua.","Go back to Safa: that is the second leg.","Continue until the seventh leg, which ends at Marwa."],"duas":[{"ar":"إِنَّ ٱلصَّفَا وَٱلۡمَرۡوَةَ مِن شَعَآئِرِ ٱللَّهِ","tr":"Inna as-Safa wa al-Marwata min sha'a'iri Allah","t":"Indeed, Safa and Marwa are among the symbols of Allah.","source":"Qur'an 2:158 (opening words) — recited on approaching Safa: Sahih Muslim 1218"},{"ar":"لاَ إِلَهَ إِلاَّ اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لاَ شَرِيكَ لَهُ لَهُ الْمُلْكُ وَلَهُ الْحَمْدُ وَهُوَ عَلَى كُلِّ شَىْءٍ قَدِيرٌ لاَ إِلَهَ إِلاَّ اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ أَنْجَزَ وَعْدَهُ وَنَصَرَ عَبْدَهُ وَهَزَمَ الأَحْزَابَ وَحْدَهُ","tr":"La ilaha illa Allahu wahdahu la sharika lah, lahu al-mulku wa lahu al-hamd, wa huwa 'ala kulli shay'in qadir. La ilaha illa Allahu wahdah, anjaza wa'dah, wa nasara 'abdah, wa hazama al-ahzaba wahdah","t":"There is no god but Allah alone, He has no partner. His is the sovereignty and His is the praise, and He has power over all things. There is no god but Allah alone; He fulfilled His promise, gave victory to His servant and defeated the confederates alone.","source":"Dhikr on Safa and Marwa — Sahih Muslim 1218"}]},{"id":"halq-taqsir","title":"Shaving or trimming the hair","body":"The last act of Umrah is to shave the head (halq) or shorten the hair (taqsir). For men, shaving the whole head is better; if they trim instead, the common practice is to shorten the hair of the whole head, not just a few strands. Women never shave: they gather their hair and cut from its end about the length of a fingertip.\n\nA man who performs Umrah shortly before Hajj (tamattu) often chooses to trim now, so that hair remains to be shaved on the tenth day.\n\nUse the barbers outside the mosque rather than cutting hair in the sa'y area, and women should cut their hair in private. With this act the Umrah is complete, and all the restrictions of ihram come to an end.","steps":["Leave the sa'y area once the seventh leg is finished.","Men: shave the head, or shorten the hair of the whole head.","Women: cut a fingertip's length from the end of the hair, in private.","You are now out of ihram: your Umrah is complete."],"duas":[]},{"id":"after","title":"After the Umrah","body":"Your Umrah is finished: thank Allah, who allowed you to reach His House. Everything that ihram had forbidden is permitted again, and you may put on your usual clothes.\n\nMake good use of your remaining days in Makkah. Pray the five prayers in the Sacred Mosque as often as you can, perform extra tawaf when it is not too crowded, read the Qur'an and make dua for yourself, your family and all Muslims. Be gentle with other pilgrims and patient with tiredness and crowds.\n\nMany pilgrims also travel to Madinah to pray in the Prophet's Mosque. This visit is a blessed deed in itself, but it is not a part of Umrah. Back home, try to keep the good habits you gained there.","steps":["Thank Allah and change back into your usual clothes.","Pray in the Sacred Mosque as often as you can.","Perform extra tawaf, read the Qur'an and make much dua.","Keep the good habits of the journey once you are home."],"duas":[]}]},"hajj":{"title":"Hajj day by day","intro":"Hajj is the fifth pillar of Islam. It is an obligation once in a lifetime for every adult Muslim who is able to make the journey (Qur'an 3:97). Its main rites take place from the 8th to the 13th of Dhul-Hijjah, between Makkah, Mina, Arafat and Muzdalifah.\n\nThere are three ways to perform it. In tamattu, you perform a full Umrah during the months of Hajj, leave ihram, then enter ihram again for Hajj on the 8th. In qiran, you join Umrah and Hajj in a single ihram, without leaving it in between. In ifrad, you enter ihram for Hajj alone. A sacrifice is due for tamattu and qiran (Qur'an 2:196), not for ifrad. All three are valid; scholars differ on which is best, and most pilgrims coming from far away perform tamattu.\n\nThis guide is a summary. It does not replace a qualified teacher, nor the instructions of the authorities on site and of your group, which organise movements and timings for everyone's safety. Where the schools of law differ on a detail, we give the common practice: ask a scholar you trust for your case.","chapters":[{"id":"day-8-tarwiyah","title":"8 Dhul-Hijjah: the day of Tarwiyah, in Mina","body":"The Hajj begins on the 8th of Dhul-Hijjah, called the day of Tarwiyah. The pilgrim who is performing tamattu enters ihram again, this time for Hajj, from the place where he is staying in Makkah: bath, ihram garments, intention, then the talbiyah. Those performing qiran or ifrad are still in ihram since the miqat.\n\nEveryone then goes to Mina, a valley of tents a few kilometres from Makkah. There you pray Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib, Isha and then Fajr of the 9th, each at its own time; the four-rak'at prayers are shortened to two, without being combined.\n\nThis day and night in Mina are a sunnah and a time of preparation. Rest, repeat the talbiyah, read the Qur'an and get your heart ready for tomorrow, the greatest day of the Hajj.","steps":["Tamattu: take a bath and put on ihram where you are staying in Makkah.","Intend Hajj in your heart and say: Labbayka Allahumma hajjan.","Go to Mina with your group, repeating the talbiyah.","Pray Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib and Isha in Mina, each at its time, the four-rak'at prayers shortened.","Spend the night in Mina and pray Fajr there."],"duas":[{"ar":"لَبَّيْكَ اللَّهُمَّ لَبَّيْكَ، لَبَّيْكَ لاَ شَرِيكَ لَكَ لَبَّيْكَ، إِنَّ الْحَمْدَ وَالنِّعْمَةَ لَكَ وَالْمُلْكَ، لاَ شَرِيكَ لَكَ","tr":"Labbayka Allahumma labbayk, labbayka la sharika laka labbayk, inna al-hamda wa an-ni'mata laka wa al-mulk, la sharika lak","t":"Here I am, O Allah, here I am. Here I am, You have no partner, here I am. Truly all praise and all blessing are Yours, and all sovereignty. You have no partner.","source":"The talbiyah — Sahih al-Bukhari 1549; Sahih Muslim 1184"}]},{"id":"day-9-arafat","title":"9 Dhul-Hijjah: Arafat, then Muzdalifah","body":"Standing at Arafat is the pillar of Hajj: whoever misses it has missed the Hajj. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ taught that the Hajj is Arafat (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 889).\n\nAfter sunrise, leave Mina for Arafat and check that you are inside its boundaries, marked by signs. After the sermon, Dhuhr and Asr are prayed together and shortened, at the time of Dhuhr. Then comes the heart of the Hajj: from midday until sunset, face the qiblah and call upon Allah, ask forgiveness, and pray for those you love. There is no imposed text. Do not leave before sunset.\n\nAfter sunset, go calmly to Muzdalifah. There you pray Maghrib and Isha together, Isha shortened, then you rest for the night in the open air. You may gather there the small pebbles for the stoning.","steps":["After sunrise, go from Mina to Arafat, repeating the talbiyah.","Check that you are inside the boundaries of Arafat.","Pray Dhuhr and Asr combined and shortened at the time of Dhuhr.","Spend the afternoon in dua, dhikr and repentance, facing the qiblah.","Stay in Arafat until the sun has fully set.","Go calmly to Muzdalifah and pray Maghrib and Isha combined there.","Gather small pebbles, the size of a chickpea, then sleep.","Pray Fajr, make dua facing the qiblah, and leave for Mina before sunrise."],"duas":[]},{"id":"day-10-nahr","title":"10 Dhul-Hijjah: the day of sacrifice","body":"The 10th is the day of Eid and the busiest day of the Hajj. Four acts are performed, ideally in this order. First, in Mina, throw seven pebbles one by one at Jamrat al-Aqaba, the largest pillar, saying the takbir with each; the talbiyah stops here (Sahih al-Bukhari 1685). Second, the sacrifice, due for tamattu and qiran, often arranged through an official voucher. Third, men shave or trim and women cut a fingertip's length. Fourth, in Makkah, tawaf al-ifadah, a pillar of the Hajj, followed by the sa'y of Hajj for those who still owe it.\n\nAfter the stoning and the shaving, the restrictions of ihram are lifted, except marital relations, which become lawful after tawaf al-ifadah. The schools differ on the details: ask a scholar you trust for your case.\n\nIf the order changes, there is no harm: the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said so to those who asked that day (Sahih al-Bukhari 83).","steps":["In Mina, throw seven pebbles one by one at Jamrat al-Aqaba, saying Allahu akbar with each.","Stop the talbiyah when you begin the stoning.","Offer the sacrifice, or make sure it is done on your behalf (tamattu and qiran).","Men shave or trim the whole head; women cut a fingertip's length.","Change into ordinary clothes: all is permitted again except marital relations.","Go to Makkah for tawaf al-ifadah, without idtiba or raml, and pray two rak'at.","Perform the sa'y of Hajj if you still owe it.","Return to Mina to spend the night there."],"duas":[{"ar":"اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ","tr":"Allahu akbar","t":"Allah is the Greatest.","source":"Takbir with each pebble — Sahih al-Bukhari 1751"}]},{"id":"days-11-13-tashriq","title":"11–13 Dhul-Hijjah: the days of Tashriq","body":"The days of Tashriq are spent in Mina. They are days of remembering Allah (Qur'an 2:203), and the nights are spent there too.\n\nEach day, after midday, you stone the three jamarat in order: the small one, the middle one, then Jamrat al-Aqaba, with seven pebbles each, saying the takbir with every throw. After the first and after the second, step aside, face the qiblah and make a long dua with raised hands; after the third, leave without stopping (Sahih al-Bukhari 1751).\n\nYou may leave Mina on the 12th after the stoning, before sunset; whoever stays stones again on the 13th. Both are allowed (Qur'an 2:203). Follow the timetable given to your group. If you are weak or ill, or about the exact time of stoning, ask a scholar you trust for your case.","steps":["Spend the nights of the 11th and 12th in Mina.","Each day after midday, take 21 pebbles to the jamarat.","Stone the small jamrah with seven pebbles, then step aside and make dua.","Stone the middle jamrah with seven pebbles, then step aside and make dua.","Stone Jamrat al-Aqaba with seven pebbles and leave without stopping.","On the 12th, leave Mina before sunset if you wish to leave early.","Otherwise, stay the night and stone the three jamarat again on the 13th."],"duas":[]},{"id":"farewell-tawaf","title":"The farewell tawaf","body":"When all the rites are finished and you are about to leave Makkah, the last thing you do is the farewell tawaf (tawaf al-wada). People were commanded to make the House their last stop; only the woman in her menses was excused from it (Sahih al-Bukhari 1755; Sahih Muslim 1328).\n\nIt is an ordinary tawaf of seven circuits in everyday clothes, without idtiba or raml, followed by two rak'at. There is no sa'y after it. Do it once your luggage is ready and your departure is near; a short wait for transport or for your companions does no harm.\n\nLeave the Sacred Mosque with a grateful heart. Ask Allah to accept your Hajj, to forgive you, and to let you come back. Then try to return home a better person than the one who left.","steps":["Finish all your rites and prepare your luggage first.","Perform seven circuits around the Kaaba in ordinary clothes.","Pray two rak'at after the tawaf.","Make dua that Allah accepts your Hajj, then leave Makkah without delay.","A woman in her menses leaves without this tawaf."],"duas":[]}]},"mistakes":[{"title":"Pushing to kiss the Black Stone","body":"Kissing the Black Stone is a sunnah; harming a Muslim is forbidden. If the crowd is dense, simply point towards the Stone and say the takbir: your tawaf is complete and nobody gets hurt."},{"title":"Believing each circuit has its own required dua","body":"Some booklets give one supplication per circuit. Nothing of the kind is required: there is no fixed obligatory dua for the circuits of tawaf or the legs of sa'y. Speak to Allah with your own words, in your own language."},{"title":"Leaving Arafat before sunset","body":"The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ stayed at Arafat until the sun had set (Sahih Muslim 1218). Do not rush to the buses in the late afternoon: these are the most precious hours of the whole Hajj."},{"title":"Climbing Jabal ar-Rahmah thinking it is required","body":"The whole plain of Arafat is a place of standing. Climbing the hill is not part of the Hajj and brings no extra reward; the crush there is tiring and dangerous. Stay where you can pray in peace."},{"title":"Standing outside the boundaries of Arafat","body":"Some pilgrims settle just outside Arafat without noticing. Since the standing is the pillar of the Hajj, look for the boundary signs and ask your guide if you are in any doubt."},{"title":"Collecting pebbles that are too large","body":"The pebbles are small, about the size of a chickpea. Big stones, shoes or other objects are not part of the rite and can injure people. Stoning is an act of obedience to Allah, done calmly, not an outburst of anger."},{"title":"Photographing instead of worshipping","body":"A souvenir photo takes a few seconds; filming the whole tawaf or posting live from Arafat takes your heart away. Put the phone away during the rites and protect the sincerity of your intention."},{"title":"Neglecting the miqat when flying","body":"The plane crosses the miqat in the air. Put on the ihram cloths before boarding or early in the flight, and make the intention when the miqat is announced, or a little before. If you passed it without ihram, ask a scholar you trust for your case."},{"title":"Keeping the right shoulder uncovered all the time","body":"Idtiba is only for men and only during the arrival tawaf. Before it and after it, including during prayer and sa'y, both shoulders are covered."},{"title":"Counting a round trip as one leg of sa'y","body":"Safa to Marwa is one leg and Marwa to Safa is another. Sa'y is seven legs and ends at Marwa. Counting each round trip as one would mean walking fourteen legs."},{"title":"Insisting on praying right behind Maqam Ibrahim","body":"Praying in the path of those making tawaf blocks them and puts you at risk. When it is crowded, the two rak'at can be prayed anywhere in the mosque."},{"title":"Arguing and losing patience","body":"Heat, queues and tiredness test everyone. The Qur'an forbids obscenity, wrongdoing and quarrelling during Hajj (2:197). Give way, smile, forgive: good character is part of your pilgrimage."}],"checklist":[{"id":"passport","group":"documents","label":"Passport valid for at least six months"},{"id":"visa-permit","group":"documents","label":"Hajj or Umrah visa and official permit"},{"id":"tickets-bookings","group":"documents","label":"Flight tickets and accommodation bookings"},{"id":"id-copies","group":"documents","label":"Paper and digital copies of all documents"},{"id":"emergency-contacts","group":"documents","label":"Card with your group, hotel and emergency contacts"},{"id":"money-card","group":"documents","label":"Some local cash and a bank card"},{"id":"ihram-garments","group":"clothing","label":"Men: two sets of ihram cloths"},{"id":"modest-clothing","group":"clothing","label":"Women: loose, modest, comfortable clothing"},{"id":"sandals","group":"clothing","label":"Comfortable sandals already worn in"},{"id":"ihram-belt","group":"clothing","label":"Ihram belt or pouch for money and papers"},{"id":"small-bag","group":"clothing","label":"Small bag for shoes and pebbles"},{"id":"umbrella","group":"clothing","label":"Light-coloured umbrella against the sun"},{"id":"vaccinations","group":"health","label":"Required vaccinations and certificate"},{"id":"medication","group":"health","label":"Usual medication with prescription"},{"id":"unscented-toiletries","group":"health","label":"Unscented soap and toiletries for ihram"},{"id":"water-bottle","group":"health","label":"Reusable water bottle"},{"id":"masks-sanitizer","group":"health","label":"Face masks and unscented hand sanitiser"},{"id":"first-aid","group":"health","label":"Small first-aid kit with blister plasters"},{"id":"sincere-intention","group":"spiritual","label":"Renew your intention: for Allah alone"},{"id":"repentance-debts","group":"spiritual","label":"Repent, settle debts and ask forgiveness"},{"id":"learn-rites","group":"spiritual","label":"Learn the rites and their order"},{"id":"talbiyah-memorised","group":"spiritual","label":"Memorise the talbiyah"},{"id":"dua-list","group":"spiritual","label":"Write a list of personal duas for Arafat"},{"id":"quran-dhikr","group":"spiritual","label":"Pocket Qur'an or app for reading and dhikr"}]}$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (kind, lang) do update set payload = excluded.payload, updated_at = now();
insert into public.premium_content (kind, lang, payload) values ('quiz', 'en', $tk$[{"id":"q001","cat":"prophets","q":"According to the Qur'an, who was the first human being created by God?","options":["Nuh (Noah)","Adam","Ibrahim (Abraham)","Idris"],"answer":1,"explain":"Adam (peace be upon him) is the first human being and the father of humankind. The Qur'an tells of his creation in 2:30-37."},{"id":"q002","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him) built the ark by God's command?","options":["Musa (Moses)","Yunus (Jonah)","Nuh (Noah)","Hud"],"answer":2,"explain":"Nuh (peace be upon him) was ordered to build the ark before the flood, as told in the Qur'an (11:37)."},{"id":"q003","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him) was swallowed by a great fish?","options":["Ayyub (Job)","Yusuf (Joseph)","Ilyas (Elijah)","Yunus (Jonah)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Yunus (peace be upon him) was swallowed by a great fish, called on God from the darkness and was saved (37:139-144)."},{"id":"q004","cat":"prophets","q":"Which son helped Ibrahim (peace be upon him) raise the foundations of the Kaaba?","options":["Ishaq (Isaac)","Yaqub (Jacob)","Ismail (Ishmael)","Lut (Lot)"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Qur'an says that Ibrahim and Ismail (peace be upon them) raised the foundations of the House together (2:127)."},{"id":"q005","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him) was thrown into a well by his brothers when he was a child?","options":["Yahya (John)","Yusuf (Joseph)","Harun (Aaron)","Dawud (David)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Yusuf (peace be upon him) was thrown into a well by his jealous brothers. His story fills Surah Yusuf, the 12th surah (12:15)."},{"id":"q006","cat":"prophets","q":"To which prophet (peace be upon him) was the Tawrat (Torah) revealed?","options":["Isa (Jesus)","Dawud (David)","Ibrahim (Abraham)","Musa (Moses)"],"answer":3,"explain":"The Tawrat was given to Musa (peace be upon him), who led the Children of Israel out of Egypt."},{"id":"q007","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him) received the Zabur (Psalms)?","options":["Dawud (David)","Sulayman (Solomon)","Musa (Moses)","Zakariyya (Zechariah)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Qur'an states that God gave the Zabur to Dawud (peace be upon him) (4:163 and 17:55)."},{"id":"q008","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him) received the Injil (Gospel)?","options":["Isa (Jesus)","Yahya (John)","Musa (Moses)","Yunus (Jonah)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Injil was given to Isa, son of Maryam (peace be upon them), as the Qur'an says in 5:46."},{"id":"q009","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet was the brother of Musa (peace be upon him) and helped him in his mission?","options":["Yusha (Joshua)","Shuayb","Ilyas (Elijah)","Harun (Aaron)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Musa asked God to give him his brother Harun as a helper (20:29-32). Peace be upon them both."},{"id":"q010","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him), tested by a long hardship, is famous for his patience and for calling on his Lord as 'the Most Merciful of the merciful'?","options":["Ayyub (Job)","Harun (Aaron)","Lut (Lot)","Salih"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ayyub (peace be upon him) stayed patient through his hardship and called on his Lord, who relieved him (21:83-84)."},{"id":"q011","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him) was sent to the people of 'Ad?","options":["Salih","Shuayb","Hud","Lut (Lot)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Hud (peace be upon him) was sent to the people of 'Ad to call them to worship God alone (7:65)."},{"id":"q012","cat":"prophets","q":"The she-camel given as a sign to the people of Thamud is linked to which prophet (peace be upon him)?","options":["Hud","Salih","Nuh (Noah)","Yunus (Jonah)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Salih (peace be upon him) was sent to Thamud, and the she-camel was a sign from God for them (7:73)."},{"id":"q013","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him) was thrown into a fire by his people, and God made it cool and safe for him?","options":["Musa (Moses)","Ibrahim (Abraham)","Yahya (John)","Hud"],"answer":1,"explain":"When his people threw Ibrahim (peace be upon him) into the fire, God ordered it to be coolness and safety for him (21:68-69)."},{"id":"q014","cat":"prophets","q":"According to the Qur'an, which prophet (peace be upon him) understood the words of an ant warning the other ants?","options":["Yunus (Jonah)","Isa (Jesus)","Sulayman (Solomon)","Yusuf (Joseph)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Sulayman (peace be upon him) heard an ant warn the others as his army came near, and he smiled and thanked God (27:18-19)."},{"id":"q015","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him) was the father of Yahya (John)?","options":["Imran","Yaqub (Jacob)","Ayyub (Job)","Zakariyya (Zechariah)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Zakariyya (peace be upon him) prayed for a child in his old age and was given the good news of Yahya (19:7)."},{"id":"q016","cat":"seerah","q":"In which city was the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ born?","options":["Medina","Mecca","Ta'if","Jerusalem"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ was born in Mecca, in the tribe of Quraysh."},{"id":"q017","cat":"seerah","q":"Where did the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ receive the first revelation?","options":["In the cave of Thawr","In the cave of Hira","On Mount Uhud","On the plain of Arafat"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first revelation came in the cave of Hira, on the Mountain of Light near Mecca, where he used to retreat."},{"id":"q018","cat":"seerah","q":"Which angel brought the revelation to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ?","options":["Mikail (Michael)","Jibril (Gabriel)","Israfil","Malik"],"answer":1,"explain":"The angel Jibril (peace be upon him) brought the Qur'an to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ."},{"id":"q019","cat":"seerah","q":"What was the first word of the Qur'an revealed to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ?","options":["Qul (Say)","Qum (Arise)","Iqra (Read)","Sabbih (Glorify)"],"answer":2,"explain":"The first revelation begins with the word 'Iqra', meaning read or recite (96:1)."},{"id":"q020","cat":"seerah","q":"Who was the first wife of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ?","options":["Khadija bint Khuwaylid","Aisha bint Abi Bakr","Hafsa bint Umar","Sawda bint Zam'a"],"answer":0,"explain":"Khadija, a respected merchant of Mecca, was his first wife. He married no other woman while she was alive."},{"id":"q021","cat":"seerah","q":"In which year of the common era did the Hijra, the migration from Mecca to Medina, take place?","options":["622","570","610","632"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Hijra took place in 622 CE. That year later became year 1 of the Islamic calendar."},{"id":"q022","cat":"seerah","q":"What was the city of Medina called before the Hijra?","options":["Khaybar","Tabuk","Ta'if","Yathrib"],"answer":3,"explain":"The city was called Yathrib. After the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ settled there it became known as Medina, 'the City'."},{"id":"q023","cat":"seerah","q":"In which cave did the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ take shelter during the Hijra?","options":["The cave of Thawr","The cave of Hira","A cave on Mount Uhud","A cave on Mount Safa"],"answer":0,"explain":"He hid in the cave of Thawr with his companion Abu Bakr while Quraysh searched for them. The Qur'an mentions this moment in 9:40."},{"id":"q024","cat":"seerah","q":"In which year of the Hijra did the Battle of Badr take place?","options":["Year 2","Year 1","Year 5","Year 8"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Battle of Badr took place in Ramadan of the year 2 AH. It was the first major battle of the Muslims."},{"id":"q025","cat":"seerah","q":"What was the name of the grandfather who took care of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ after his mother died?","options":["Abu Lahab","Abu Sufyan","Abd al-Muttalib","Waraqa ibn Nawfal"],"answer":2,"explain":"His grandfather Abd al-Muttalib, a leader of Mecca, looked after him. After the grandfather's death, his uncle Abu Talib took him in."},{"id":"q026","cat":"seerah","q":"What was the name of the mother of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ?","options":["Halima as-Sa'diyya","Fatima bint Asad","Umm Ayman","Amina bint Wahb"],"answer":3,"explain":"His mother was Amina bint Wahb. She died when he was still a small child. Halima as-Sa'diyya was his wet nurse."},{"id":"q027","cat":"seerah","q":"To which tribe did the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ belong?","options":["Aws","Khazraj","Thaqif","Quraysh"],"answer":3,"explain":"He belonged to Quraysh, the tribe of Mecca, in the clan of Banu Hashim."},{"id":"q028","cat":"seerah","q":"What is the name of the truce agreed between the Muslims and Quraysh in the year 6 AH?","options":["The Pledge of Aqaba","The Charter of Medina","The Treaty of Hudaybiyya","The Pact of the Virtuous (Hilf al-Fudul)"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Treaty of Hudaybiyya was agreed in 6 AH near Mecca. It brought a period of peace during which Islam spread widely."},{"id":"q029","cat":"seerah","q":"In which year of the Hijra did the Conquest of Mecca take place?","options":["Year 2","Year 4","Year 8","Year 6"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Muslims entered Mecca in the year 8 AH (630 CE). The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ forgave its people."},{"id":"q030","cat":"seerah","q":"Before prophethood, what nickname did the people of Mecca give to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ?","options":["Al-Hakim (the Wise)","Al-Karim (the Generous)","Ash-Shuja (the Brave)","Al-Amin (the Trustworthy)"],"answer":3,"explain":"He was known as al-Amin, the Trustworthy, because of his honesty. People left their valuables in his care."},{"id":"q031","cat":"companions","q":"Who was the first muezzin of Islam?","options":["Salman al-Farisi","Anas ibn Malik","Bilal ibn Rabah","Zayd ibn Haritha"],"answer":2,"explain":"Bilal ibn Rabah, a freed slave of Abyssinian origin, was chosen by the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ to call the adhan."},{"id":"q032","cat":"companions","q":"Which companion travelled with the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ during the Hijra?","options":["Bilal ibn Rabah","Mus'ab ibn Umayr","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Abu Bakr"],"answer":3,"explain":"Abu Bakr was his companion on the journey from Mecca to Medina and stayed with him in the cave of Thawr."},{"id":"q033","cat":"companions","q":"Which companion suggested digging a trench to defend Medina?","options":["Bilal ibn Rabah","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari","Salman al-Farisi","Mus'ab ibn Umayr"],"answer":2,"explain":"Salman al-Farisi, who came from Persia, suggested the trench. This is why the battle of 5 AH is called the Battle of the Trench."},{"id":"q034","cat":"companions","q":"Which companion is known by the title 'Sword of God' (Sayf Allah)?","options":["Khalid ibn al-Walid","Bilal ibn Rabah","Zayd ibn Thabit","Anas ibn Malik"],"answer":0,"explain":"Khalid ibn al-Walid, a famous commander, is known as Sayf Allah, the Sword of God."},{"id":"q035","cat":"companions","q":"Who was the first person to believe in the message of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ?","options":["Umar ibn al-Khattab","Khadija bint Khuwaylid","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Abu Sufyan"],"answer":1,"explain":"His wife Khadija was the first to believe in him. She comforted and supported him from the very first revelation."},{"id":"q036","cat":"companions","q":"Under which caliph was the Hijri calendar adopted, with the year of the Hijra as its starting point?","options":["Umar ibn al-Khattab","Harun al-Rashid","Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan","Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Hijri calendar was set up during the caliphate of Umar ibn al-Khattab, a few years after the death of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ."},{"id":"q037","cat":"companions","q":"Which companion, a scribe of the revelation, was given the task of gathering the Qur'an into one written collection?","options":["Bilal ibn Rabah","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Salman al-Farisi","Zayd ibn Thabit"],"answer":3,"explain":"Zayd ibn Thabit, one of the scribes of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, gathered the written Qur'an into one collection during the caliphate of Abu Bakr."},{"id":"q038","cat":"companions","q":"Which companion was sent to Yathrib before the Hijra to teach Islam to its people?","options":["Mus'ab ibn Umayr","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Anas ibn Malik","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mus'ab ibn Umayr was sent to Yathrib after the first pledge of Aqaba. Many of its people accepted Islam through him."},{"id":"q039","cat":"companions","q":"In which battle was Hamza, the uncle of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, killed?","options":["Badr","The Trench","Hunayn","Uhud"],"answer":3,"explain":"Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib fell as a martyr at the Battle of Uhud, in the year 3 AH."},{"id":"q040","cat":"companions","q":"Who is remembered as the first martyr of Islam?","options":["Umm Ayman","Sumayya bint Khayyat","Halima as-Sa'diyya","Umm Salama"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sumayya bint Khayyat, the mother of Ammar ibn Yasir, was killed in Mecca for refusing to give up her faith."},{"id":"q041","cat":"companions","q":"Which companion spoke for the Muslim emigrants before the Negus, the king of Abyssinia?","options":["Bilal ibn Rabah","Ja'far ibn Abi Talib","Zayd ibn Thabit","Anas ibn Malik"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ja'far ibn Abi Talib explained Islam to the Negus, who then let the Muslims live in safety in his land."},{"id":"q042","cat":"companions","q":"Which daughter of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ married Ali ibn Abi Talib?","options":["Fatima","Zaynab","Ruqayya","Umm Kulthum"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fatima married Ali ibn Abi Talib in Medina. They were the parents of al-Hasan and al-Husayn."},{"id":"q043","cat":"companions","q":"What name is given to the people of Medina who welcomed and helped the emigrants from Mecca?","options":["The Muhajirun (Emigrants)","The Quraysh","The Tabi'un (Successors)","The Ansar (Helpers)"],"answer":3,"explain":"The Muslims of Medina are called the Ansar, the Helpers. Those who emigrated from Mecca are called the Muhajirun."},{"id":"q044","cat":"companions","q":"Who slept in the bed of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ on the night he left Mecca for the Hijra?","options":["Zayd ibn Haritha","Bilal ibn Rabah","Ali ibn Abi Talib","Anas ibn Malik"],"answer":2,"explain":"The young Ali ibn Abi Talib took his place in the bed that night and stayed behind to return the goods people had entrusted to him."},{"id":"q045","cat":"companions","q":"In whose house did the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ live in Medina while his mosque was being built?","options":["Salman al-Farisi","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Mus'ab ibn Umayr"],"answer":1,"explain":"He stayed in the house of Abu Ayyub al-Ansari until his mosque and his rooms were built."},{"id":"q046","cat":"quran","q":"How many surahs does the Qur'an contain?","options":["114","99","110","120"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Qur'an has 114 surahs, from al-Fatiha to an-Nas."},{"id":"q047","cat":"quran","q":"Which is the longest surah of the Qur'an?","options":["Al-Imran","An-Nisa","Ya-Sin","Al-Baqara"],"answer":3,"explain":"Al-Baqara, the 2nd surah, is the longest in the Qur'an."},{"id":"q048","cat":"quran","q":"Which is the shortest surah of the Qur'an?","options":["Al-Ikhlas","Al-Kawthar","Al-Falaq","Al-Fatiha"],"answer":1,"explain":"Al-Kawthar, the 108th surah, is the shortest: it has only three short verses."},{"id":"q049","cat":"quran","q":"Which surah opens the Qur'an?","options":["Al-Baqara","Al-Ikhlas","Al-Fatiha","Al-Alaq"],"answer":2,"explain":"Al-Fatiha, 'the Opening', is the first surah of the Qur'an and is recited in every prayer."},{"id":"q050","cat":"quran","q":"Which surah is placed last in the Qur'an?","options":["Al-Falaq","Al-Ikhlas","An-Nas","An-Nasr"],"answer":2,"explain":"An-Nas, the 114th surah, closes the Qur'an."},{"id":"q051","cat":"quran","q":"Into how many parts (juz') is the Qur'an divided?","options":["7","30","60","114"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Qur'an is divided into 30 juz', which makes it easy to read it all in one month."},{"id":"q052","cat":"quran","q":"In which month did the revelation of the Qur'an begin?","options":["Ramadan","Rajab","Muharram","Dhul-Hijja"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Qur'an says that it was sent down in the month of Ramadan (2:185)."},{"id":"q053","cat":"quran","q":"In which surah is Ayat al-Kursi, the Verse of the Throne, found?","options":["Al-Imran","Al-Baqara","Al-Kahf","Al-Mulk"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ayat al-Kursi is verse 255 of Surah al-Baqara (2:255)."},{"id":"q054","cat":"quran","q":"Which is the only surah that does not begin with the basmala?","options":["At-Tawba","Al-Anfal","Yunus","Al-Mulk"],"answer":0,"explain":"At-Tawba, the 9th surah, is the only one that does not open with 'In the name of God, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful'."},{"id":"q055","cat":"quran","q":"In which surah does the basmala appear twice?","options":["An-Nahl","Al-Ankabut","Saba","An-Naml"],"answer":3,"explain":"In Surah an-Naml the basmala comes at the opening and again in the letter of Sulayman, peace be upon him (27:30)."},{"id":"q056","cat":"quran","q":"Which surah of the Qur'an bears the name of the mother of Isa (peace be upon him)?","options":["An-Nisa","Maryam","Al-Imran","An-Nur"],"answer":1,"explain":"The 19th surah is named after Maryam, the mother of Isa (peace be upon them). She is the only woman mentioned by name in the Qur'an."},{"id":"q057","cat":"quran","q":"Over about how many years was the Qur'an revealed?","options":["About 23 years","About 3 years","About 10 years","About 40 years"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Qur'an was revealed little by little over about 23 years, first in Mecca and then in Medina."},{"id":"q058","cat":"quran","q":"How many verses does Surah al-Fatiha have?","options":["3","5","10","7"],"answer":3,"explain":"Al-Fatiha has seven verses. It is recited in every unit of the prayer."},{"id":"q059","cat":"quran","q":"What are the surahs revealed before the Hijra called?","options":["Medinan (Madani)","Muhkam","Meccan (Makki)","Mufassal"],"answer":2,"explain":"Surahs revealed before the Hijra are called Meccan, and those revealed after it are called Medinan."},{"id":"q060","cat":"quran","q":"Which is the longest verse of the Qur'an?","options":["Ayat al-Kursi (2:255)","The verse of light (24:35)","The verse of debt (2:282)","The first verse of al-Fatiha"],"answer":2,"explain":"The longest verse is verse 282 of al-Baqara. It teaches people to write down their debts and to call witnesses."},{"id":"q061","cat":"history","q":"Which city was the capital of the Umayyad caliphate?","options":["Baghdad","Damascus","Cairo","Medina"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Umayyads ruled from Damascus, in Syria, from 661 to 750."},{"id":"q062","cat":"history","q":"Which Abbasid caliph founded the city of Baghdad in 762?","options":["Harun al-Rashid","Al-Mansur","Al-Ma'mun","Al-Mu'tasim"],"answer":1,"explain":"Caliph al-Mansur founded Baghdad in 762 on the banks of the Tigris. It became one of the greatest cities in the world."},{"id":"q063","cat":"history","q":"Who captured and destroyed Baghdad in 1258?","options":["The Mongols","The Crusaders","The Seljuks","The Ottomans"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Mongol army led by Hulagu took Baghdad in 1258, which ended the Abbasid caliphate of Baghdad."},{"id":"q064","cat":"history","q":"Which Ottoman sultan took Constantinople in 1453?","options":["Mehmed II","Suleiman the Magnificent","Selim I","Osman I"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mehmed II, known as 'the Conqueror', took Constantinople in 1453. The city became the Ottoman capital, Istanbul."},{"id":"q065","cat":"history","q":"Who led the Muslim army that crossed from North Africa into the Iberian Peninsula in 711?","options":["Uqba ibn Nafi","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Salah ad-Din (Saladin)","Tariq ibn Ziyad"],"answer":3,"explain":"Tariq ibn Ziyad crossed the strait in 711. The rock of Gibraltar is named after him: Jabal Tariq, 'the mountain of Tariq'."},{"id":"q066","cat":"history","q":"Who took Jerusalem back from the Crusaders in 1187?","options":["Baybars","Nur ad-Din Zangi","Salah ad-Din (Saladin)","Mehmed II"],"answer":2,"explain":"Salah ad-Din al-Ayyubi retook Jerusalem in 1187, after his victory at the Battle of Hattin."},{"id":"q067","cat":"history","q":"Which city, the last Muslim kingdom of al-Andalus, fell in 1492?","options":["Cordoba","Granada","Seville","Toledo"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Nasrid kingdom of Granada fell in 1492, ending almost eight centuries of Muslim rule in parts of the Iberian Peninsula."},{"id":"q068","cat":"history","q":"Who defeated the Mongols at the Battle of Ain Jalut in 1260?","options":["The Mamluks of Egypt","The Ottomans","The Seljuks","The Almohads"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Mamluks of Egypt stopped the Mongol advance at Ain Jalut, in Palestine, in 1260."},{"id":"q069","cat":"history","q":"Which dynasty took over the caliphate in 750 and later made Baghdad its capital?","options":["The Ottomans","The Mamluks","The Almoravids","The Abbasids"],"answer":3,"explain":"The Abbasids replaced the Umayyads in 750 and founded Baghdad in 762."},{"id":"q070","cat":"history","q":"Which Umayyad prince fled to al-Andalus and founded the emirate of Cordoba in 756?","options":["Tariq ibn Ziyad","Harun al-Rashid","Salah ad-Din (Saladin)","Abd al-Rahman I"],"answer":3,"explain":"Abd al-Rahman I, a survivor of the Umayyad family, reached al-Andalus and founded the emirate of Cordoba in 756."},{"id":"q071","cat":"history","q":"The Ottoman dynasty takes its name from its founder. Who was he?","options":["Mehmed II","Timur (Tamerlane)","Alp Arslan","Osman I"],"answer":3,"explain":"Osman I founded the Ottoman state in Anatolia around 1300. The word 'Ottoman' comes from his name."},{"id":"q072","cat":"history","q":"Who founded the Mughal Empire in India in 1526?","options":["Akbar","Shah Jahan","Babur","Aurangzeb"],"answer":2,"explain":"Babur founded the Mughal Empire after his victory at the Battle of Panipat in 1526."},{"id":"q073","cat":"history","q":"Which 16th-century Ottoman sultan is known as 'the Magnificent' and 'the Lawgiver'?","options":["Mehmed II","Suleiman I","Osman I","Abdulhamid II"],"answer":1,"explain":"Suleiman I ruled from 1520 to 1566, when the Ottoman Empire was at its height. He is called 'Kanuni', the Lawgiver."},{"id":"q074","cat":"history","q":"Mansa Musa, famous for his pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324, ruled which empire?","options":["The Songhai Empire","The Ghana Empire","The Mali Empire","The Kanem Empire"],"answer":2,"explain":"Mansa Musa ruled the Mali Empire in West Africa. His pilgrimage of 1324 made the wealth of Mali famous far and wide."},{"id":"q075","cat":"history","q":"Which Seljuk sultan won the Battle of Manzikert against the Byzantines in 1071?","options":["Salah ad-Din (Saladin)","Baybars","Alp Arslan","Osman I"],"answer":2,"explain":"Alp Arslan defeated the Byzantine army at Manzikert in 1071, which opened Anatolia to the Turks."},{"id":"q076","cat":"sciences","q":"Which branch of mathematics takes its name from the book 'al-Jabr' by al-Khwarizmi?","options":["Geometry","Algebra","Trigonometry","Arithmetic"],"answer":1,"explain":"The word algebra comes from 'al-jabr', in the title of the book that al-Khwarizmi wrote in Baghdad in the 9th century."},{"id":"q077","cat":"sciences","q":"The word 'algorithm' comes from the Latin form of which scholar's name?","options":["Al-Khwarizmi","Al-Kindi","Al-Biruni","Al-Battani"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Algorithm' comes from 'Algoritmi', the Latin form of the name of the mathematician al-Khwarizmi."},{"id":"q078","cat":"sciences","q":"Who wrote the 'Canon of Medicine' (al-Qanun fi al-Tibb)?","options":["Al-Razi (Rhazes)","Al-Zahrawi","Ibn Sina (Avicenna)","Ibn al-Nafis"],"answer":2,"explain":"Ibn Sina wrote the Canon of Medicine in the 11th century. It was used to teach medicine for centuries, in the Muslim world and in Europe."},{"id":"q079","cat":"sciences","q":"Which scholar wrote the 'Book of Optics' and explained that we see because light enters the eye?","options":["Al-Khwarizmi","Ibn al-Haytham","Ibn Khaldun","Al-Idrisi"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ibn al-Haytham, who lived in the 10th and 11th centuries, wrote the Book of Optics (Kitab al-Manazir) and relied on careful experiments."},{"id":"q080","cat":"sciences","q":"The Andalusian scholar al-Zahrawi is famous as a pioneer in which field?","options":["Astronomy","Surgery","Geography","Algebra"],"answer":1,"explain":"Al-Zahrawi, who lived near Cordoba in the 10th century, described surgical operations and many instruments in his encyclopedia al-Tasrif."},{"id":"q081","cat":"sciences","q":"Which physician wrote a famous treatise that told smallpox apart from measles?","options":["Al-Kindi","Al-Farabi","Al-Idrisi","Al-Razi (Rhazes)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Al-Razi, a great physician of the 9th and 10th centuries, wrote a treatise on smallpox and measles that carefully describes both illnesses."},{"id":"q082","cat":"sciences","q":"Which 13th-century physician described how blood passes from the heart through the lungs?","options":["Al-Khwarizmi","Al-Biruni","Ibn al-Nafis","Ibn Khaldun"],"answer":2,"explain":"Ibn al-Nafis described the pulmonary circulation of the blood in the 13th century, long before it was known in Europe."},{"id":"q083","cat":"sciences","q":"Which 11th-century scholar calculated the radius of the Earth and wrote a major study of India?","options":["Ibn Rushd (Averroes)","Jabir ibn Hayyan","Al-Zahrawi","Al-Biruni"],"answer":3,"explain":"Al-Biruni worked out the radius of the Earth by an ingenious method, with a result close to the modern value, and wrote a detailed book about India."},{"id":"q084","cat":"sciences","q":"Which geographer drew a famous map of the world for King Roger II of Sicily in the 12th century?","options":["Ibn Battuta","Ibn Khaldun","Al-Idrisi","Al-Khwarizmi"],"answer":2,"explain":"Al-Idrisi completed his map of the world and the book that goes with it in 1154, at the court of Roger II in Sicily."},{"id":"q085","cat":"sciences","q":"Jabir ibn Hayyan is especially known for his work in which field?","options":["Optics","Surgery","Chemistry","Geography"],"answer":2,"explain":"Jabir ibn Hayyan is one of the great names of early chemistry (alchemy), with many works attributed to him."},{"id":"q086","cat":"sciences","q":"In which city was the House of Wisdom (Bayt al-Hikma), the famous Abbasid library linked to the translation movement?","options":["Baghdad","Cordoba","Cairo","Damascus"],"answer":0,"explain":"The House of Wisdom was in Abbasid Baghdad, where scholars translated and studied works from Greek, Persian and Indian traditions."},{"id":"q087","cat":"sciences","q":"Who wrote the 'Muqaddima', a famous work on history and society?","options":["Ibn Khaldun","Ibn Battuta","Ibn Rushd (Averroes)","Al-Ghazali"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ibn Khaldun, born in Tunis in 1332, wrote the Muqaddima. He is seen as a forerunner of sociology and of the modern study of history."},{"id":"q088","cat":"sciences","q":"What was the astrolabe, an instrument perfected by Muslim scholars, used for?","options":["Measuring temperature","Weighing gold and silver","Grinding grain","Observing the position of the sun and stars"],"answer":3,"explain":"The astrolabe was used to observe the sun and stars, which helped people tell the time and find directions."},{"id":"q089","cat":"sciences","q":"What is the engineer al-Jazari, who finished his famous book in 1206, known for?","options":["Ingenious machines and water clocks","A map of the world","A treatise on smallpox","A book on algebra"],"answer":0,"explain":"Al-Jazari wrote the Book of Knowledge of Ingenious Mechanical Devices, which describes water clocks, pumps and automata."},{"id":"q090","cat":"sciences","q":"By what Latin name is the Andalusian scholar Ibn Rushd known in Europe?","options":["Avicenna","Alhazen","Rhazes","Averroes"],"answer":3,"explain":"Ibn Rushd of Cordoba is known as Averroes. His commentaries on Aristotle were read widely in medieval Europe."},{"id":"q091","cat":"pillars","q":"How many pillars does Islam have?","options":["Three","Five","Four","Seven"],"answer":1,"explain":"Islam has five pillars: the testimony of faith, prayer, zakat, fasting in Ramadan and the pilgrimage to Mecca."},{"id":"q092","cat":"pillars","q":"What is the first pillar of Islam?","options":["The prayer (salat)","The fast (sawm)","The pilgrimage (hajj)","The testimony of faith (shahada)"],"answer":3,"explain":"The first pillar is the shahada: to bear witness that there is no god but God and that Muhammad ﷺ is His Messenger."},{"id":"q093","cat":"pillars","q":"How many obligatory prayers are there each day?","options":["Two","Three","Five","Seven"],"answer":2,"explain":"There are five obligatory prayers each day: Fajr, Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib and Isha."},{"id":"q094","cat":"pillars","q":"What is the name of the dawn prayer?","options":["Dhuhr","Fajr","Asr","Isha"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fajr is the dawn prayer, the first of the five daily prayers."},{"id":"q095","cat":"pillars","q":"How many obligatory units (rak'at) does the Fajr prayer have?","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"answer":1,"explain":"The obligatory Fajr prayer has two rak'at."},{"id":"q096","cat":"pillars","q":"Which month of the Islamic calendar is Ramadan?","options":["The ninth","The first","The seventh","The twelfth"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ramadan is the ninth month of the Hijri calendar. It is the month of fasting."},{"id":"q097","cat":"pillars","q":"In Ramadan, when does each day's fast begin?","options":["At dawn","At sunrise","At noon","At midnight"],"answer":0,"explain":"The fast begins at dawn, when the first light appears, as the Qur'an explains in 2:187."},{"id":"q098","cat":"pillars","q":"What is the meal that breaks the fast in the evening called?","options":["Suhur","Walima","Aqiqa","Iftar"],"answer":3,"explain":"Iftar is the meal that breaks the fast. Suhur is the meal taken before dawn."},{"id":"q099","cat":"pillars","q":"What is the usual rate of zakat on savings such as money, gold and silver?","options":["2.5%","1%","5%","15%"],"answer":0,"explain":"Zakat on savings is 2.5%, one fortieth, once the minimum amount has been held for a full lunar year."},{"id":"q100","cat":"pillars","q":"In which month does the Hajj take place?","options":["Ramadan","Dhul-Hijja","Muharram","Shawwal"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Hajj takes place in Dhul-Hijja, the twelfth and last month of the Hijri calendar."},{"id":"q101","cat":"pillars","q":"How many times do pilgrims go around the Kaaba in one tawaf?","options":["Three","Five","Seven","Nine"],"answer":2,"explain":"A tawaf is made of seven circuits around the Kaaba."},{"id":"q102","cat":"pillars","q":"Between which two hills do pilgrims walk during the sa'i?","options":["Uhud and Thawr","Arafat and Mina","Safa and Marwa","Hira and Thawr"],"answer":2,"explain":"Pilgrims walk seven times between Safa and Marwa, which the Qur'an calls symbols of God (2:158)."},{"id":"q103","cat":"pillars","q":"On which day of Dhul-Hijja do pilgrims stand at Arafat?","options":["The 9th","The 1st","The 10th","The 13th"],"answer":0,"explain":"The standing at Arafat is on the 9th of Dhul-Hijja. It is the heart of the Hajj."},{"id":"q104","cat":"pillars","q":"How many times in a lifetime is the Hajj required of a Muslim who is able to make it?","options":["Three times","Every year","Every five years","Once"],"answer":3,"explain":"The Hajj is required once in a lifetime for those who are able to make the journey (3:97)."},{"id":"q105","cat":"pillars","q":"Towards which city did the first Muslims pray before the qibla became the Kaaba?","options":["Medina","Damascus","Ta'if","Jerusalem"],"answer":3,"explain":"The first Muslims prayed towards Jerusalem. The qibla was then turned towards the Sacred Mosque in Mecca (2:144)."},{"id":"q106","cat":"civilisation","q":"Who founded the mosque of al-Qarawiyyin in Fez in the 9th century, which grew into a famous university?","options":["Rabia al-Adawiyya","Fatima al-Fihri","Zubayda bint Ja'far","Shajar al-Durr"],"answer":1,"explain":"According to tradition, Fatima al-Fihri founded al-Qarawiyyin in Fez, Morocco, in 859. It is often described as the oldest university still in activity."},{"id":"q107","cat":"civilisation","q":"In which city of al-Andalus is the great mosque famous for its forest of columns and its red and white arches?","options":["Seville","Cordoba","Granada","Toledo"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Great Mosque of Cordoba was begun in the 8th century, when Cordoba was the capital of al-Andalus."},{"id":"q108","cat":"civilisation","q":"In which city is the Alhambra palace?","options":["Granada","Cordoba","Marrakesh","Fez"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Alhambra stands in Granada, in the south of Spain. It was the palace of the Nasrid rulers."},{"id":"q109","cat":"civilisation","q":"Which Mughal emperor had the Taj Mahal built?","options":["Akbar","Babur","Aurangzeb","Shah Jahan"],"answer":3,"explain":"Shah Jahan had the Taj Mahal built in Agra in the 17th century, in memory of his wife Mumtaz Mahal."},{"id":"q110","cat":"civilisation","q":"In which city was the great traveller Ibn Battuta born?","options":["Fez","Tunis","Cairo","Tangier"],"answer":3,"explain":"Ibn Battuta was born in Tangier, Morocco, in 1304. He travelled for nearly thirty years across Africa, Asia and Europe."},{"id":"q111","cat":"civilisation","q":"Timbuktu, a famous centre of learning and manuscripts, is in which present-day country?","options":["Egypt","Morocco","Mali","Sudan"],"answer":2,"explain":"Timbuktu is in Mali. Its mosques and schools drew students and scholars from across West Africa."},{"id":"q112","cat":"civilisation","q":"In which city is al-Azhar, one of the oldest universities in the world?","options":["Baghdad","Cairo","Damascus","Kairouan"],"answer":1,"explain":"Al-Azhar was founded in Cairo in the 10th century and is still a major centre of learning today."},{"id":"q113","cat":"civilisation","q":"In which city is the Dome of the Rock?","options":["Jerusalem","Damascus","Medina","Cairo"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Dome of the Rock is in Jerusalem. It was completed at the end of the 7th century under the Umayyad caliph Abd al-Malik."},{"id":"q114","cat":"civilisation","q":"Which Ottoman architect designed the Süleymaniye Mosque in Istanbul?","options":["Al-Jazari","Ulugh Beg","Mimar Sinan","Ibn Khaldun"],"answer":2,"explain":"Mimar Sinan, chief architect of the Ottoman Empire in the 16th century, designed the Süleymaniye in Istanbul and the Selimiye in Edirne."},{"id":"q115","cat":"civilisation","q":"In the medieval Muslim world, what was a 'bimaristan'?","options":["A library","An observatory","A covered market","A hospital"],"answer":3,"explain":"A bimaristan was a hospital. Cities such as Baghdad, Damascus and Cairo had large hospitals where the sick were treated."},{"id":"q116","cat":"civilisation","q":"What is the first month of the Hijri calendar?","options":["Muharram","Ramadan","Rajab","Shawwal"],"answer":0,"explain":"Muharram is the first of the twelve months of the Hijri calendar, which follows the moon."},{"id":"q117","cat":"civilisation","q":"In a mosque, what is the mihrab?","options":["The pulpit used for the Friday sermon","The niche that shows the direction of Mecca","The tower used for the call to prayer","The fountain used for ablutions"],"answer":1,"explain":"The mihrab is a niche in the wall that shows the qibla, the direction of Mecca. The pulpit is called the minbar and the tower the minaret."},{"id":"q118","cat":"civilisation","q":"The Great Mosque of Kairouan, one of the oldest in Africa, is in which country?","options":["Tunisia","Morocco","Egypt","Syria"],"answer":0,"explain":"Kairouan is in Tunisia. Its Great Mosque goes back to the founding of the city by Uqba ibn Nafi in 670."},{"id":"q119","cat":"civilisation","q":"The Kufic script, one of the oldest styles of Arabic calligraphy, is named after which city?","options":["Cordoba","Konya","Kufa","Kabul"],"answer":2,"explain":"Kufic script takes its name from Kufa, in Iraq. Many early copies of the Qur'an were written in this angular style."},{"id":"q120","cat":"civilisation","q":"In Islamic civilisation, what is a 'waqf'?","options":["A trade caravan","A style of calligraphy","A charitable endowment","A tax on harvests"],"answer":2,"explain":"A waqf is property set aside for good, such as a school, a hospital or a fountain. Its income keeps serving people over time."}]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (kind, lang) do update set payload = excluded.payload, updated_at = now();
insert into public.premium_content (kind, lang, payload) values ('days', 'en', $tk${"new-year":{"detail":"The first day of Muharram opens the Hijri year. The Islamic calendar is lunar: each month begins with the new crescent, so the year is about eleven days shorter than the solar year.\n\nThe calendar was organised under the caliph Umar ibn al-Khattab, some years after the death of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. The Companions chose to count the years from the Hijra, the emigration of the Prophet ﷺ from Mecca to Medina in 622 CE, and to keep Muharram as the first month. The Hijra itself took place later in the year, in the month of Rabi' al-Awwal.\n\nMuharram is one of the four sacred months mentioned in the Qur'an (9:36). The Prophet ﷺ taught that the best fast after Ramadan is the fast of Allah's month, Muharram (Sahih Muslim 1163).\n\nNo special prayer or celebration is prescribed for this day. Many Muslims simply use it to remember the Hijra, look back on the past year and make good resolutions.","sources":["Qur'an 9:36","Sahih Muslim 1163"]},"tasua":{"detail":"Tasu'a is the ninth day of Muharram, the day before Ashura.\n\nWhen the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ fasted Ashura and told the Muslims to fast it, his Companions pointed out that Jews and Christians also honoured that day. He answered that the following year, if Allah willed, they would fast the ninth as well. He passed away before the next Muharram came (Sahih Muslim 1134).\n\nFor this reason, scholars recommend fasting the ninth together with the tenth. This gives the Muslim fast its own form while keeping the link with the day on which Allah saved Moses (peace be upon him).\n\nThis fast is voluntary. Someone who can only fast the tenth still fasts Ashura, and nobody who is ill, travelling or unable to fast should feel guilty. Muharram as a whole is a sacred month, and the Prophet ﷺ taught that fasting in it is the best fast after Ramadan (Sahih Muslim 1163).","sources":["Sahih Muslim 1134","Sahih Muslim 1163"]},"ashura":{"detail":"Ashura is the tenth day of Muharram. Its story goes back to Moses (peace be upon him): on this day Allah saved him and the Children of Israel from their enemy.\n\nWhen the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ came to Medina, he found the Jews fasting on this day in memory of that rescue. He said that the Muslims had more right to Moses, so he fasted it and told the Muslims to fast it (Sahih al-Bukhari 2004). When the fast of Ramadan became obligatory, fasting Ashura remained a voluntary, recommended act (Sahih al-Bukhari 2002).\n\nAsked about its reward, the Prophet ﷺ said that it expiates the sins of the past year (Sahih Muslim 1162). It is recommended to fast the ninth day along with it (Sahih Muslim 1134).","sources":["Sahih al-Bukhari 2004","Sahih al-Bukhari 2002","Sahih Muslim 1162","Sahih Muslim 1134"]},"mawlid":{"detail":"The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ was born in Mecca, in the clan of Banu Hashim of the tribe of Quraysh, in the year known as the Year of the Elephant, around 570 CE. His father Abdullah died before his birth, and his mother Amina died when he was a small child.\n\nIt is established that he was born on a Monday: asked about fasting on Mondays, he said it was the day he was born and the day revelation came to him (Sahih Muslim 1162). Most historians place his birth in the month of Rabi' al-Awwal. The 12th is the best-known date, but other days have been suggested and none is certain.\n\nMuslims differ about marking this day. Many gather to recall his life, send blessings upon him and share food. Others do not single the day out, because the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions did not do so. All agree on loving him and following his example every day; the Qur'an describes him as a mercy to the worlds (21:107).","sources":["Sahih Muslim 1162","Qur'an 21:107"]},"isra-miraj":{"detail":"Al-Isra is the night journey of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ from the Sacred Mosque in Mecca to al-Aqsa Mosque in Jerusalem. The Qur'an mentions it at the opening of Surah al-Isra (17:1). Al-Mi'raj is his ascension from there through the heavens, where he met earlier prophets (peace be upon them) and was shown great signs of his Lord; Surah an-Najm alludes to this (53:13-18).\n\nIt happened in Mecca, before the Hijra, in a period of hardship for the Prophet ﷺ. The most lasting gift of that night is the five daily prayers, which were made obligatory then (Sahih al-Bukhari 349).\n\nThe exact date is not known with certainty. The 27th of Rajab is the date most commonly remembered, but early historians mention other dates.\n\nNo particular prayer or fast is established for this night. Many Muslims use the occasion to retell the event and to remember the value of the prayer; others do not mark the date. Rajab is one of the four sacred months (9:36).","sources":["Qur'an 17:1","Qur'an 53:13-18","Sahih al-Bukhari 349","Qur'an 9:36"]},"mid-shaban":{"detail":"Sha'ban is the eighth month, just before Ramadan. Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) reported that she never saw the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ fast in any month, apart from Ramadan, as much as he did in Sha'ban (Sahih al-Bukhari 1969). It is a month for getting ready: voluntary fasts, making up fasts missed in the previous Ramadan, and reading the Qur'an.\n\nThe night of the 15th, in the middle of the month, is known in many countries by names such as Laylat al-Bara'a, Shab-e-Barat or Nisfu Sya'ban. Several reports speak of Allah's forgiveness on this night. Scholars differ about how strong these reports are, and so they differ about the practice: many Muslims spend part of the night in prayer and supplication and fast during the day; others treat it like any other night of Sha'ban and do not set aside special worship for it.\n\nWhat is agreed is that asking for forgiveness, mending relations with others and voluntary fasting are good throughout Sha'ban, the doorway to Ramadan.","sources":["Sahih al-Bukhari 1969"]},"ramadan-start":{"detail":"Ramadan is the ninth month of the Hijri year, the month in which the Qur'an was sent down as guidance for people (2:185). Fasting during it is one of the five pillars of Islam (Sahih al-Bukhari 8). It was prescribed in Medina, in the second year after the Hijra.\n\nThe month begins when the new crescent is sighted; if it cannot be seen, Sha'ban is completed as thirty days (Sahih al-Bukhari 1909). This is why the start may differ by a day from one country to another.\n\nFrom dawn to sunset, those who fast give up food, drink and marital relations. The sick and travellers are exempt and make up the days later, and those who cannot fast at all feed a poor person instead (2:184-185). The Qur'an gives the aim of the fast: to grow in God-consciousness (2:183).\n\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said that when Ramadan begins, the gates of heaven are opened, the gates of Hell are closed and the devils are chained (Sahih al-Bukhari 1899). It is a month of prayer, Qur'an and generosity.","sources":["Qur'an 2:183-185","Sahih al-Bukhari 8","Sahih al-Bukhari 1909","Sahih al-Bukhari 1899"]},"last-ten-nights":{"detail":"The last ten nights of Ramadan begin on the evening before the 21st day and run to the end of the month, which has 29 or 30 days. They are the most precious part of Ramadan, because Laylat al-Qadr is among them.\n\nAisha (may Allah be pleased with her) reported that when these ten began, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ would tighten his belt, spend the night in prayer and wake his family (Sahih al-Bukhari 2024). He strove in worship in these nights more than at any other time (Sahih Muslim 1175).\n\nHe also practised i'tikaf, staying in the mosque to devote himself to worship, during the last ten days of Ramadan until he died (Sahih al-Bukhari 2026).\n\nWhat is recommended: night prayer, reading the Qur'an, plenty of supplication, asking forgiveness and charity. Those who cannot stay up for long can still pray a little each night, so that they do not miss the night that is better than a thousand months (97:3). Zakat al-fitr is also prepared in these days, before the Eid prayer.","sources":["Sahih al-Bukhari 2024","Sahih Muslim 1175","Sahih al-Bukhari 2026","Qur'an 97:3"]},"laylat-al-qadr":{"detail":"Laylat al-Qadr, the Night of Decree, is the night in which the Qur'an was sent down. A whole surah is devoted to it: it is better than a thousand months, the angels and the Spirit descend in it by their Lord's permission, and it is peace until the rising of dawn (97:1-5). The Qur'an also calls it a blessed night (44:3).\n\nIts exact date has been kept hidden. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ told the believers to look for it in the odd nights of the last ten of Ramadan (Sahih al-Bukhari 2017): the 21st, 23rd, 25th, 27th or 29th. The 27th is the night most often mentioned, and many communities gather on it, but it is not certain, and scholars hold different views. The wisest course is to make an effort on all ten nights.\n\nThe Prophet ﷺ said that whoever stands in prayer on Laylat al-Qadr with faith and hoping for reward will have his past sins forgiven (Sahih al-Bukhari 2014). Recommended acts are night prayer, reciting the Qur'an, supplication and asking Allah for pardon.","sources":["Qur'an 97:1-5","Qur'an 44:3","Sahih al-Bukhari 2017","Sahih al-Bukhari 2014"]},"eid-al-fitr":{"detail":"Eid al-Fitr, the Festival of Breaking the Fast, falls on the first day of Shawwal and closes the month of Ramadan. The Qur'an asks the believers to complete the number of days and to glorify Allah for having guided them (2:185).\n\nBefore the Eid prayer, zakat al-fitr is given: a small charity of staple food, due for every member of the household, so that the poor can share in the day. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ ordered it to be paid before people go out to the prayer (Sahih al-Bukhari 1503).\n\nIn the morning, Muslims say the takbir, wear their best clothes and gather for the Eid prayer, which is followed by a sermon (Sahih al-Bukhari 956). The Prophet ﷺ would not leave for the prayer on this day until he had eaten some dates (Sahih al-Bukhari 953).\n\nFasting on the day of Eid is not permitted (Sahih al-Bukhari 1991). It is a day of thanks, family visits and kindness to neighbours. Voluntary fasts, such as the six days of Shawwal, can start from the second day.","sources":["Qur'an 2:185","Sahih al-Bukhari 1503","Sahih al-Bukhari 956","Sahih al-Bukhari 953","Sahih al-Bukhari 1991"]},"six-shawwal":{"detail":"Shawwal is the month that follows Ramadan. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said that whoever fasts Ramadan and then follows it with six days of Shawwal, it is as if he had fasted the whole year (Sahih Muslim 1164).\n\nScholars explain this with the rule that a good deed is rewarded ten times over (6:160): the month of Ramadan counts as ten months, and six more days count as two months, which makes a full year.\n\nAny six days of the month count. The app shows the 2nd to the 7th only as a convenient range: the days may be fasted one after another or spread out, at the beginning, middle or end of Shawwal. The only day excluded is the 1st, the day of Eid, when fasting is not permitted.\n\nThis fast is voluntary, not an obligation. Someone who still has days of Ramadan to make up should plan for them too: many scholars advise making them up first, while others allow the six days before. It is a gentle way to keep the good habits of Ramadan alive.","sources":["Sahih Muslim 1164","Qur'an 6:160"]},"dhul-hijja-nine":{"detail":"Dhul-Hijja is the twelfth month, the month of the Hajj. Its first ten days are among the best days of the year: the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said that no good deeds are better than those done in these days (Sahih al-Bukhari 969). Many commentators also understand the ten nights by which Allah swears in the Qur'an (89:2) as referring to them.\n\nSince fasting is one of the best deeds, it is recommended to fast during the first nine days, as much as one can. The ninth, the Day of Arafat, is the most emphasised. The tenth is Eid al-Adha, when fasting is not permitted.\n\nOther recommended acts are to repeat the takbir, to praise Allah often, to pray, to give charity and to read the Qur'an. The Qur'an speaks of mentioning Allah's name on known days (22:28).\n\nWhoever intends to offer a sacrifice is told not to cut his hair or nails from the start of the month until the sacrifice is done (Sahih Muslim 1977). During these days, pilgrims from all over the world gather in Mecca.","sources":["Sahih al-Bukhari 969","Qur'an 89:2","Qur'an 22:28","Sahih Muslim 1977"]},"arafat":{"detail":"The ninth of Dhul-Hijja is the Day of Arafat, the heart of the Hajj. From midday until sunset the pilgrims stand on the plain of Arafat, near Mecca, in prayer and supplication. Standing at Arafat is the essential pillar of the pilgrimage.\n\nIt was on this day, a Friday, during the Farewell Pilgrimage of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, that the verse announcing that Allah had perfected the religion was revealed (5:3), as Umar ibn al-Khattab reported (Sahih al-Bukhari 45).\n\nThe Prophet ﷺ said that there is no day on which Allah frees more servants from the Fire than the Day of Arafat (Sahih Muslim 1348).\n\nFor those who are not on Hajj, fasting this day is strongly recommended: the Prophet ﷺ said that it expiates the sins of the previous year and of the coming year (Sahih Muslim 1162). The pilgrims themselves are not asked to fast; the Prophet ﷺ did not fast while standing at Arafat (Sahih al-Bukhari 1988).\n\nIt is a day for supplication, remembrance of Allah and seeking forgiveness, wherever one is.","sources":["Qur'an 5:3","Sahih al-Bukhari 45","Sahih Muslim 1348","Sahih Muslim 1162","Sahih al-Bukhari 1988"]},"eid-al-adha":{"detail":"Eid al-Adha, the Festival of Sacrifice, falls on the tenth of Dhul-Hijja, the day after Arafat. It coincides with the main rites of the Hajj at Mina and Mecca.\n\nIt recalls the trial of Abraham (peace be upon him). He saw in a dream that he was sacrificing his son; both submitted to Allah's command, and Allah ransomed the son with a great sacrifice (37:102-107).\n\nThe day begins with the Eid prayer, followed by a sermon (Sahih al-Bukhari 956). After the prayer, those who are able sacrifice a sheep, a goat, a cow or a camel. The meat is eaten by the family, offered to relatives and neighbours and given to the poor. The Qur'an reminds believers that neither the meat nor the blood reaches Allah; what reaches Him is their piety (22:37).\n\nFasting is not permitted on this day (Sahih al-Bukhari 1991). Muslims say the takbir, wear their best clothes, visit one another and share food. The sacrifice may also be made on the days that follow.","sources":["Qur'an 37:102-107","Sahih al-Bukhari 956","Qur'an 22:37","Sahih al-Bukhari 1991"]},"tashriq":{"detail":"The days of Tashriq are the 11th, 12th and 13th of Dhul-Hijja, the three days after Eid al-Adha. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ described them as days of eating, drinking and remembrance of Allah (Sahih Muslim 1141).\n\nDuring these days the pilgrims stay at Mina and throw pebbles at the three pillars called the jamarat each day. The Qur'an tells them to remember Allah during these numbered days, and says that there is no sin on whoever hurries to leave after two days, nor on whoever stays longer (2:203). So pilgrims may leave on the 12th or stay until the 13th.\n\nFasting on these days is not permitted. The only exception reported is for pilgrims who owe a sacrificial offering and cannot find one (Sahih al-Bukhari 1997).\n\nFor everyone else, these are days of joy and gratitude: sharing the meat of the sacrifice, visiting relatives and saying the takbir, especially after the daily prayers. The Eid sacrifice may still be offered in these days, with details that vary between the schools of law.","sources":["Sahih Muslim 1141","Qur'an 2:203","Sahih al-Bukhari 1997"]}}$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (kind, lang) do update set payload = excluded.payload, updated_at = now();

-- ===== 10-premium-ar =====
-- Contenu Premium — ar
-- Fichier GÉNÉRÉ par scripts/build-seed.mjs : ne pas modifier à la main. Idempotent (peut être relancé).

insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$adam$tk$, 'ar', 1, $tk$آدم$tk$, $tk$آدم$tk$, $tk$أول البشر وأول الأنبياء$tk$, $tk$آدم عليه السلام هو أول البشر وأول الأنبياء. ويخبرنا القرآن أن الله أعلم الملائكة أنه سيجعل في الأرض خليفة يُستخلَف فيها. فتساءلت الملائكة عن مخلوق قد يفسد فيها ويسفك الدماء. فأجابهم الله بأنه يعلم ما لا يعلمون.

خلق الله آدم من طين وعلَّمه أسماء الأشياء كلها. ولما طُلب من الملائكة أن يذكروا هذه الأسماء، أقرُّوا بأنهم لا يعلمون إلا ما علَّمهم الله. ثم أخبر آدم بها. فكان العلم أول تكريم ناله الإنسان.

وأمر الله الملائكة أن يسجدوا لآدم. فأطاعوا جميعًا إلا إبليس، فقد أبى تكبُّرًا، وزعم أنه، وقد خُلق من نار، خير من مخلوق من طين. فطُرد، وأقسم أن يسعى في إضلال بني آدم.

وأُسكن آدم وزوجه الجنة. وكان لهما أن يأكلا من كل شيء كما يشاءان، إلا من شجرة واحدة. فوسوس لهما الشيطان، وأقسم لهما أنه ناصح مخلص، ووعدهما بالخلود. فأكلا كلاهما من الشجرة. والقرآن لا يجعل اللوم على الزوجة وحدها: بل يذكر أن الاثنين زلَّا. فانكشفت لهما عورتاهما، وأخذا يستتران بورق الجنة.

وما جاء بعد ذلك هو قلب القصة. فآدم وزوجه لم يجادلا ولم يعتذرا بالأعذار، كما فعل إبليس. بل توجَّها إلى ربهما، واعترفا بأنهما ظلما نفسيهما، وسألاه المغفرة والرحمة. وتلقَّى آدم من ربه كلمات، فتاب الله عليه. ويذكر القرآن أن ربه اصطفاه بعد ذلك وتاب عليه وهداه.

وأُهبط آدم وزوجه إلى الأرض، حيث يحيا البشر ويموتون ثم يُبعثون. ووعد الله أن يأتيهم منه هدى، وأن من اتبعه فلا خوف عليه ولا حزن.

والقرآن لا يذكر أين كانت الجنة، ولا أي شجرة كانت، ولا كم عاش آدم، ولا حاجة إلى التخمين. أما ما يعلِّمه فهو أن البشر جميعًا من أب واحد وأم واحدة، وأن باب التوبة مفتوح منذ اليوم الأول.$tk$, $tk$كل إنسان يخطئ، والمهم أن يعترف بخطئه بلا أعذار وأن يسارع بالرجوع إلى الله. والكِبر يغلق الباب الذي يفتحه التواضع.$tk$, $tk$["2:30-39","7:11-25","20:115-123"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$idris$tk$, 'ar', 2, $tk$إدريس$tk$, $tk$إدريس$tk$, $tk$صدِّيق رُفع مكانًا عليًّا$tk$, $tk$إدريس عليه السلام من الأنبياء الذين لم يذكر القرآن عنهم إلا القليل جدًّا. فقد ذُكر باسمه في موضعين فقط، وفي كل مرة بكلمات قليلة.

ففي سورة مريم، بعد قصص عدد من الأنبياء، يأمر الله النبي محمدًا ﷺ أن يذكر إدريس في الكتاب. ويوصف بأنه كان صدِّيقًا نبيًّا، ويضيف القرآن أن الله رفعه إلى مكان عليٍّ. وفي سورة الأنبياء، يُذكر مع إسماعيل وذي الكفل: فالثلاثة كانوا من الصابرين، وأدخلهم الله في رحمته، وكانوا من الصالحين.

هذا كل ما يخبرنا به القرآن. فهو لا يذكر متى عاش إدريس، ولا إلى أي قوم أُرسل، ولا ما جرى في أثناء رسالته. وقد فسَّر العلماء المكان العليَّ بأوجه مختلفة: فمنهم من يفهمه على أنه رفعة المنزلة والشرف عند الله، ومنهم من يفهمه على أنه مكان مرتفع في السماوات. وفي الحديث الصحيح عن الإسراء والمعراج، يخبر النبي محمد ﷺ أنه لقي إدريس في السماء الرابعة، وأن إدريس رحَّب به (صحيح البخاري، رقم 3887).

وتضيف كتب متأخرة كثيرة تفاصيل: أنه أول من خطَّ بالقلم، أو خاط الثياب، أو نظر في النجوم، أو أنه هو نفسه شخصية معروفة في كتب أقدم. وهذه الأخبار لا تأتي من القرآن ولا من الحديث الصحيح. فلا يصح أن تُعرض على أنها حقائق ثابتة، والأحكم تركها جانبًا.

وما يبقى قصير لكنه ثمين. فمن حياة كاملة، حفظ القرآن ثلاث صفات: الصدق والصبر والصلاح. والكلمة العربية التي وُصف بها، صدِّيق، تدل على من بلغ الغاية في الصدق، فوافق فعلُه قولَه، ووافق قولُه إيمانَه. ولم يقترن باسمه خبر عن سلطان أو مال أو شهرة، وإنما حُفظت أخلاقه وحدها.

والآيات القليلة عن إدريس تذكِّرنا أيضًا بأن قدر الإنسان عند الله لا يتوقف على مقدار ما يعرفه الناس عنه. فقد يُكرَّم نبي في القرآن في سطرين، ويكفي هذان السطران ليكون قدوة.$tk$, $tk$كن صادقًا صبورًا حتى لو لم ينتبه إليك أحد: فالأخلاق عند الله أثقل وزنًا من الشهرة.$tk$, $tk$["19:56-57","21:85-86"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$nuh$tk$, 'ar', 3, $tk$نوح$tk$, $tk$نوح$tk$, $tk$الداعي الذي لا يكلُّ وصانع السفينة$tk$, $tk$أُرسل نوح عليه السلام إلى قوم وقعوا في عبادة الأصنام. والقرآن يذكر حتى أسماء هذه الأصنام: ودّ وسُواع ويغوث ويعوق ونسر. فجاءهم نوح برسالة بسيطة: اعبدوا الله وحده، واستغفروه، يغفر لكم.

ويذكر القرآن أن نوحًا لبث في قومه ألف سنة إلا خمسين. دعاهم ليلًا ونهارًا، جهرًا وسرًّا. وذكَّرهم بأن الله غفَّار، وأنه سيرسل عليهم المطر غزيرًا. لكن كلما زادهم دعوة زادوا إعراضًا. فكانوا يضعون أصابعهم في آذانهم ويتغطَّون بثيابهم لئلا يسمعوه.

واحتقره كبراء قومه. وقالوا إنه ليس إلا بشرًا مثلهم، وإنه لم يتبعه إلا أراذل الناس. فأجابهم نوح بأنه لا يسألهم مالًا، وأن أجره على الله وحده، وأنه لن يطرد الذين آمنوا أبدًا.

ولما أُوحي إلى نوح أنه لن يؤمن من قومه أحد غير من آمن، أمره الله أن يصنع السفينة برعايته. وكان الكبراء يسخرون منه كلما مرُّوا به. ثم جاء أمر الله، وبدأ الطوفان. وأُمر نوح أن يحمل فيها زوجين من كل صنف من المخلوقات، وأهله ومن آمن، وما كانوا إلا قليلًا.

وجرت السفينة في موج كالجبال. ورأى نوح أحد أبنائه منعزلًا، فناداه أن يركب معهم. فأجاب الابن بأنه سيلجأ إلى جبل. فحال الموج بينهما، وكان من المغرَقين. وكلَّم نوح ربه في شأن ابنه، فأخبره الله أن هذا الابن ليس من أهله حقًّا، لأن عمله غير صالح. فتقبَّل نوح ذلك بتواضع.

ثم أُمرت الأرض أن تبلع ماءها، والسماء أن تمسك مطرها. واستقرَّت السفينة على جبل الجوديِّ، وقيل لنوح أن يهبط بسلام، وببركات عليه وعلى من معه. ويذكر القرآن أن السفينة تُركت آية للناس جميعًا.$tk$, $tk$واصِل فعل الصواب حتى لو تأخرت النتائج. فالنجاح يُقاس بالثبات والوفاء، لا بعدد من يتبعونك.$tk$, $tk$["11:25-48","71:1-28","29:14-15","54:9-15"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$hud$tk$, 'ar', 4, $tk$هود$tk$, $tk$هود$tk$, $tk$الرسول المبعوث إلى قوم عاد$tk$, $tk$أُرسل هود عليه السلام إلى قوم عاد، الذين كانوا يسكنون منطقة يسمِّيها القرآن الأحقاف، أي كثبان الرمل. وقد جاؤوا بعد قوم نوح وأُوتوا قوة عظيمة. وكان هود واحدًا منهم: فالقرآن يسمِّيه أخاهم.

وكانت رسالته هي رسالة كل نبي: اعبدوا الله وحده، فليس لكم إله غيره. وأخبرهم أنه لا يسألهم أجرًا. ودعاهم إلى أن يستغفروا ربهم ويتوبوا إليه، ووعدهم بأن الله سيرسل عليهم حينئذ المطر غزيرًا ويزيدهم قوة إلى قوتهم.

فردَّ كبراء عاد ردًّا غليظًا. واتهموه بالسفه والكذب. بل قال بعضهم إن أحد آلهتهم لا بد أن يكون قد أصابه بسوء. فأجابهم هود بهدوء بأنه ليس به سفه، وأنه رسول من رب العالمين، وأنه لهم ناصح أمين. وذكَّرهم بأن الله جعلهم خلفاء من بعد قوم نوح، وطلب منهم أن يذكروا نِعَمه.

ثم قال كلامًا فيه شجاعة عظيمة. فأعلن أنه بريء من الآلهة الباطلة التي يعبدونها، وتحدَّاهم أن يكيدوا له جميعًا، من غير أن يمهلوه. وقال إنه متوكل على الله، ربه وربهم، الذي بيده أمر كل دابة.

ولم يتغيَّر قوم عاد. بل طلبوا من هود أن يأتيهم بالعذاب الذي يحذِّرهم منه. وذات يوم رأوا سحابًا مقبلًا على أوديتهم، ففرحوا وظنوا أنه سيمطرهم. لكنه كان في الحقيقة ريحًا عاتية تحمل عذابًا أليمًا. ويذكر القرآن أنها هبَّت عليهم سبع ليال وثمانية أيام متتابعة، فتركت القوم صرعى كجذوع نخل خاوية. وبعدها لم يعد يُرى إلا مساكنهم.

ونجا هود والذين آمنوا معه برحمة من الله. والقرآن لا يخبرنا كم كانوا ولا أين ذهبوا بعد ذلك.

وقصة عاد تتكرر في القرآن مرات، تحذيرًا لكل قوم أقوياء: فالقوة والرخاء عطايا، ولا يحميان أحدًا يستعملهما في ردِّ الحق والاستكبار.$tk$, $tk$القوة والموهبة والمال نِعَم تستوجب الشكر، لا أسباب للتعالي على الآخرين. وإذا نصحك أحد بإخلاص، فاسمع قبل أن تحكم.$tk$, $tk$["7:65-72","11:50-60","46:21-25","69:6-8"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$salih$tk$, 'ar', 5, $tk$صالح$tk$, $tk$صالح$tk$, $tk$الرسول المبعوث إلى ثمود$tk$, $tk$أُرسل صالح عليه السلام إلى قوم ثمود، الذين جاؤوا بعد قوم عاد. وكانوا بنَّائين مهرة: فالقرآن يذكر أنهم كانوا يبنون القصور في السهول وينحتون البيوت في الجبال. وكان صالح واحدًا منهم، وكان قومه قبل رسالته يعقدون عليه آمالًا كبيرة.

دعاهم إلى عبادة الله وحده. وذكَّرهم بأن الله هو الذي أنشأهم من الأرض وأسكنهم فيها، وطلب منهم أن يستغفروه وألا يفسدوا في الأرض. فكان جوابهم خيبة أمل: كيف ينهاهم عن عبادة ما كان يعبد آباؤهم؟ وقالوا إنهم في شك شديد من رسالته.

وآمن به قليل من الناس، أكثرهم من الضعفاء. فسألهم الكبراء المتكبِّرون، في ازدراء، هل يظنون حقًّا أن صالحًا مرسَل من ربه. فأجاب المؤمنون بوضوح أنهم مؤمنون بما أُرسل به.

ثم آتى الله ثمود آية بيِّنة: ناقة. والقرآن يسمِّيها ناقة الله. وطلب صالح من قومه أن يتركوها ترعى حرَّة في أرض الله، وأن يحترموا نصيبها من الشرب، وألا يمسُّوها بسوء، وإلا أخذهم عذاب. والقرآن لا يصف كيف ظهرت الناقة، والقصص المفصَّلة التي تُروى في ذلك غير ثابتة.

كان الامتحان بسيطًا، لكنهم سقطوا فيه. ويذكر القرآن أيضًا تسعة رجال في المدينة كانوا يفسدون، وتحالفوا على أن يهاجموا صالحًا وأهله ليلًا. وانبعث أشقاهم، فعُقرت الناقة وقُتلت. ثم تحدَّوا صالحًا أن يأتيهم بالعذاب الذي وعدهم به.

فأخبرهم صالح أنه لم يبقَ لهم إلا ثلاثة أيام يتمتعون فيها في ديارهم، وأن ذلك وعد لا يُكذَب. ثم جاء العذاب: أخذتهم صيحة عظيمة ورجفة، فأصبحوا هامدين في ديارهم، كأنهم لم يعيشوا فيها قط. ونجا صالح والذين آمنوا معه برحمة من الله.

ويذكر القرآن آخر كلام صالح وهو ينصرف عنهم: أنه أبلغهم رسالة ربه ونصح لهم بإخلاص، لكنهم لا يحبون الناصحين.$tk$, $tk$النعمة مسؤولية أيضًا: فاحترم ما اؤتُمنت عليه. ورحِّب بالنصيحة المخلصة، حتى لو لم تكن ما تحب أن تسمعه.$tk$, $tk$["7:73-79","11:61-68","27:45-53","91:11-15"]$tk$::jsonb)
